      "text": ":\nOkay, let's see. I need to figure out if Bob is white based on the given information. Let me start by listing all the given statements and then see how they connect.\n\nFirst, the context:\n\n1. Bob is quiet.\n2. Bob is smart.\n3. Charlie is quiet.\n4. Charlie is not white.\n5. Fiona is young.\n6. Harry is big.\n7. Harry is nice.\n8. If something is quiet and white then it is green. (So, Quiet \u2227 White \u2192 Green)\n9. All smart things are green. (Smart \u2192 Green)\n10. Green things are white. (Green \u2192 White)\n11. All smart, young things are big. (Smart \u2227 Young \u2192 Big)\n12. White things are not young. (White \u2192 \u00acYoung)\n13. If something is smart and not young then it is nice. (Smart \u2227 \u00acYoung \u2192 Nice)\n14. If Bob is green and Bob is nice then Bob is big. (Bob: (Green \u2227 Nice) \u2192 Big)\n\nThe question is whether Bob is white. The options are True, False, or Unknown.\n\nLet me start by analyzing Bob's attributes. From the context, Bob is quiet (1) and smart (2). Since all smart things are green (9), Bob must be green because he's smart. So Bob is green.\n\nNow, since green things are white (10), if Bob is green, he must be white. Wait, that would mean Bob is white? But let me check that again.\n\nWait, the statement 10 says \"Green things are white\", so Green \u2192 White. Therefore, if Bob is green, then he must be white. So that would mean Bob is white. But hold on, maybe there's a contradiction somewhere?\n\nWait, let's check other constraints. Let me see.\n\nAlso, from statement 8: If something is quiet and white, then it is green. But since Bob is quiet and if he is white, then that would make him green, which he already is. So that's consistent.\n\nBut let's check another angle. Let me see if there's any reason Bob couldn't be white.\n\nLooking at statement 12: White things are not young. So if Bob is white, then he can't be young. But the problem doesn't say anything about Bob's youth. Fiona is young, but Bob's age isn't mentioned. So that's okay. So being white would just mean Bob isn't young, which is allowed unless there's another constraint.\n\nWait, also, looking at statement 11: All smart, young things are big. But since Bob's youth isn't stated, maybe that's irrelevant here.\n\nBut let's see another point. There's also statement 13: If something is smart and not young, then it is nice. So if Bob is smart (which he is) and not young, then he must be nice. But do we know if Bob is nice?\n\nThe problem doesn't directly say Bob is nice, but there's statement 14: If Bob is green and nice, then he is big. Hmm, so statement 14 is a conditional. Let's see.\n\nWait, let's recap Bob's attributes so far:\n\n- Quiet (1)\n- Smart (2)\n- Therefore, green (from 9)\n- Therefore, white (from 10)\n- If he's white, then from 12, he can't be young (so \u00acYoung)\n- If he's smart and not young (since \u00acYoung), then by statement 13, he must be nice. So Smart \u2227 \u00acYoung \u2192 Nice. Since Bob is smart and if he's not young, he must be nice. But we just concluded he's not young (because he's white), so he must be nice. So Bob must be nice.\n\nNow, if Bob is nice and green (since he's smart), then according to statement 14: (Green \u2227 Nice) \u2192 Big. So Bob would have to be big. But the problem doesn't say Bob is big. Wait, but the problem doesn't say he isn't either. So is there a contradiction here?\n\nWait, but maybe that's okay. Because the question is about whether Bob is white, not whether he's big. Let's see.\n\nWait, but let's check if there's any inconsistency. Let me go through step by step again to be sure.\n\n1. Bob is smart \u2192 green (from 9).\n2. Green \u2192 white (from 10). So Bob is white.\n3. If Bob is white, then by 12, he's not young (\u00acYoung).\n4. Since Bob is smart and \u00acYoung (from 3), then by 13, he must be nice (Smart \u2227 \u00acYoung \u2192 Nice). Therefore, Bob is nice.\n5. Now, Bob is green (from 1) and nice (from 4), so by 14, (Green \u2227 Nice) \u2192 Big. Therefore, Bob must be big.\n\nBut the problem doesn't state that Bob is big. However, the problem also doesn't state that Bob isn't big. So does that matter? The question is only about whether Bob is white, so even if there's an implication that he must be big, unless that's contradicted by another statement, it's okay. Since there's no statement that Bob is not big, the fact that he must be big is just an additional conclusion, but not a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, the chain of reasoning leads us to conclude that Bob must be white, so the answer would be A) True.\n\nWait, but let me check if there's any possibility that Bob isn't white. Let's see if there's another path.\n\nSuppose Bob is not white. Then, since he's smart (so green), but Green \u2192 White (from 10), so if he's green, he must be white. Therefore, Bob cannot be not white if he's green. Since he is smart, he must be green, so he must be white. Therefore, Bob must be white. Therefore, the answer is True (A).\n\nBut wait, let me check another angle. The problem says \"If something is quiet and white then it is green.\" So if Bob is quiet and white, then he's green. Since Bob is quiet and we just concluded he is white, that would make him green, which is consistent with the previous conclusion. So that's okay.\n\nAnother point: Charlie is not white (statement 4). So Charlie's not white, but Charlie is quiet. So Charlie is quiet but not white, so the antecedent of statement 8 (quiet and white) isn't met, so that's fine.\n\nFiona is young (statement 5), so since White \u2192 \u00acYoung (statement 12), Fiona can't be white. But that's okay.\n\nHarry is big and nice. Harry's attributes don't conflict with anything here. Since Harry is big, maybe he's a smart and young? Wait, Fiona is the only one stated to be young. Since Harry is big, maybe he's a smart and young? But Fiona is young, but Harry's youth isn't mentioned. So maybe Harry is big because of some other reason, but the problem says All smart, young things are big. So unless he's smart and young, but since his youth isn't stated, that's possible but not necessary. Anyway, that's not directly related to Bob.\n\nTherefore, all the steps lead to Bob being white. So the answer should be A) True.\n\nWait, but hold on. Let me check again the statement 10: Green things are white. So if something is green, it must be white. Since Bob is green, he must be white. So that's a direct conclusion. So the answer is True. So A is correct. So why is the option C even there? Maybe I made a mistake.\n\nWait, let me check if there's any way Bob could not be white. Suppose Bob is not white. Then, since he's green (because he's smart), but Green \u2192 White, that would be a contradiction. Therefore, Bob must be white. Therefore, the answer is True. So option A.\n\nHmm, maybe I'm missing something. Let me re-express all the premises as formal logic to be precise.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet P(x) mean x is quiet.\n\nQ(x) mean x is smart.\n\nR(x) mean x is white.\n\nS(x) mean x is young.\n\nT(x) mean x is big.\n\nU(x) mean x is nice.\n\nGiven:\n\n1. P(Bob)\n\n2. Q(Bob)\n\n3. P(Charlie)\n\n4. \u00acR(Charlie)\n\n5. S(Fiona)\n\n6. T(Harry)\n\n7. U(Harry)\n\n8. \u2200x ( (P(x) \u2227 R(x)) \u2192 G(x) )\n\nWait, but G(x) is green. Wait, the problem says \"If something is quiet and white then it is green.\" So Green is another predicate. Let me define G(x) as x is green.\n\nSo:\n\n8. \u2200x ( (P(x) \u2227 R(x)) \u2192 G(x) )\n\n9. \u2200x ( Q(x) \u2192 G(x) )\n\n10. \u2200x ( G(x) \u2192 R(x) )\n\n11. \u2200x ( (Q(x) \u2227 S(x)) \u2192 T(x) )\n\n12. \u2200x ( R(x) \u2192 \u00acS(x) )\n\n13. \u2200x ( (Q(x) \u2227 \u00acS(x)) \u2192 U(x) )\n\n14. (G(Bob) \u2227 U(Bob)) \u2192 T(Bob)\n\nNow, the question is R(Bob)?\n\nLet me proceed step by step.\n\nFrom 2, Q(Bob). By 9, Q\u2192G, so G(Bob).\n\nFrom 10, G\u2192R, so R(Bob). Therefore, Bob is white. So that's it. So R(Bob) is necessarily tru