is.\n\nAlternatively, can we infer the tiger is big?\n\nStatement 14 says if something sees the mouse, then it\'s big. If the tiger sees the mouse, then it must be big. But do we know if the tiger sees the mouse?\n\nThe mouse eats the tiger (statement 8), but that\'s the mouse\'s action. The mouse doesn\'t see the tiger (statement 9). But the tiger seeing the mouse isn\'t stated.\n\nAlternatively, maybe another entity sees the mouse and thus must be big. For example, the cat chases the mouse (statement 2), but does that imply it sees the mouse? Not necessarily, unless there\'s a rule about chasing requiring seeing. The problem doesn\'t state that.\n\nAlternatively, the dog sees the cat (statement 6), but that doesn\'t involve the mouse.\n\nSo we can\'t confirm that the tiger sees the mouse, so statement 14 doesn\'t help us there.\n\nAnother thought: The tiger eats the dog (statement 10). If eating requires seeing, then the tiger must see the dog to eat it. But the problem\'s rules don\'t state that. Unless there\'s an implicit assumption, but in logic problems, we can only use the given statements.\n\nSince there\'s no rule that says "if X eats Y then X sees Y", we can\'t assume that. So the tiger could eat the dog without seeing it, unless stated otherwise.\n\nHmm. Let\'s see if there\'s another path. Let\'s look at statement 18 again: If something sees the dog and chases the tiger, then the tiger chases the dog.\n\nSuppose that the tiger is the "something" here. If the tiger sees the dog and chases the tiger (itself?), then the tiger would have to chase the dog. But chasing itself might not be required, but the antecedent requires seeing the dog and chasing the tiger. If the tiger does both, then the consequent is that the tiger chases the dog, which it already does via eating (but eating isn\'t chasing). Wait, no, the tiger\'s action of eating the dog is separate from chasing.\n\nWait, actually, the problem doesn\'t state that the tiger chases the dog. It only says the tiger eats the dog. So maybe the tiger chases the dog to eat it, but that\'s an assumption. But the given statements don\'t explicitly say that.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the dog is being chased by the cat, and the tiger is eating the dog, but unless there\'s a rule that links eating to chasing, we can\'t make that connection.\n\nHmm. Let me try to see if there\'s a way to get the tiger to see the dog through statement 20. If the tiger chases the cat, then it must see the dog. So if we can establish that the tiger chases the cat, then we can conclude that it sees the dog, making the statement "the tiger does not see the dog" false (so answer B).\n\nBut does the tiger chase the cat?\n\nThe original statements say the cat chases the tiger (statement 3). There\'s no direct statement that the tiger chases the cat. Unless there\'s a rule that requires it.\n\nLooking at statement 17: If something chases the tiger and the tiger eats the cat, then the cat sees the mouse. The first part is if something chases the tiger. The cat does chase the tiger (statement 3), so if the tiger eats the cat, then the cat sees the mouse. But the tiger doesn\'t eat the cat (it eats the dog), so the condition isn\'t met. So that doesn\'t help.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the tiger\'s chasing the cat isn\'t required by any rules, so we can\'t assume it does. Thus, statement 20\'s antecedent isn\'t triggered, so we can\'t conclude the tiger sees the dog via that route.\n\nHmm. So perhaps there\'s no direct or indirect way to conclude that the tiger sees the dog, but also no way to conclude it doesn\'t. Let\'s check for contradictions if we assume it does or doesn\'t.\n\nSuppose the tiger does see the dog. Is there any contradiction? Let\'s see:\n\nIf the tiger sees the dog, then statement 19 could apply if it\'s green and big. But since we don\'t know those, no contradiction there. Also, statement 18 might be involved if the tiger also chases the tiger (itself?), but that\'s not necessary. So n