ce from 1), so by premise 9 (Furry ∧ Nice → Smart), Bob is smart. \n5. Now, Bob is nice (1) and smart (from step 4), so by premise 12 (Nice ∧ Smart → Quiet), he must be quiet. \n\nIs there any possible contradiction here? Let\'s see if there are any other rules that might affect this. \n\nWait, premise 10 says if someone is furry and white, then they are nice. Since Bob is furry and white, that would also confirm he\'s nice, which aligns with premise 1. So that\'s consistent. \n\nAnother check: Bob is round (premise 2). Premise 13 says Round → White, which is already satisfied because he\'s white. So no conflict there. \n\nIs there any possibility that being white and blue could interfere? Since all white are blue (premise 11), Bob\'s being blue is necessary, so that\'s okay. \n\nTherefore, all the premises lead to Bob being quiet, so the answer should be A) True. \n\nWait a second, hold on. Wait, let me check if there\'s a circularity here. Let me see again:\n\nBob is white → blue (from 11). So blue → furry (8). So Bob is furry. Then, since he\'s furry and nice (1), he\'s smart (9). Then smart and nice → quiet (12). So yes, all steps hold. \n\nHmm. But wait, another thought: The premise says "All white people are blue" (11). So if someone is white, they must be blue. So Bob is white, so he must be blue. Therefore, he is blue, so furry. \n\nTherefore, all steps are valid. So the conclusion is that Bob is quiet. Therefore, the answer is A) True. \n\nWait, but I just realized that premise 10 says: If someone is furry and white, then they are nice. So Bob is furry and white, so that would make him nice. But Bob was already stated as nice in premise 1, so that\'s consistent, but does that mean that premise 10 is redundant here? Not really. It just confirms that being furry and white would require him to be nice, which he already is. So no problem.\n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is True (A). But let me check the options again. The options are A) True, B) False,