      "text": ":\nOkay, let's try to tackle this problem step by step. First, I need to parse all the given information and see how it connects to determine if Harry is furry. Let me list out each of the premises one by one and see what conclusions I can draw.\n\nStarting with the context:\n\n1. Erin is smart.\n2. Fiona is quiet.\n3. Gary is blue.\n4. Gary is kind.\n5. Gary is quiet.\n6. Gary is red.\n7. Harry is smart.\n8. If Harry is smart then Harry is kind. (So, since Harry is smart (from 7), this would mean Harry is kind. That's modus ponens here.)\n9. Quiet, smart things are blue. (So, anything that is both quiet and smart must be blue.)\n10. If something is green and smart then it is not blue. (So, green \u2227 smart \u2192 \u00acblue)\n11. Kind things are quiet. (So, if something is kind, then it's quiet.)\n12. All blue, green things are smart. (Hmm, blue \u2227 green \u2192 smart)\n13. Red things are furry. (So, red \u2192 furry)\n14. If Harry is smart and Harry is blue then Harry is red. (So, (smart \u2227 blue) \u2192 red for Harry specifically.)\n\nThe question is whether Harry is furry. To determine this, I need to see if Harry is red, because if he's red, then by statement 13, he must be furry. Alternatively, maybe there's another way he could be furry, but the only direct way given is through being red. So the key is to see if Harry is red.\n\nFirst, let's note that Harry is smart (from 7). Then, from statement 8, since Harry is smart, he must be kind. So Harry is kind. From statement 11, kind things are quiet, so Harry is quiet. So now Harry has the attributes: smart, kind, quiet.\n\nNow, looking at statement 9: Quiet and smart things are blue. Since Harry is both quiet and smart, this would imply that Harry must be blue. Wait, so that would mean Harry is blue. Let me check that again. Statement 9 says \"Quiet, smart things are blue\" which translates to (quiet \u2227 smart) \u2192 blue. Since Harry is both, he must be blue. Therefore, Harry is blue.\n\nNow, moving on. Since Harry is smart and blue (from above), we can look at statement 14: If Harry is smart and blue, then he is red. So (smart \u2227 blue) \u2192 red. Since he is both, this would mean Harry is red. Therefore, Harry is red. \n\nIf Harry is red, then by statement 13 (red \u2192 furry), Harry must be furry. So that would make the statement \"Harry is furry\" true. So the answer should be A) True?\n\nWait, but let me check for any contradictions or possible alternative scenarios. Let me go through each step again to confirm.\n\nFirst, Harry is smart (given). Therefore, by statement 8, he must be kind. Kindness leads to quietness via statement 11. So Harry is quiet. Then, since he's quiet and smart, by statement 9, he must be blue. That's solid so far.\n\nNow, being smart and blue, statement 14 applies. So he must be red. Then red implies furry. So that's a chain: smart \u2192 kind \u2192 quiet \u2192 (with smart) blue \u2192 (with smart) red \u2192 furry. So yes, that seems to hold.\n\nWait, but is there any possibility that Harry isn't blue? Let me see. The only way Harry wouldn't be blue is if statement 9's antecedent isn't met. But Harry is both quiet and smart, so the antecedent is met, so blue must follow. So no, he must be blue. Then statement 14 is triggered, leading to red. So red leads to furry. So the conclusion is Harry is furry, so the answer is A) True.\n\nBut wait, another thought: statement 10 says if something is green and smart, then it's not blue. But Harry is blue, so if he were green, that would be a contradiction. However, there's no information that Harry is green. So we can't assume he is green unless stated. Since the premises don't mention Harry being green, we can't infer that. Therefore, Harry's green status is unknown, but since he is blue, if he were green, that would conflict, but since there's no evidence he is green, that's irrelevant here. \n\nAnother point: statement 12 says all blue and green things are smart. But