      "text": "\nOkay, let's try to figure this out step by step. The question is whether the tiger is blue, and the options are True, False, or Unknown. Let me go through the given context and see what information I can extract that relates to the tiger's color.\n\nFirst, the context lists several statements about different animals: bald eagle, bear, rabbit, and tiger. Each has attributes like being big, red, needing others, seeing others, visiting others, etc. The problem involves applying logical rules to determine the truth of the statement \"The tiger is blue.\"\n\nLet me list out all the given facts and rules:\n\nFacts:\n1. The bald eagle is big.\n2. The bald eagle is red.\n3. The bald eagle sees the tiger.\n4. The bald eagle visits the bear.\n5. The bear needs the bald eagle.\n6. The bear sees the bald eagle.\n7. The bear does not see the tiger.\n8. The rabbit is big.\n9. The rabbit needs the bald eagle.\n10. The rabbit needs the bear.\n11. The rabbit visits the tiger.\n12. The tiger needs the bald eagle.\n13. The tiger needs the rabbit.\n14. The tiger visits the bear.\n\nRules (conditional statements):\nA. If something is big then it needs the tiger. (If Big(X) \u2192 Needs(X, Tiger))\nB. If something needs the tiger and it sees the bald eagle then the tiger is big. (If (Needs(X, Tiger) \u2227 Sees(X, BaldEagle)) \u2192 Big(Tiger))\nC. If something needs the tiger and it visits the bear then the tiger needs the rabbit. (If (Needs(X, Tiger) \u2227 Visits(X, Bear)) \u2192 Needs(Tiger, Rabbit))\nD. If the bald eagle visits the bear and the bald eagle needs the tiger then the bear needs the tiger. (If (Visits(BaldEagle, Bear) \u2227 Needs(BaldEagle, Tiger)) \u2192 Needs(Bear, Tiger))\nE. If something needs the tiger then it is red. (If Needs(X, Tiger) \u2192 Red(X))\nF. If the bear sees the rabbit then the rabbit sees the tiger. (If Sees(Bear, Rabbit) \u2192 Sees(Rabbit, Tiger))\nG. If something is blue then it visits the bear. (If Blue(X) \u2192 Visits(X, Bear)