el (fact3), but does the cow see the cow? Well, the cow would see itself, but that\'s a bit odd. Wait, the conditional says "someone" who sees the cow (so someone else seeing the cow?) and chases the squirrel. Hmm, perhaps the cow is the one chasing the squirrel, but the "someone" here would have to be another entity. Wait, maybe I need to parse this again.\n\nLet me think again. The eighth conditional: If someone (X) sees the cow and X chases the squirrel, then X sees the rabbit. \n\nSo for example, if the squirrel sees the cow and chases the squirrel (wait, but the squirrel is chasing the rabbit, per fact10). Wait, perhaps the squirrel is the one who chases the squirrel? No, that\'s not possible. Maybe another animal. Let\'s see. The cow chases the squirrel (fact1). So the cow is the one who chases the squirrel, and the cow does see the squirrel (fact3). Therefore, according to the eighth conditional, if the cow sees the cow (which is trivial?), and chases the squirrel, then the cow must see the rabbit. \n\nWait, the cow seeing the cow might not make sense, but perhaps the condition is that the cow sees the cow (so, itself?), which is a bit strange, but maybe the condition is that someone else sees the cow. Alternatively, maybe "someone" here can be the cow itself. Let me think: The cow sees the cow (maybe it\'s a typo, but in the problem\'s context, perhaps it\'s okay). \n\nWait, the cow does see the squirrel (fact3), but the conditional\'s first part is "sees the cow". So if the cow is the "someone" in question, then does the cow see the cow? Well, perhaps that\'s not necessary. Maybe the conditional is referring to another animal. Let\'s see:\n\nThe cow chases the squirrel (fact1), and the cow sees the squirrel (fact3). But the conditional requires that the someone (the cow in this case) sees the cow (themselves?), and chases the squirrel, leading to seeing the rabbit. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the conditional is meant to be "if someone sees the cow and chases