f something is blue then it is furry. (B → Furry)\n20. All blue, smart things are furry. (Hmm, maybe redundant with 19? Wait, maybe it\'s specifying that if something is blue and smart, then it must be furry. But since blue already implies furry, perhaps it\'s emphasizing that even if blue and smart, it still holds. Not sure if needed yet.)\n21. If Bob is furry and Bob is not rough then Bob is green. (If Furry ∧ ¬R → G)\n22. If something is green then it is blue. (G → B)\n23. If something is red and not nice then it is not blue. (R ∧ ¬N → ¬B)\n\nNow, the question is about Bob being not green. So we need to see if we can conclude Bob\'s color based on the given info. \n\nFirst, let\'s note Bob\'s known attributes:\n\nFrom the context, Bob is rough (fact 1). So R(Bob) = true. \n\nNow, we can look at the implications. Since Bob is rough (R), according to rule 18 (If R → N), so Bob must be nice. So N(Bob) is true. \n\nNow, since Bob is nice (N) and rough (R), let\'s see the first conditional: rule 16 says if something is nice and not rough, then it is red. But Bob is nice AND rough, so the antecedent (N ∧ ¬R) is false. Therefore, the implication holds but doesn\'t tell us anything about Bob\'s color here. \n\nNext, since Bob is nice, by rule 17 (N → S), so Bob must be smart. So S(Bob) is true. \n\nNow, looking at rule 15: "Smart things are green." So if something is smart, then it is green. So since Bob is smart, then according to this, Bob must be green. Wait, but that would mean Bob is green. But the question is asking if "Bob is not green" is true. If this is the case, then the statement would be false, so answer B. But I need to check if there are any contradictions or exceptions here. \n\nWait, hold on. Let\'s think again. Rule 15 says "Smart things are green." So S → G. Therefore, since Bob is smart (from above), he must be green. But then, according to rule 22, which says "If something is green, then it is blue." So G → B. Therefore, if Bob is green, he must also