so Bob must be green.\n\n5. From G → B (rule 22), so Bob is blue.\n\n6. From B → Furry (rule 19), so Bob must be furry.\n\nWait, so even though the original context didn\'t mention Bob\'s furrieness, through the chain of reasoning, we can conclude Bob must be furry. Because he is blue (from step 5), and being blue requires being furry. \n\nTherefore, Bob is furry. \n\nNow, going back to rule 21: "If Bob is furry and Bob is not rough then Bob is green." \n\nBut in Bob\'s case, he is furry (from step 6) and he is rough (given), so the antecedent (furry ∧ ¬R) is false. Therefore, the implication holds but doesn\'t add anything. \n\nTherefore, all the rules are satisfied. \n\nTherefore, according to this, Bob must be green. Therefore, the statement "Bob is not green" is false. So the correct answer would be B) False. \n\nWait, but let me double-check if there\'s any other rule that could contradict this. \n\nAnother angle: Let\'s see if there\'s a way Bob could not be green. Suppose someone claims Bob is not green. Let\'s see if that\'s possible. \n\nIf Bob were not green, then since he is smart (from steps 2-3), rule 15 (S → G) would be violated because S is true but G is false. That\'s a contradiction. Therefore, Bob must be green. \n\nTherefore, the statement "Bob is not green" must be false, so the answer is B. \n\nWait, but the options are A) True, B) False, C) Unknown. So B would mean the statement is false, which would mean Bob is green. \n\nBut let me check the rules again to see if there\'s any possibility of another conclusion. \n\nWait, another point: Rule 22 says "If something is green then it is blue." So Bob being green implies blue. Then, since blue implies furry (rule 19), Bob must be furry. \n\nBut the original context didn\'t state Bob\'s furrieness. But through the implications, we derived that he must be furry. \n\nIs there any contradiction here? Let me see. \n\nThe only rule that might involve furrieness for Bob is rule 21. Since we\'ve established