iet (statement 3), so if Anne were young, that would create a contradiction. Therefore, Anne cannot be young. Wait, does that mean Anne is not young?\n\nLet me see:\n\nSuppose Anne is young. Then, since she is furry (statement 2), by statement 11, she must be quiet. But statement 3 says she\'s not quiet. Therefore, Anne cannot be young. So, Anne is not young. That\'s an important point.\n\nBut does being not young affect anything else? Let\'s see if there are other rules that involve age and other attributes.\n\nLooking at statement 13: Kind, big things are young. So if something is both kind and big, it must be young. But we know Charlie is kind (statement 4). But we don\'t know if Charlie is big or not. But this might not affect Anne directly.\n\nStatement 14: If something is kind and not furry, then it is not young. Since Anne is furry, this doesn\'t apply to her. But Charlie is kind, so if Charlie were not furry, then he would not be young. But since kind things are furry (statement 8), Charlie must be furry. So Charlie is kind and furry, so statement 14 doesn\'t apply to him either.\n\nBack to Anne. Since we\'ve established that Anne is big (from being furry → nice → big), the only way the conclusion that Anne is big could be wrong is if one of the premises that leads to that conclusion is somehow negated. But the premises are given as facts. Since Anne is furry, and the rules are that furry implies nice and nice implies big, then unless there\'s a contradiction in the rules, Anne must be big. Therefore, the statement "Anne is not big" is false. So the answer should be B) False.\n\nWait, but let me check again for any possible oversight. Let me retrace:\n\nAnne is furry → nice (statement 10). Then nice → big (statement 9). So yes, that\'s a direct chain. Unless there\'s a rule that says something else can override that, but I don\'t see any. The only way Anne wouldn\'t be big is if she\'s not nice, but since she\'s furry, she must be nice, so she must be big. T