erred that the cow does need the mouse (because of statement 23\'s implication), and the mouse does see the cow (statement 11), then the antecedent of statement 19 is now true. Therefore, the conclusion must hold: the mouse is big. Therefore, the mouse must be big, so the statement "the mouse is not big" would be false. Therefore, the answer would be B) False.\n\nWait, but let me double-check this chain of reasoning because it\'s a bit involved.\n\nFirst, the cow is big (statement 4). Therefore, by statement 22, if someone is big, they see the bald eagle. Therefore, the cow must see the bald eagle. So that\'s an inference.\n\nThen, using statement 23: If someone is big and sees the bald eagle, then they need the mouse. The cow is big and sees the bald eagle (from above), so the cow needs the mouse. So now, the cow needs the mouse is true.\n\nNow, statement 19 says: If the cow needs the mouse and the mouse sees the cow, then the mouse is big. Since both conditions are met (cow needs mouse and mouse sees cow), then the mouse must be big. Therefore, the mouse is big. So the statement "The mouse is not big" is false. Therefore, the answer should be B) False.\n\nBut wait, is there any possibility that this creates a contradiction? Let me see. Let me check all steps again.\n\n1. The cow is big (given).\n\n2. From statement 22: "If someone is big then they see the bald eagle." Since the cow is big, it must see the bald eagle. So cow sees bald eagle is inferred.\n\n3. Then, statement 23: "If someone is big and they see the bald eagle then they need the mouse." Since cow is big and sees the bald eagle, therefore, the cow needs the mouse. So that\'s a new inferred fact.\n\n4. Now, the cow needs the mouse (inferred) and the mouse sees the cow (given in statement 11). Therefore, the antecedent of statement 19 is true (cow needs mouse AND mouse sees cow). Therefore, the conclusion must be true: the mouse is big.\n\nThus, the mouse must be big. Hence, the statement "The mouse is 