o be both red and white, which can't be. Therefore, the conclusion is that Gary cannot be quiet, because that would create a contradiction. Therefore, the statement \"Gary is quiet\" must be false.\n\nWait, but how does that work? Let me go through again step by step.\n\n1. Gary is big (given) \u2192 round (from B\u2192Ro). So round.\n\n2. Gary is red (given) and round (from above), so by statement 11 (R & Ro \u2192 Q), he must be quiet.\n\nBut if he is quiet, then since he is also red (from given), by statement 9 (Q & R \u2192 S), he must be smart.\n\nNow, he is smart (from above) and big (given), so by statement 8 (S & B \u2192 W), he must be white.\n\nBut Gary is red (given). Contradiction. Therefore, the assumption that he is quiet leads to a contradiction. Therefore, Gary cannot be quiet. Hence the statement \"Gary is quiet\" must be false. So the answer would be B) False.\n\nWait, but that seems like the correct path. Let me see if there's another angle. Alternatively, perhaps there's a rule that says someone can't be both red and white? The problem doesn't state any such rule, but in the given information, Erin is white, but there's no mention of her color conflicting with others. So the problem might allow someone to be multiple colors, unless stated otherwise. But in this case, if the conclusion from being smart and big requires white, but he's red, that's a direct contradiction. Therefore, the only way this doesn't happen is if Gary is not quiet. Therefore, the statement that Gary is quiet must be false, so answer B.\n\nAlternatively, is there a way to avoid this contradiction?\n\nSuppose Gary isn't quiet. Then, the step from R & Ro \u2192 Q would require that if he's red and round, he must be quiet. But if he's not quiet, that would violate the rule. So the only way for the rules to hold without contradiction is if Gary is not round? Wait, but he is big, so he must be round (B\u2192Ro). Therefore, he is round. So he must be quiet. But that leads to the contradiction.\n\nTherefore, the only way this can be resolved is that the assumption that Gary is quiet must be false, but the rules force him to be quiet, which is a contradiction. Wait, that would mean the premises are inconsistent? But that can't be. Maybe I made a mistake.\n\nWait, let's check statement 11 again: \"Red, round people are quiet.\" So if someone is red and round, then they are quiet. So if Gary is red and round, he must be quiet. But being quiet leads to him being smart, which leads to needing to be white. But he's red, so contradiction. Therefore, the only way this works is if the initial premises have an inconsistency, but the problem says to answer based on the given info, so perhaps the conclusion is that the statement must be false because assuming it's true leads to a contradiction, so the statement can't be true. Hence the answer is B) False.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I missed something else. Let me check other rules. Let's see if there's another way.\n\nWait, Erin's case: Erin is white. Let's see if there's any info on Erin's quietness or roundness. The last statement says \"If Erin is round and Erin is quiet then Erin is smart.\" So unless Erin is both round and quiet, we can't infer her smartness. Since Erin is white, but we don't know if she's round or quiet. So that might not affect Gary.\n\nHarry is green and smart. Let's see if Harry's attributes are okay. Since Harry is smart and green, does that interact with any rules? Statement 14: red and green \u2192 big. Harry isn't red, so that's okay. Also, since Harry is smart and green, but not red, so no problem. Also, Harry is smart and... is he big? Not that we know. But since he's smart, and if he were big, then by statement 8, he must be white. But he's green, not necessarily conflicting with white? Wait, maybe colors can overlap? The problem doesn't say you can't be multiple colors. Hmm, but Erin is white, so maybe each person has one color? Not sure. But since the problem doesn't specif