ere\'s another premise, we can\'t assume the tiger is nice. Therefore, this conditional might not trigger.\n\nStatement 16: If someone sees the mouse and the mouse is red, then the mouse visits the tiger. The mouse is red (statement 8), so if someone sees the mouse, then the mouse visits the tiger. But does someone see the mouse? Let\'s see: The tiger visits the mouse (statement 11), so does that mean the tiger sees the mouse? Wait, statement 12 says if someone visits the tiger, then the tiger sees the mouse. Wait, that\'s a bit confusing. Let me parse statement 12 again: "If someone visits the tiger then the tiger sees the mouse." So the direction is: if X visits the tiger, then the tiger sees the mouse. \n\nBut in statement 11, the tiger visits the mouse. That\'s the reverse. So that doesn\'t trigger the condition in 12. However, maybe someone else visits the tiger? For example, the cat or dog? The premises don\'t mention anyone else visiting the tiger except perhaps via other conditions. \n\nWait, statement 17 says if someone visits the cat and the cat visits the dog, then the dog visits the tiger. The cat does visit the dog (statement 4), so if someone visits the cat, then the dog would visit the tiger. But who visits the cat? The tiger eats the cat (statement 9), but does eating imply visiting? Not necessarily. Also, statement 14 says if someone eats the dog, they visit the cat. Since the tiger eats the dog (statement 10), then the tiger must visit the cat (from statement 14). So the tiger visits the cat. Then, according to statement 17, since someone (the tiger) visits the cat and the cat visits the dog (which is true), then the dog must visit the tiger. \n\nSo from that, the dog visits the tiger. Then, does that lead to anything? Not sure yet. \n\nBack to statement 16: The mouse is red (statement 8). If someone sees the mouse, then the mouse visits the tiger. Who sees the mouse? The tiger visits the mouse (statement 11), but visiting doesn\'t necessarily mean