\n3. From "All nice things are blue," Charlie being nice means Charlie is blue. (Nice → Blue → Charlie is blue)\n4. Now, Charlie is young and blue. According to the rule "If something is young and blue then it is smart," Charlie is smart. (Young & Blue → Smart)\n5. Now, Charlie is smart and blue. The next rule says "All blue, smart things are furry." So Charlie must be furry. (Blue & Smart → Furry)\n6. Now, let\'s check if there\'s any contradiction here. But so far, Charlie\'s attributes are: young, blue, smart, furry, nice. \n\nNow, the question is whether Charlie is white. To be white, Charlie would have to satisfy any of the conditions leading to being white. Let\'s see:\n\nLooking at the given info, Fiona is blue and white. The rules about being white are:\n\n- Furry and nice things are white. (Furry ∧ Nice → White)\n- Also, "All smart, white things are furry." Wait, that might not directly help here.\n\nWait, the first statement about white: "Furry, nice things are white." So if something is furry and nice, then it must be white. Let me parse that again. The original says "Furry, nice things are white." So that\'s Furry ∧ Nice → White. \n\nSince Charlie is furry (from step 5) and nice (from step 2), then according to this rule, Charlie must be white. Because Furry and Nice → White. \n\nWait, that would mean Charlie is white. But let me check again because maybe I made a mistake here. Let me retrace:\n\nCharlie is young → nice (step 2). Charlie is blue (step 3). Young and blue → smart (step4). Blue and smart → furry (step5). Since Charlie is furry and nice (from young→nice), then Furry ∧ Nice → White. Therefore Charlie must be white, so the answer would be True (A). But wait, let me check other rules to see if there\'s any conflicting info.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a contradiction here? Let\'s see. Let me check all the rules again to make sure I didn\'t miss anything that could affect Charlie.\n\nWait, another rule: "If Fiona is smart and Fiona is young