other condition that could affect Charlie\'s whiteness. For instance, is there a rule that says something can\'t be both blue and white? The problem doesn\'t state that. Unless being blue and white is impossible, but the context mentions Fiona is both blue and white. So that\'s allowed. So Charlie could be blue and white. The question is just whether Charlie is white. Since the rule says if you\'re furry and nice, you must be white, then yes, Charlie is white.\n\nWait, but let me check another angle. The problem says "If something is young and blue then it is smart." Charlie is young and blue, so smart. Then, blue and smart → furry. So Charlie is furry. Then, since Charlie is furry and nice (because young→nice), then Charlie must be white. So that\'s a chain of implications leading to Charlie being white. Therefore the answer should be A) True. \n\nHmm, but why is the option C there? Maybe I missed something. Let me check again.\n\nWait, the problem says "Furry, nice things are white." So if something is both furry and nice, then it must be white. Charlie is both, so he must be white. Therefore the answer is True. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is that "nice things are blue," so Charlie is blue, but can something be both blue and white? Since Fiona is blue and white, that\'s allowed. So there\'s no contradiction. Therefore, Charlie can be both blue and white, so the statement "Charlie is white" is true. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be A) True. \n\nWait, but let me check the problem again to ensure I didn\'t miss any other constraints. Let me go through each given statement again:\n\n- Charlie is young. (given)\n- Erin is blue, not kind.\n- Fiona is blue and white.\n- Gary is blue and kind.\n- Furry and nice → white.\n- Young and blue → smart.\n- Blue and smart → furry.\n- Smart and white → furry. (Wait, that\'s "All smart, white things are furry." So if something is smart and white, then it is furry. But that\'s redundant if they are smart and white, but maybe not conflicting.)\n- Young → nice.\n- If Fiona is smart and young → not furry. But Fiona\'s age is not given, so unless we know she\'s young, this doesn\'t apply.\n- If Erin is kind → furry. But Erin isn\'t kind, so no.\n- If Gary is smart and white → not kind. But Gary is blue, so unless he\'s white, but he\'s blue. Unless blue and white? But Fiona is both. But the problem says Gary is blue, but not necessarily not white. Wait, the problem says "Gary is blue. Gary is kind." It doesn\'t say he\'s not white. So maybe Gary could be blue and white? But then if he were, then if he were smart and white, he would have to be furry. But since he is kind, the rule says if he were smart and white, then he\'s not kind. But he is kind, so that would be a contradiction. Therefore, Gary cannot be both smart and white. But since he is blue (so maybe he is white?), but since he is kind, if he were smart and white, that would violate the rule. So perhaps Gary is not smart? Wait, but how?\n\nWait, maybe I need to check Gary\'s status. Gary is blue. Is he young? Not stated. So unless he\'s young and blue, he\'s not necessarily smart. Since his age isn\'t given, he might not be smart. Therefore, the rule about Gary being smart and white doesn\'t apply unless he is smart. Since we don\'t know if he\'s smart, maybe that\'s okay. \n\nBut back to Charlie. All the steps for Charlie lead to him being white. Therefore, the answer should be A) True. Hmm, but maybe there\'s a mistake here. Let me think again.\n\nWait a second, the problem says "Furry, nice things are white." So Furry ∧ Nice → White. Therefore, if something is furry and nice, it must be white. So Charlie is both furry (from being blue, smart, and the rule) and nice (from being young). Therefore, Charlie must be white. So the answer is True. Therefore, option A. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is that "Furry, nice things are white" is a biconditional? Like "If and only if"? But the problem states it as a one-way