s the cow (fact 3), and the cow eats the bear (fact 7). So that's possible, but perhaps the cow can't eat the cat because the cat eats the cow. Wait, but those are just facts; they can coexist unless there's a rule against it. \n\nWait, the problem states all the given statements are facts, so even if contradictory, they are all true. Wait, but the problem says \"given a context and a question\", so maybe all the statements are true, even if they might seem contradictory. For example, the bear eats the cow (fact1) and the cow eats the bear (fact7). That's a cycle, but maybe allowed. \n\nBut going back to statement 20: \"If the cow eats the cat then the cat is big.\" \n\nDoes the cow eat the cat? Fact 3 says the cat eats the cow. So the cow doesn't eat the cat, so the antecedent is false, so the implication holds (since in logic, if the antecedent is false, the implication is true regardless). Therefore, statement 20 doesn't give us new info here. \n\nAnother conditional to check: statement 18: \"If someone visits the cat and the cat eats the mouse then they eat the cow.\" \n\nThe cat visits the mouse (fact6), but the cat does not eat the mouse. Wait, the mouse eats the cat (fact12), so the cat does not eat the mouse. Therefore, the second part of the antecedent (cat eats the mouse) is false. Therefore, the whole antecedent is false, so the implication holds, but doesn't give us anything. \n\nStatement 24: \"If someone eats the cat and they are cold then the cat visits the bear.\" \n\nThe mouse eats the cat (fact12). If the mouse is cold, then the cat visits the bear. But is the mouse cold? Let's see. \n\nThe mouse visits the mouse? No, but the mouse visits the cat and the cow (facts15 and16). Wait, the mouse visits the cat (fact15?), wait fact15 is the mouse sees the cat. Fact15: \"The mouse sees the cat.\" Fact16 is the mouse visits the cow. \n\nWait, the mouse's visits are to the cat (fact15? Wait no, fact15 is \"The mouse sees the cat.\" The visits are in facts 1