ear. So the cat visiting the bear is now an implied fact. \n\nNow, the cat visiting the bear would then trigger other conditionals. For example, statement 21: "If someone visits the bear then the bear visits the mouse." So if the cat visits the bear (as per above), then the bear must visit the mouse. \n\nBut the bear\'s actions are given in facts 1 and 2 (eats cow and is eaten by cat). So the bear visiting the mouse is a new implication. \n\nAdditionally, the cat visiting the bear (from the coldness) might have other effects. \n\nBut let\'s see if this path leads us to the mouse eating the cow. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the mouse eats the cow is directly stated? Looking back at the facts, the mouse\'s eating is only the bear and the cat (statements 11 and 12). So there\'s no direct fact. \n\nAnother angle: The cow eats the bear (fact 7). The cow is big (fact 8). \n\nStatement 20: "If the cow eats the cat then the cat is big." \n\nThe cow does eat the bear (fact 7), but does the cow eat the cat? Fact 3 says the cat eats the cow. Wait, fact 3: "The cat eats the cow." Wait, that\'s conflicting. Wait, fact 3 says the cat eats the cow. Fact 7 says the cow eats the bear. Wait, so the cat eats the cow (fact 3), and the cow eats the bear (fact 7). So that\'s possible, but perhaps the cow can\'t eat the cat because the cat eats the cow. Wait, but those are just facts; they can coexist unless there\'s a rule against it. \n\nWait, the problem states all the given statements are facts, so even if contradictory, they are all true. Wait, but the problem says "given a context and a question", so maybe all the statements are true, even if they might seem contradictory. For example, the bear eats the cow (fact1) and the cow eats the bear (fact7). That\'s a cycle, but maybe allowed. \n\nBut going back to statement 20: "If the cow eats the cat then the cat is big." \n\nDoes the cow eat the cat? Fact 3 says the cat eats the cow. So the cow doesn\'t eat the cat, so the antecedent is fa