ay. But does being nice lead us to any other conclusions? For instance, from point 12, young things are nice. But the reverse isn\'t stated. So being nice doesn\'t necessarily mean young. So Erin could be nice without being young, but then we need another reason.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a path where Erin\'s niceness is contradicted. Let\'s see.\n\nLooking at point 14: If Erin is kind then Erin is furry. But we know Erin is not kind (point 3), so the antecedent here is false, so the implication holds regardless. So that doesn\'t give us info about Erin\'s fur or niceness.\n\nWait, perhaps if we can find out if Erin is furry, but not sure. Let me see.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s another rule. Let me go through each rule again.\n\nPoint 9: If something is young and blue, then it is smart. Charlie is young, but we don\'t know Charlie\'s color except that Charlie is young. Wait, actually, the first point says Charlie is young, but there\'s no mention of Charlie\'s color. So unless other info, maybe Charlie isn\'t involved here.\n\nFiona is blue and white (points 4 and 5). So Fiona has two colors. Now, looking at point 13: If Fiona is smart and young, then Fiona is not furry. But we don\'t know if Fiona is smart or young. Let\'s see if Fiona can be smart. To be smart, Fiona would need to be young and blue (from point 9). But Fiona is blue (point 4), but is she young? The context doesn\'t say Fiona is young. So maybe Fiona isn\'t smart unless she\'s young. Since we don\'t know Fiona\'s age, maybe Fiona\'s smartness is unknown. So that might not help.\n\nGary is blue and kind (points 6 and 7). Now, point 15: If Gary is smart and white, then he\'s not kind. But Gary is kind (point7), so if Gary were both smart and white, that would be a contradiction. But Gary\'s color is blue (point6). Wait, Gary is blue, so he can\'t be white. Therefore, the antecedent (Gary being smart and white) is impossible, so the implication is trivially true, but that doesn\'t give us 