t since there\'s only one lion mentioned, then "the lion sees the lion" would be the only possibility. Alternatively, perhaps "the lion" in the consequent is a different lion? But the context doesn\'t mention multiple lions. So it must refer to the same lion. \n\nTherefore, the conditional requires that the lion (since it\'s young) must see the lion (itself). Therefore, the statement is true, so the answer is A. \n\nWait, but maybe I made a mistake here. Let me check again. The conditional says "If someone is young then they see the lion." The "they" refers to the same someone. So if the someone is the lion, then "they see the lion" is "the lion sees the lion". Therefore, yes, that\'s required. \n\nTherefore, the answer should be True (A). But wait, in the original context, the lion\'s seeing is listed as seeing the cow, dog, rabbit. So the explicit statements don\'t mention seeing itself, but the conditional requires it. Since the problem is asking whether "The lion sees the lion" is true based on all the information, then the conditional must be taken into account. So that makes it true. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the original statements are all the facts, and the conditionals are just additional rules. So the lion\'s seeing the lion is not listed in the explicit "sees" relationships (points 7-9), but according to the conditional, since it\'s young, it must see the lion (itself). Therefore, the answer is A. \n\nHmm, but another thought: maybe the conditional is a rule that applies unless contradicted by explicit information. Wait, but the explicit information doesn\'t contradict it. The explicit info just doesn\'t mention it. So the conditional would require it to be true, so the answer is True. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem is that "they see the lion" could mean that the young someone must see the lion (the same lion), so the lion must see the lion. Therefore, the answer is A. \n\nWait, but maybe the problem is that "the lion sees the lion" is a reflexive 