thing visits the tiger and the tiger is not red then it is not nice.\n\nThe question is whether "The dog does not visit the dog" is true, false, or unknown. The options are A, B, C.\n\nFirst, let me note down all the facts given. The dog\'s actions and attributes:\n\n- Dog chases lion (from 1)\n- Dog is nice (2)\n- Tiger eats dog (9)\n- The tiger visits the lion (11)\n- From Rule 4: If something chases the lion, it is big. Since the dog chases the lion (1), then the dog must be big (from Rule 4). Wait, so Dog is big?\n\nWait, let\'s check that again. Rule 4: If something chases the lion, then it is big. The dog chases the lion (statement 1), so according to Rule 4, the dog must be big. So that\'s a fact: Dog is big.\n\nBut wait, the lion is not big (statement 4). That\'s okay, since the lion is a different entity.\n\nAlso, the tiger chases the lion (statement 8). So the tiger also chases the lion, so by Rule 4, the tiger must be big as well. Wait, does that apply? Let me see:\n\nRule 4 applies to anything that chases the lion. So the dog and the tiger both chase the lion, so both must be big. So Tiger is big, Dog is big. That\'s important.\n\nNow, Rule 6 says: If something is big, then it visits the dog. So since the dog is big, then the dog must visit the dog? Wait, that would mean "if X is big, then X visits the dog." Wait, no, the phrasing here is important. The rule is: "If something is big then it visits the dog." So for any entity X, if X is big, then X visits the dog. So, since the dog is big (from Rule 4), then the dog must visit the dog. \n\nWait, that\'s a key point. Because if the dog is big (from Rule 4 because it chases the lion), then by Rule 6, the dog (being big) must visit the dog. So that would mean the dog does visit the dog. \n\nBut the question is asking whether the statement "The dog does not visit the dog" is true. So if according to the rules, the dog must visit itself, then the statement "does not visit" would be false, so the answer would be B (False). But maybe there\'s a contradiction or another rule that negates this?\n\nWait, but let\'s check again step by step.\n\nFirst, confirm the dog is big:\n\n- Dog chases lion (statement 1). Rule 4: If something chases the lion, it is big. Therefore, Dog is big.\n\nTherefore, Rule 6 applies to the dog: Since the dog is big, it must visit the dog (i.e., itself). So Dog visits Dog. Therefore, the statement "The dog does not visit the dog" would be false. So answer B?\n\nBut hold on, is there any other rule that could affect this? Let\'s see.\n\nWait, Rule 12 says: If something visits the dog and it is kind then it visits the mouse. Hmm, but that\'s a conditional. The dog visiting the dog would trigger this only if the dog is kind. Wait, the dog is nice (statement 2). "Kind" might be the same as "nice"? The problem uses "kind" in Rule 12. Maybe "kind" is equivalent to "nice"? Let me check the original problem\'s wording.\n\nLooking back at the context:\n\nRule 12: "If something visits the dog and it is kind then it visits the mouse."\n\nThe dog is described as "nice" in statement 2. The problem uses "kind" here. Maybe "kind" is a synonym for "nice" here. Assuming that "kind" means "nice", then since the dog is nice (statement 2), then if the dog visits the dog, then by Rule 12, it would have to visit the mouse. But does that affect anything?\n\nWait, the dog visiting itself would lead to Rule 12\'s antecedent being true (since it\'s visiting the dog (itself) and is kind (nice)), so then the dog must visit the mouse. But that\'s an extra condition, but does that create a problem?\n\nWait, but the question is only about whether the dog does not visit the dog. The main point is whether the dog does visit the dog. From Rule 6, since the dog is big, it must visit the dog (itself). So that\'s a direct conclusion. The fact that this would also require visiting the mouse is another implication, but unless that creates a contradiction, the main point is that the dog doe