t be round (fact6). Then round \u2192 cold (fact10). So Gary would be cold. \n\nBut fact7 says \"If Gary is cold then he is smart.\" Since he is cold (from being round), that requires him to be smart, which he is. So that's okay. \n\nAdditionally, being cold and blue, but any other attributes? \n\nAlso, since he is round (from being big and blue), fact12 says round things are blue, which he already is. \n\nNo contradictions here. \n\nTherefore, all the premises lead to Gary being big. So the answer should be True (A). \n\nWait, but hold on, let me check if there's another path where maybe being blue and furry doesn't require big. But fact8 says \"All blue, furry things are big.\" So if something is both blue and furry, then it must be big. Since Gary is blue and furry, he must be big. \n\nTherefore, yes, the conclusion holds. So the answer is A) True.\n</think>\n\nGary is smart (given), so by \"All smart things are furry,\" he is furry. From \"If Gary is furry then Gary is blue,\" he must be blue. Now, since Gary is both blue and furry, \"All blue, furry things are big\" requires him to be big. This chain of reasoning leads to Gary being big without contradiction. \n\n**Answer: A) True**",
