ut that\'s the converse. To get Bob\'s youth, maybe through another route.\n\nWait, let\'s see if Bob can be smart. Let me see.\n\nFirst, to check if Bob is quiet. Because quiet things are furry (rule 12), so if Bob is quiet, then he\'s furry.\n\nTo be quiet, he must be both green and smart (rule 8). Since Bob is green (fact 1), if he\'s also smart, then he\'s quiet and hence furry.\n\nSo is Bob smart?\n\nLooking at the rules, how can we determine if Bob is smart?\n\nRule 14: Nice and green things are smart. Bob is green but not nice (since only Fiona is nice, per fact 6). So that doesn\'t apply.\n\nAlternatively, is there another way? Let\'s see.\n\nAnother angle: Since Bob is kind (fact 2), and rule 11 says young → kind. Wait, but the converse isn\'t necessarily true. So being kind doesn\'t mean he\'s young. So we can\'t infer that yet.\n\nWait, let\'s think about Charlie. Charlie is green (fact 3) and not smart (fact 4). So Charlie is green but not smart, so he doesn\'t get the quiet attribute.\n\nFiona is nice and young (facts 6 and 7). From rule 14: Fiona is nice and green? Wait, Fiona is nice (fact 6) but is she green? The problem doesn\'t say. Fiona\'s attributes are nice and young. So unless stated otherwise, we can\'t assume she\'s green. So Fiona being nice and young, but not necessarily green, so rule 14 (nice and green → smart) doesn\'t apply to her. So Fiona\'s smartness isn\'t determined by that rule.\n\nBack to Bob. To see if Bob is smart, maybe via another rule. Let me check all the rules again.\n\nHmm, rule 10 says smart → young. So if someone is smart, they must be young. But that doesn\'t help us determine if Bob is smart unless we know he\'s young.\n\nAlternatively, rule 11: young → kind. Since Bob is kind, maybe he could be young? But that\'s only if being kind is sufficient for being young, which it isn\'t. The rule is one-way: young implies kind, but not necessarily the other way around.\n\nWait, perhaps through another path. Let me see.\n\nSu