ce we don\'t know if he\'s smart, we can\'t confirm he\'s furry. However, is there any other way he could be furry?\n\nRule15 is the only other mention of furry. It says if Bob is kind and not green, then he\'s furry. But Bob is green, so that doesn\'t apply. The other furry rules are via quietness. So the only path is quiet → furry, which requires being green and smart. Since green is already true, it\'s about smartness.\n\nTherefore, since smartness is unknown, the answer is Unknown (C).\n\nBut wait, hold on a second. Let me check the rule14 again: "Nice, green things are smart." So if something is nice and green, then it\'s smart. But does that mean that only nice and green things can be smart, or are there other ways to be smart?\n\nThe wording is "Nice, green things are smart." That is, if X is nice and green, then X is smart. It doesn\'t say that all smart things are nice and green. So there could be other ways to be smart, but the rules don\'t specify any others. So perhaps Bob could be smart through some other unstated path, but since there\'s no rule allowing that, we can\'t assume.\n\nTherefore, the only way to be smart is via being nice and green. Since Bob isn\'t nice, he can\'t be smart via that rule, and there\'s no other rule allowing him to be smart. Therefore, Bob cannot be smart. Wait, that\'s a key point!\n\nAh! Here\'s the mistake I made earlier. The rule says "Nice, green things are smart." That means that if something is both nice and green, then it\'s smart. But it doesn\'t mean that only nice and green things can be smart. There might be other things that are smart without being nice and green, but according to the given rules, the only way to be smart is via that rule? Or are there other possibilities?\n\nWait, actually, in logic, unless a rule specifies that "All smart things are nice and green," which it doesn\'t. The rule is only one direction: nice and green → smart. So smartness could be obtained through other means not mentioned, but since we have no information about other ways, we can\'t assume that. So in the absence of other rules, can we conclude that the only way to be smart is via that rule?\n\nNo, actually, in formal logic, if a rule states "If A then B," it doesn\'t preclude other ways for B to be true. So unless there\'s a universal statement like "Only nice and green things are smart," we can\'t assume that. Therefore, there might be other ways to be smart, but since the rules don\'t specify, we can\'t use them. Therefore, we can only use the given rules to make deductions.\n\nTherefore, since the only rule that allows someone to be smart is if they\'re nice and green (rule14), and since Bob isn\'t nice, he can\'t be smart via that rule. There\'s no other rule that allows someone to be smart. Therefore, Bob cannot be smart. Wait, is that correct?\n\nWait, the problem states "Green, smart things are quiet." That\'s a different rule (rule8). So "Green and smart → quiet". That doesn\'t state anything about how to get smartness, just a consequence.\n\nSimilarly, "Smart things are young" (rule10), which is another consequence. So the only way to be smart is via rule14. So if someone isn\'t nice and green, they can\'t be smart unless there\'s another rule. Since there\'s no other rule, we can conclude that Bob isn\'t smart because he doesn\'t meet the conditions of the only rule that grants smartness.\n\nTherefore, Bob is not smart, so he can\'t be quiet, hence not furry. Therefore, the answer would be B) False?\n\nWait, this is a crucial point. Let me clarify:\n\nRule14 says "Nice, green things are smart." So that\'s a sufficient condition but not a necessary one. But if there are no other rules that make someone smart, then in the context of the problem, the only way to be smart is via being nice and green. Since Bob isn\'t nice, he can\'t be smart. Therefore, he isn\'t smart, so he can\'t be quiet, so he\'s not furry. Hence, the answer is False (B).\n\nWait, but maybe there\'s another way