rapositive is: if something is green, then either it\'s not smart, or it is red. So Green → (¬Smart ∨ Red).\n\n- Rule5: All furry things are green. But since furry ↔ red, then all red things are green.\n\n- Rule6: If red and green, then blue. So red ∧ green → blue.\n\n- Rule8: If blue and green, then smart.\n\n- Rule9: Blue, red things are smart. Maybe that\'s blue and red? So if something is both blue and red, then it\'s smart.\n\nNow, Fiona is green. Let\'s see if she can be blue. Let\'s suppose Fiona is blue. Then she would have blue and green. Then by rule8, if blue and green, then smart. So Fiona would be smart. But do we have any constraints on Fiona\'s smartness?\n\nFiona is big and green. Are there any rules that connect being big with other attributes?\n\nWait, the other rules involving smart:\n\n- Rule7: If young and big, then smart.\n\n- Rule8: Blue and green → smart.\n\n- Rule9: Maybe blue and red → smart.\n\n- Also, rule4: If smart and not red, then not green. So if Fiona is smart and not red, she can\'t be green, but Fiona is green. So if Fiona is smart and not red, that would be a contradiction. Therefore, if Fiona is smart, she must be red. (Because the contrapositive: Green and smart → must be red.)\n\nSo if Fiona is blue and green (so smart via rule8), then she must also be red (because being smart and green requires her to be red). Therefore, Fiona would have to be red, blue, and green. Is that possible?\n\nBut being red would mean she\'s furry (rule1 and 2), so that\'s okay. Then, being red and green makes her blue via rule6, so that\'s consistent. So if Fiona is blue, then she must also be red, but that\'s okay. However, there\'s no rule that says she can\'t be red. The problem didn\'t state Fiona\'s color except that she\'s green and big. So maybe Fiona could be blue, but only if she\'s also red?\n\nWait, but let\'s see if there\'s a contradiction. Let\'s assume Fiona is blue. Then, since she\'s blue and green, she is smart (rule8). Since she i