ading to visiting the cow. Then, visiting the cow and seeing the cow (since it does see the cow) leads to visiting the bald eagle via statement 11. Then, visiting the bald eagle makes it nice and big. So yes, that\'s the chain.\n\nTherefore, I think the answer is A) True. But maybe I\'m missing something? Alternatively, perhaps the rabbit visiting the cat doesn\'t lead to visiting the rabbit because the "something" in statement 16 is a different entity. Wait no, statement 16 says if any entity X visits the cat, then X visits the rabbit. So if the rabbit (X) visits the cat, then X (the rabbit) must visit the rabbit. That\'s correct.\n\nHmm. I think the answer is A. But I\'m a bit uncertain because sometimes in these problems, you have to consider that the premises might not fully support it, but in this case, the chain seems solid. Alternatively, perhaps the problem is designed to have the answer as C) Unknown because the rabbit\'s size isn\'t directly stated, but according to the logical steps, it must be true. So unless there\'s a flaw in the reasoning, it\'s A. I\'ll go with A.\n'] 23%|██▎       | 136/600 [4:08:44<14:02:05, 108.89s/it]
