ow if something is visiting the mouse, we can't infer the mouse's size. \n\nPremise 10: If the dog is young and the dog doesn't visit the mouse, then the mouse doesn't visit the rabbit. But the rabbit is young (point6). But we don't know about the dog's age. Wait, the dog's age isn't mentioned except in premise 5 where the rabbit is young. The dog's youth isn't stated, so maybe the dog isn't young. But that's not directly relevant to the tiger's color. \n\nPremise 12: Since the tiger is green (point7), it must visit the dog (because any green thing visits the dog). So the tiger visits the dog. \n\nIs there any contradiction here? Let's see. The tiger is green, so it must visit the dog. The mouse is green, so it must also visit the dog. \n\nPremise 13: If something visits the rabbit and the rabbit is young (which it is), then that something is round. Since the rabbit is young, any visitor to the rabbit must be round. \n\nPremise 14: If something is round and visits the dog, then it chases the tiger. \n\nPremise 15: If something is big, then it visits the rabbit. \n\nBut none of these seem to affect the color of the tiger. Since the tiger's color is directly stated as green, the only possible answer is that the statement \"tiger is not green\" is false. Therefore, the correct answer should be B) False. \n\nWait, but let me check again. Maybe there's a trick here. Let me confirm that there are no other statements that could override the direct statement in point7. \n\nThe problem's first sentence says \"The dog does not eat the rabbit.\" Then \"the mouse eats the tiger.\" So the mouse is eating the tiger. Since the tiger is green (point7), and the mouse is green (point3). \n\nWait, but maybe there's an implication that if something is green and does some action, but I don't see anything that would negate the tiger's color. The only direct statement is that the tiger is green. \n\nTherefore, the answer must be B) False because the tiger is green, so saying it's not gree