ment "tiger is not green" would be false. So that would make the answer B) False. \n\nWait, but maybe there\'s some contradiction or some inference that could override this? Let me check again. \n\nWait, the problem might be trying to trick me. Let me make sure I didn\'t misread any of the premises. Let me go through again:\n\nPoint 7 clearly states "The tiger is green." So unless there\'s another statement that contradicts this, the answer should be B (False) because the tiger is indeed green, making the statement "tiger is not green" false. \n\nHowever, let\'s check the other statements to ensure there\'s no hidden contradiction. For example, maybe some implication leads to a different conclusion. Let\'s see.\n\nLooking at the conditional statements, maybe some of them could affect the color of the tiger. Let me check each relevant one.\n\nPoint 3 says the mouse is green. Point 7 says the tiger is green. \n\nPoint 12 says "If something is green then it visits the dog." So since the mouse and tiger are both green, they must visit the dog. \n\nBut does that affect their color? No, their color is given. \n\nPoint 11: If something is green and chases the rabbit, then the rabbit doesn\'t eat the mouse. The rabbit is young (point 6) and eats the dog (point5). The rabbit does not chase the tiger (point4), but does the rabbit get chased by anything? Not sure yet. \n\nWait, perhaps the tiger is green, so the statement in question is "tiger is not green" which would be false, so the answer is B. \n\nBut let me check all the implications again just in case. Let me see if any of the conditional statements could lead to a different conclusion. \n\nAnother angle: The problem asks if "The tiger is not green" is true, so if the original statement says the tiger is green, then the answer is False (so B is correct). Unless there is some hidden contradiction in the premises that would make the tiger not green, but I don\'t see that. \n\nWait, let me check the second premise: "The mouse eats the tiger." Then point 9 says: If something eats the tiger, then the tiger visits the mouse. So since the mouse eats the tiger (from premise 2), then according to premise 9, the tiger must visit the mouse. \n\nBut does that affect the color? Not directly. The color is already stated in premise 7 as green. \n\nAnother check: premise 8 says if something visits the mouse, then the mouse is big. But unless we know if something is visiting the mouse, we can\'t infer the mouse\'s size. \n\nPremise 10: If the dog is young and the dog doesn\'t visit the mouse, then the mouse doesn\'t visit the rabbit. But the rabbit is young (point6). But we don\'t know about the dog\'s age. Wait, the dog\'s age isn\'t mentioned except in premise 5 where the rabbit is young. The dog\'s youth isn\'t stated, so maybe the dog isn\'t young. But that\'s not directly relevant to the tiger\'s color. \n\nPremise 12: Since the tiger is green (point7), it must visit the dog (because any green thing visits the dog). So the tiger visits the dog. \n\nIs there any contradiction here? Let\'s see. The tiger is green, so it must visit the dog. The mouse is green, so it must also visit the dog. \n\nPremise 13: If something visits the rabbit and the rabbit is young (which it is), then that something is round. Since the rabbit is young, any visitor to the rabbit must be round. \n\nPremise 14: If something is round and visits the dog, then it chases the tiger. \n\nPremise 15: If something is big, then it visits the rabbit. \n\nBut none of these seem to affect the color of the tiger. Since the tiger\'s color is directly stated as green, the only possible answer is that the statement "tiger is not green" is false. Therefore, the correct answer should be B) False. \n\nWait, but let me check again. Maybe there\'s a trick here. Let me confirm that there are no other statements that could override the direct statement in point7. \n\nThe problem\'s first sentence says "The dog does not eat the rabbit." Then "the mouse eats the tiger." So the mouse is eating the tiger. Since the tiger is green (point7), and the mouse is green (point3). \n\nWait, but maybe there\'s an implication that if something is green and does some action, but I don\'t see anything that would negate the tiger\'s color. The only direct statement is that the tiger is green. \n\nTherefore, the answer must be B) False because the tiger is green, so saying it\'s not green is false.\n'] 23%|██▎       | 139/600 [4:13:51<12:29:18, 97.52s/it] 
