es Involving the Mouse or Eating:**\n   - **Rule 12:** If something is young and eats the tiger, it eats the dog. The mouse eats the tiger, but its age is unspecified. Without knowing if the mouse is young, this rule cannot confirm the mouse eats the dog.\n   - **Rule 13:** If something sees the mouse and eats the mouse, then the mouse sees the tiger. This is irrelevant because the mouse doesn\u2019t eat itself and the rule doesn\u2019t involve the dog.\n   - **Rule 10:** The tiger eats the dog, but this does not imply the mouse does the same. No transitive property is stated for \"eats.\"\n\n3. **Other Relationships:**\n   - The cat eats the dog (statement 4), and the tiger eats the dog (statement 10). However, these do not provide information about the mouse\u2019s actions toward the dog.\n   - The mouse\u2019s eating the tiger (statement 8) does not create an indirect link to the dog via any stated rules.\n\n4. **Absence of Contradiction:**\n   - There is no rule prohibiting the mouse from eating the dog. The absence of such a rule means the statement isn\u2019t inherently false.\n\n5. **Conclusion:**\n   - Since there\u2019s no direct evidence or applicable rule requiring the mouse to eat the dog, and no contradiction exists, the status of \"The mouse eats the dog\" remains **unknown**. \n\n**Answer:** C) Unknown",
