e any rule that would say if something eats the tiger, it can\'t eat the dog? Not that I can see. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the tiger eating the dog (statement 10) and the mouse eating the tiger (statement 8) could imply something. But unless there\'s a transitive relationship, which there isn\'t, that\'s not helpful. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the fact that the mouse eats the tiger (statement 8) and the tiger eats the dog (statement 10), so the mouse indirectly affects the dog, but that doesn\'t mean it directly eats it. \n\nSo since there\'s no direct or indirect evidence, the answer should be unknown. \n\nWait, but let me check if any of the conditionals could be triggered by the mouse\'s actions. For example, if the mouse sees the dog, does that do anything? \n\nStatement 14 says if something sees the mouse, then it is big. But seeing the dog is different. \n\nStatement 20: If something chases the cat, it sees the dog. Not relevant here. \n\nStatement 19: If tiger is green and big, then sees the dog. But we don\'t know if tiger is green. \n\nHmm. \n\nAnother angle: The question is "The mouse eats the dog." The context says "The mouse eats the tiger." There\'s no mention of the mouse eating the dog, so unless there\'s a rule that requires it, it\'s unknown. \n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is C) Unknown.\n'] 23%|██▎       | 140/600 [4:15:49<13:16:28, 103.89s/it]
