uiet, that would require him to be green, but since he isn\'t stated to be green, that might be impossible. Hence, maybe Gary cannot be quiet, making the statement false?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the fact that quiet people are kind (premise 10) doesn\'t help here. Wait, premise 10 is a one-way implication. So being quiet makes you kind, but being kind doesn\'t make you quiet.\n\nAlternatively, let\'s see if there\'s a way for Gary to be quiet without being green. Suppose he is quiet and kind. Then by premise 9, he must be green. Therefore, if he\'s quiet, he must be green. But since we don\'t have information that he is green, can we say that he can\'t be quiet? Because if he were quiet, he would have to be green, but there\'s no evidence of him being green. So that would mean that the only way for him to be quiet is if he is green, which we don\'t know. Therefore, perhaps it\'s unknown?\n\nWait, but maybe there\'s another angle. Let me check if there\'s any other rules that can link Gary\'s attributes to quietness. Let\'s see:\n\nLooking at premise 10 again: Quiet → Kind. Since Gary is kind, but that doesn\'t mean he\'s quiet. \n\nLooking at Fiona\'s case: Fiona is quiet, green, and cold. So she\'s an example of someone who is quiet and kind? Wait, no, Fiona is not stated to be kind. Wait, let me check. Fiona\'s attributes are cold, green, quiet, round. The context says "Fiona is cold. Fiona is green. Fiona is quiet. Fiona is round." So Fiona is not mentioned as kind. But according to premise 9, Quiet ∧ Kind → Green. Fiona is quiet and green, but she isn\'t stated to be kind. So that\'s okay because premise 9 says that if someone is quiet and kind, then they must be green. But Fiona isn\'t kind, so the antecedent isn\'t triggered. So that\'s okay.\n\nWait, but premise 10 says Quiet people are kind. So Fiona is quiet, so she must be kind. Wait, that\'s a problem! Because according to premise 10: Quiet → Kind. Fiona is quiet, so she must be kind. But in the given cont