t not the squirrel. The cow visits the squirrel (statement 6), but that's a visit. So maybe no one chases the squirrel, so that condition isn't triggered.\n\nBack to the main path:\n\nThe conclusion seems to be that the rabbit is red, so the statement \"The rabbit is not red\" is false. So answer B. But wait, let me check again for any other conditions that might affect the rabbit's color.\n\nLooking at statement 14: If the squirrel chases the cow and the cow is round then the cow is young. The cow is already stated as round (statement 5) and the bear is young (statement 3). But the squirrel's action: does the squirrel chase the cow? Statement 1 says the bear chases the cow, but the squirrel's actions: the squirrel eats the cow (statement 9), and visits the rabbit (10). There's no mention of the squirrel chasing the cow. So the antecedent of statement 14 is not met, so it doesn't affect anything.\n\nAnother possible angle: The rabbit visits the bear (statement 8). But that doesn't link to color.\n\nWait, the problem also includes \"All red people are big.\" Since the cow is red, it must be big. The rabbit, if it were red, would also have to be big. But the rabbit is round (statement 7), but that's a different attribute. Roundness vs. size (big). Not sure if roundness implies anything about being big. The problem doesn't say that round things are big, so maybe the rabbit could be red and round but not necessarily big? Wait, no\u2014if the rabbit is red, then by statement 18 it must be big. So the rabbit's roundness is separate. But that doesn't affect our conclusion here.\n\nWait another thing: The squirrel eats the cow (statement 9). Then, in statement 16: If someone eats the cow and the cow eats the rabbit, then the cow chases the rabbit. Wait, the first part is \"someone eats the cow\"\u2014the squirrel does that. But the second part requires the cow to eat the rabbit. But do we know if the cow eats the rabbit? The problem doesn't state that. The bear eats the cow