ig." Since the cow is red, it must be big. The rabbit, if it were red, would also have to be big. But the rabbit is round (statement 7), but that\'s a different attribute. Roundness vs. size (big). Not sure if roundness implies anything about being big. The problem doesn\'t say that round things are big, so maybe the rabbit could be red and round but not necessarily big? Wait, no—if the rabbit is red, then by statement 18 it must be big. So the rabbit\'s roundness is separate. But that doesn\'t affect our conclusion here.\n\nWait another thing: The squirrel eats the cow (statement 9). Then, in statement 16: If someone eats the cow and the cow eats the rabbit, then the cow chases the rabbit. Wait, the first part is "someone eats the cow"—the squirrel does that. But the second part requires the cow to eat the rabbit. But do we know if the cow eats the rabbit? The problem doesn\'t state that. The bear eats the cow (statement 2), the squirrel eats the cow (statement 9). There\'s no mention of the cow eating the rabbit. Therefore, the antecedent of statement 16 is only partially met (someone eats the cow, but the cow doesn\'t eat the rabbit). So the conclusion (cow chases rabbit) isn\'t necessarily required by statement 16. But we already have another reason (statement 12) that the cow chases the rabbit because the cow is big and red. So that\'s separate. So statement 16\'s condition isn\'t met, so it doesn\'t add anything here.\n\nTherefore, the main line of reasoning holds: the cow chases the rabbit, so the rabbit must be red. Therefore, the statement "The rabbit is not red" is false. So the answer is B (False).\n\nWait a second, but let me check if there\'s any other way the rabbit could be not red. Suppose that the cow doesn\'t actually chase the rabbit. Is there a way that the cow being big and red could be excluded?\n\nWait, the cow is red (statement 4), so by statement 18, it must be big. Therefore, the cow is big and red, so statement 12 applies, so the cow must chase the rabbit. There\'s no way around that unless there\'s a contradiction. But the problem doesn\'t state that the cow doesn\'t chase the rabbit. The only actions of the cow mentioned are visiting the squirrel (statement 6) and being chased by the bear (statement 1), being eaten by the bear and squirrel. So the cow\'s action of chasing the rabbit is inferred via the conditional. Since the antecedent (being big and red) is true, the consequent (chasing the rabbit) must be true. Therefore, the cow does chase the rabbit, leading to the rabbit being red via statement 17. \n\nTherefore, the rabbit must be red, so the statement is false. So answer B.\n'] 25%|██▌       | 152/600 [4:34:21<12:42:36, 102.14s/it]
