ss another rule implies that the tiger sees the mouse, which I don't see. \n\nAlternatively, the cat chases the mouse (statement2), so maybe the cat sees the mouse? But there's no rule that says chasing implies seeing. Unless there's a rule like \"If something chases X, then it sees X.\" Wait, the problem doesn't state that. So we can't assume that. \n\nWait, statement 20: \"If something chases the cat, then it sees the dog.\" So if someone chases the cat, then they see the dog. But who is chasing the cat? The given facts don't mention anything chasing the cat except maybe the tiger? Wait, the cat chases the tiger (statement3), so the cat is the chaser here. The tiger is being chased by the cat, but is the tiger chasing the cat? Not stated. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the dog chases the cat? From statement15: If something chases the dog, then the dog chases the cat. So if someone is chasing the dog, then the dog would chase the cat. But unless there's a chaser of the dog, that doesn't happen. \n\nHmm, this is getting a bit tangled. Let's try to approach this systematically. The question is whether the tiger sees the dog. To confirm that, we need to see if any of the given premises or derived conclusions lead to that.\n\nLooking at statement 19 again: If the tiger is green and big, then it sees the dog. Since we don't know if the tiger is green or big, this conditional doesn't give us a definite conclusion unless we can infer those attributes. \n\nIs there any way to determine if the tiger is big? Let's see. The dog is big (statement5). Statement14: If something sees the mouse, then it is big. Suppose the tiger sees the mouse, then the tiger must be big. Does the tiger see the mouse? The mouse eats the tiger (statement8), but does that imply the mouse is near the tiger? Not necessarily seeing. The mouse doesn't see the tiger (statement9). So the tiger might see the mouse? Not sure. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the cat sees the mouse because it chases it. But chasing doesn't