t itself, which might be a contradiction unless it\'s allowed. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem doesn\'t consider an entity visiting itself, but since it\'s not stated, we can\'t assume that. So if the bear sees the dog, then by 21, the bear must visit the bear, which would require that the bear sees the bear (from 17\'s condition, since visiting implies seeing). So the bear would have to see itself, which again is possible but perhaps not required. But since the problem doesn\'t prohibit self-visiting or self-seeing, maybe that\'s acceptable. But this seems like a possible contradiction or infinite loop, but I\'m not sure. \n\nAlternatively, maybe my approach here is overcomplicating. Let\'s see if there\'s another angle. \n\nLooking at the question again: Is the statement "The lion does not visit the bear" true, false, or unknown?\n\nTo determine this, we need to see if there\'s any information that would make it necessarily true or false, otherwise it\'s unknown.\n\nFirst, check if there\'s any direct information about the lion visiting the bear. The context doesn\'t mention it. So maybe it\'s unknown unless we can infer it.\n\nAlternatively, let\'s see if there\'s a way to prove that the lion must visit the bear, making the statement false, or that it cannot, making it true, or neither.\n\nSuppose the lion does visit the bear. Then by 17, the lion sees the bear. Then by 18, the bear sees the dog. \n\nNow, if the bear sees the dog, then by statement 21, the bear must visit the bear (since the bear is the "something" that sees the dog). So visiting the bear (itself) would then require, by 17, that the bear sees itself. But there\'s no contradiction in that unless the problem prohibits it. Since it\'s not prohibited, perhaps that\'s allowed. So the bear would have to visit itself, but that\'s possible. However, this creates a chain where the bear visiting itself would then require another condition, but maybe that\'s okay. \n\nAlternatively, this seems like a