l that holds. \n\nSo Erin is furry, young, blue, quiet, big, smart. \n\nNow, back to the original question: Is Erin not red? \n\nFrom rule 5a, blue people are red. Since Erin is blue, she must be red. Therefore, the statement "Erin is not red" would be false. So the answer should be B) False. \n\nWait a second, but let me check if there\'s any possible way that Erin could not be red. The only way that could happen is if the premise that blue people are red is somehow overridden. But the problem states all the given rules as premises, so we have to take them as true. Therefore, since Erin is blue, she must be red. Hence the statement that she is not red is false. \n\nTherefore, the answer should be B) False. But wait, let me check again if there\'s a contradiction here. Because Erin is quiet and big, so she\'s furry. Then furry implies young. Young implies blue (so that\'s consistent), and young also implies quiet (she is). \n\nWait, but let me also check if there\'s any direct statement conflicting. The context says "Erin is blue" so that\'s given, so blue → red. So Erin must be red. Therefore, the answer is B) False. \n\nHmm, but maybe I missed something. Let me go through each step again. \n\nFirst, Erin\'s attributes from context: big, blue, quiet, smart. \n\nFrom the rules:\n- Quiet → big (which holds for Erin).\n- Since quiet and big → furry (rule 5d). So Erin must be furry. \n- Furry → young (rule 5i). So Erin must be young. \n- Young → blue (rule 5e). She\'s already blue, so that\'s okay. \n- Young → quiet (rule 5f). She\'s already quiet. \n\nThus, Erin must also be young and furry. \n\nNow, blue → red (rule 5a), so Erin is red. \n\nTherefore, the statement "Erin is not red" is false. So the answer is B) False. \n\nWait, but let me check if there\'s a way the conclusion could be unknown. Is there any ambiguity in the rules?\n\nThe rules are all given as absolute statements, so we can derive Erin must be red. Therefore, the answer should be B) False. \n\nAlter