d. The tiger eats the cow. The cow is being eaten by BE and T. So unless another entity eats the tiger, premise 9 would not apply yet.\n\nPremise 10: If something is red, then it eats the tiger. Since BE is red, that would mean BE must eat the tiger. Wait, but BE is already eating the cow. So BE would also eat the tiger. Hmm, that\'s a new fact. Wait, premise 10 says "If something is red, then it eats the tiger." So any red entity must eat the tiger. Since BE is red, so BE must eat the tiger. Therefore, BE eats the tiger as well as the cow. But is there any contradiction here? Let\'s note that.\n\nSo BE eats cow (1) and tiger (from premise 10 because BE is red). Also, BE must eat itself (from 14, since it\'s red). So BE has three eat relationships: cow, tiger, and itself? That\'s a bit odd, but maybe possible.\n\nPremise 11: If something sees the bald eagle, then it eats the cow. \n\nLooking at the cow: the cow sees the BE (from 4). Therefore, by premise 11, the cow must eat the cow? Wait, because if something (the cow) sees the BE, then it must eat the cow. Wait, the "it" here refers to the cow. So the cow must eat the cow. That\'s a reflexive relationship. Hmm, but is that possible? The problem might allow that, but maybe that\'s a key point here. Let me note that the cow eats itself as a result of premise 11. \n\nWait, let me check again. The cow sees the BE (premise 4). Therefore, by premise 11 (if something sees BE, it eats the cow), so the cow must eat the cow. So the cow eats itself. Okay, that\'s an important point.\n\nMoving on:\n\nPremise 12: If something is green and it needs the dog, then it needs the bald eagle.\n\nWe don\'t know if anything is green yet, except possibly other entities. The BE is red, the cow\'s color isn\'t mentioned, the dog\'s color? Not specified yet. The tiger\'s color? Let\'s see.\n\nPremise 13: If something sees the tiger, then it needs the bald eagle.\n\nPremise 14: If something is red, then it eats the bald eagle. We already co