t 12) and big (from conditional 20 and fact 5), so by conditional 21, "Green, big things are young," so the tiger must be young. Wait, but the cat is already young (fact 3). But the problem doesn\'t say that only the cat is young. So the tiger is also young? That\'s okay.\n\nNow, let\'s see if the tiger sees the cat. The question is whether that\'s true or not. Let\'s see if any conditionals can lead us to that conclusion.\n\nLooking at the facts, the tiger chases the cat (fact 11). Now, let\'s see if any conditionals involve chasing the cat. Let\'s check conditional 16: "If something is big and it chases the cat then the cat likes the squirrel." Hmm, but the tiger chases the cat (fact 11), and we just established that the tiger is big (from conditional 20 and fact5). So substituting into conditional 16: The tiger is big and it chases the cat, so that would mean the cat likes the squirrel. But is there any information about the cat liking the squirrel? The original facts don\'t mention the cat\'s feelings towards the squirrel, so that might be a conclusion we can make here. Wait, but the problem says to answer based on the given information, so if this conditional is true, then the cat must like the squirrel. But since that\'s not stated, perhaps that\'s an inference we can make. Wait, but maybe that\'s not directly relevant to the question about the tiger seeing the cat. Let\'s see.\n\nAlternatively, looking at conditional 19: "If something is cold and it chases the cat then it sees the cat." So if an entity is cold and it chases the cat, then it sees the cat. Since the tiger chases the cat (fact 11), if the tiger is cold, then it would see the cat. Now, is the tiger cold? Well, from conditional 22: "Big things are cold." Since the tiger is big (as we concluded earlier), then the tiger is cold. Therefore, the tiger is cold and it chases the cat (fact 11), so applying conditional 19, the tiger must see the cat. Therefore, the tiger does see the cat, so the statement