e dog, then the squirrel must also see the cat. Wait, but does that matter for the cat\'s color?\n\nHmm. Also, the dog visits the cat. So the dog sees the cat? Wait, the dog visits the cat. Does visiting imply seeing? Not sure, but maybe the visiting action might lead to seeing, but the problem doesn\'t say that. Wait, the original statements don\'t mention that visiting implies seeing. So the dog visits the cat, but does that mean the dog sees the cat? The problem says "the dog sees the bald eagle" but not necessarily the cat. Wait, unless from visiting, but I think we can\'t assume that. So the dog\'s seeing the cat would have to be from another rule.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the cat seeing the dog? Wait, the cat\'s actions: the cat visits the bald eagle, but not sure if that implies seeing the bald eagle. The bald eagle is already stated to see the squirrel, but that\'s separate.\n\nLet me see statement 3: If something visits the squirrel, then the squirrel visits the dog. The cat visits the bald eagle, so that\'s not visiting the squirrel. The dog visits the cat, so that\'s not visiting the squirrel. The squirrel visits the bald eagle, so that\'s visiting the bald eagle, not the squirrel. So maybe that rule isn\'t triggered here. Unless another entity is visiting the squirrel. The squirrel itself can\'t visit itself? Hmm.\n\nLooking at the given facts, the squirrel needs the cat and visits the bald eagle. So maybe the squirrel doesn\'t visit anyone else. So maybe statement 3 isn\'t directly applicable here.\n\nNow, back to the cat\'s color. Let\'s see if there\'s any rule that links needing or visiting something to being green. The bald eagle is green because it\'s given. The cat\'s color isn\'t stated directly except maybe via the rules.\n\nLooking at statement 6 again: If something sees the cat, then that something is green. Suppose the cat itself sees the cat, but that\'s a bit circular. Alternatively, maybe another entity sees the cat, so that entity must be 