nGiven statement 10: If something eats the squirrel, then the squirrel is green.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nLet P be "something eats the squirrel"\n\nQ be "the squirrel is green"\n\nSo P → Q must hold as per the given.\n\nIf the squirrel eats itself, then P is true (since "something" could be the squirrel), so P is true. Then Q must also be true for the implication to hold. But the squirrel is red (so not green). Therefore, Q is false. Therefore, the implication P→Q would be false, which contradicts the given premise that statement 10 is true. Therefore, to avoid this contradiction, the scenario where P is true must be impossible. Therefore, the squirrel cannot eat itself. Therefore, "The squirrel does not eat the squirrel" is necessarily true. Therefore, the answer is A) True.\n\nHmm, that makes sense. The key is that the implications must hold, so if assuming the squirrel eats itself would make an existing implication false, then that assumption must be false. Therefore, the statement is indeed true.\n\nLet me check other statements to see if there\'s any other info that might affect this conclusion.\n\nLooking at statement 12: If something is blue, then it chases the squirrel. Since the squirrel is blue (from 6 and 13), then the squirrel would chase itself? Wait, but the squirrel is blue, so according to statement 12, the squirrel (being blue) must chase the squirrel (itself). So that would mean the squirrel chases itself. But does that matter here? The question is about eating, not chasing. So maybe that\'s another implication, but the chasing doesn\'t directly affect the eating.\n\nStatement 7 says the squirrel visits the bald eagle. Then statement 16 says if something visits the bald eagle and is not round, then it visits the lion. Since the squirrel is round (from above), then the "and it is not round" part would be false, so the antecedent of 16 is false, so the implication holds regardless. So that\'s okay.\n\nAnother point: The bald eagle is involved in chasing 