iting the bald eagle, not the rabbit. The dog? The dog is visited by the rabbit (statement 13 says rabbit visits dog). Wait, so the rabbit is visited by the dog? Wait, no. The rabbit visits the dog, so the dog is being visited by the rabbit. So the rabbit is the one initiating the visit. So the entities that visit the rabbit would be... Hmm, are there any? Let's see:\n\nLooking through all the statements, the bald eagle visits the rabbit (statement 4). The lion does not visit the rabbit; the lion visits the bald eagle and the dog (statements 10,11). The dog is visited by the rabbit (statement 13). The rabbit also visits the lion (statement 14). So the only visitor to the rabbit is the bald eagle? Wait no: the bald eagle visits the rabbit, so the rabbit is visited by the bald eagle. Wait, no. If the bald eagle visits the rabbit, then the rabbit is being visited by the bald eagle, so the visitor is the bald eagle. The rabbit is visited by the bald eagle. So the \"something\" in statement 20 would be the bald eagle. Therefore, since the bald eagle visits the rabbit (statement 4), then according to statement 20, the rabbit needs the bald eagle. Wait, but the rabbit needing the bald eagle? Let's see if that's stated.\n\nLooking at the given statements, the bald eagle needs the lion (statement 3). The lion needs the dog (statement 8) and the rabbit (statement 9). The dog's needs? Not mentioned except that the lion needs the dog. The rabbit's needs? Not mentioned except in the conditionals.\n\nSo according to statement 20, since the bald eagle visits the rabbit, then the rabbit needs the bald eagle. So that would mean the rabbit needs the bald eagle. But the bald eagle already needs the lion. So that's a new fact derived from statement 20. So now we can add that the rabbit needs the bald eagle.\n\nNow, moving on. Let's see other statements. Statement 19: \"If the rabbit visits the lion and the rabbit needs the dog then the dog is round.\" The rabbit does visit the lion (statement 14), so the first part is true. Now, does the rabbit need the dog? The rabbit's needs: we know the rabbit is round (statement 12), and from statement 20 we have that the rabbit needs the bald eagle. But does it need the dog? Let's see. The lion needs the dog (statement 8), but that's the lion's need. The rabbit's needs are not stated except in the conditional here. So unless the rabbit needs the dog, we can't apply this. But we don't have information that the rabbit needs the dog, so maybe that part is unknown? Hmm, but maybe there's another way.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps from another statement, we can infer that the rabbit needs the dog. Let's see. Let's check statement 21: \"If something visits the lion and the lion needs the dog then it needs the dog.\" The \"it\" here refers to the something that visits the lion. Let's parse that. So, if an entity (let's call it X) visits the lion, and the lion needs the dog (which is true, from statement 8), then X needs the dog. \n\nSo any entity that visits the lion must need the dog. \n\nWho visits the lion? The rabbit (statement 14: the rabbit visits the lion), and the bald eagle? Wait, no, the bald eagle visits the rabbit (statement 4). The lion is visited by the rabbit (statement 14) and perhaps others? Let's check:\n\nLooking at all the visit statements:\n\n- Bald eagle visits rabbit (4)\n- Lion visits bald eagle (10), visits dog (11)\n- Rabbit visits dog (13), visits lion (14)\n- Dog sees the lion (5) but that's a \"sees\" not a \"visits\"\n\nSo entities visiting the lion are the rabbit (14). The lion is visited by the rabbit. So for the rabbit, since it visits the lion, and the lion needs the dog (statement 8), then according to statement 21, the rabbit (X) must need the dog. Therefore, the rabbit needs the dog. \n\nSo from statement 21, since the rabbit visits the lion, and the lion needs the dog, the rabbit must need the dog. Therefore, the rabbit does need the dog. \n\nTherefore, going back to statement 19, since the rabbit visits the lion (true) and the rabbit needs the dog (now established as true via statement 21), then the consequent of statement 19 is triggered, meaning the dog is round. \n\nSo the dog is round (from statement 19). But wait, statement 12 says the rabbit is round, and statement 15: \"If something is round then it is rough.\" Therefore, the rabbit is rough (since it's round). Also, the dog is now round (from statement 19), so the dog is also rough. \n\nNow, let's see statement 17: \"If something is rough then it visits the lion.\" So since the rabbit is rough (from being round), it must visit the lion. But the rabbit does visit the lion (statement 14), so that's consistent. The dog is rough (since it's round), so the dog must visit the lion. Does the dog visit the lion? Looking back, statement 11 says the lion visits the dog, which is the lion going to the dog, but the dog visiting the lion would be the dog going to the lion. The dog's visits are not directly stated except in the conditional here. Wait, statement 11 says the lion visits the dog, but that's the lion going to the dog. The dog's own actions: the dog sees the lion (statement 5). But visiting vs seeing? Not sure if those are the same. \n\nWait, statement 17 says if something is rough, then it visits the lion. Since the dog is rough, it must visit the lion. So the dog must visit the lion. But is there any statement that says the dog visits the lion? Let me check. The given statements mention the lion visiting the dog (statement 11), but the dog visiting the lion is not stated unless inferred. \n\nTherefore, according to statement 17, since the dog is rough, the dog must visit the lion. So that's a new fact: the dog visits the lion. \n\nNow, moving on. Let's see statement 18: \"If the dog needs the rabbit and the rabbit sees the bald eagle then the bald eagle sees the dog.\" \n\nWe need to see if the antecedent is true. The dog needs the rabbit? The lion needs the dog and the rabbit (state