 10 (furry \u2192 green), Anne must be green. \n\nIf Anne is green, then from fact 11 (green \u2192 white), she must be white. Therefore, Anne is white. The question is asking if \"Anne is not white\" is true. If she is white, then the statement \"Anne is not white\" would be false, so the answer would be B) False. \n\nWait, but let me check again to make sure I didn't skip anything.\n\nWait, let me go through each step again carefully.\n\nAnne is smart \u2192 young (from 9). Then young \u2192 cold (from 8). So Anne is cold. Then cold \u2192 furry (from 15). So Anne is furry. Then, being furry, she must be green (from 10). Green \u2192 white (from 11). Therefore, Anne is white. Therefore, the statement \"Anne is not white\" is false, so the answer should be B) False. \n\nBut let me check if there are any contradictions or exceptions. For example, is there any condition that could make Anne not green even if she is furry? The problem states \"If someone is furry then they are green,\" so furry implies green. So no, that's a one-way implication. So if someone is furry, they must be green. So Anne being furry would require her to be green, so that holds. Then green leads to white. So that's solid.\n\nWait, but let me also check the other rules. For instance, fact 14 says green people are furry. That's the converse of fact 10? Wait, fact 10 is furry \u2192 green, and fact 14 is green \u2192 furry. Wait, that's interesting. So together, those two statements (10 and 14) mean that furry and green are equivalent? Because if furry implies green and green implies furry, then they are biconditional. So being furry is equivalent to being green. So anyone who is furry is green, and anyone who is green is furry. \n\nSo that's an important point. So in that case, if Anne is furry, she must be green, and since she's green, she must be furry. So that's consistent. So that doesn't create any problem. \n\nAdditionally, fact 12 says if someone is green and cold, then they are white. But since green already implies white via fact 11, this is redundant, but maybe just emphasizing. So that doesn't change anything.\n\nNow, what about the other people? Let's see if there's any conflicting info that might affect Anne's status. For example, Harry is green (fact7), not cold (fact6). So Harry is green, so he must be furry (from 14). Also, since Harry is green and not cold, does that affect anything? The fact 13 says if Harry is green and young, then he must be furry. But Harry's youth isn't established here. Wait, since Harry is not cold (fact6), and all young people are cold (fact8), so if someone is young, they must be cold. Therefore, if Harry is not cold, he cannot be young. Because if he were young, he would have to be cold, but he isn't. So Harry is not young. \n\nTherefore, Harry being green and not young, so the antecedent of fact13 (Harry is green and young) is false, so the implication holds but doesn't require anything. So that's okay.\n\nBut back to Anne. All steps seem to lead to Anne being white. Therefore, the statement \"Anne is not white\" is false. So the answer should be B) False. \n\nWait, but let me check another angle. Let me see if there's a possibility that Anne isn't green. Wait, she is furry (from being cold, via being young from being smart), and since furry implies green, she must be green. So no way around that. Therefore, she must be white. Therefore, the statement \"Anne is not white\" is false. So the answer is B.\n</think>\n\nThe problem requires determining whether the statement \"Anne is not white\" is true, false, or unknown based on the provided premises. Here's the step-by-step analysis:\n\n1. **Anne's Attributes**:  \n   - Anne is **smart** (given).  \n   - From the rule *\"If someone is smart, they are young\"*, Anne must be **young**.  \n   - From *\"All young people are cold\"*, being young implies Anne is **cold**.  \n   - From *\"All cold people are furry\"*, being cold means Anne is **furry**.  \n   - From *\"If someone is furry, they are green\"*, being furry means Anne is **green**.  \n   - From *\"Green people are white\"*, being green means Anne is **white**.  \n\n2. **Conclusion**:  \n   Since Anne is white, the statement *\"Anne is not white\"* is **false**.  \n\n**Answer**: B) False",
