apply to him.\n\n3. If something is nice, then it is big. Bob is big, but is he nice? The context doesn\'t say Bob is nice, so that\'s okay.\n\n4. If something is kind, then it is nice. Bob isn\'t kind, so that\'s fine.\n\n5. If something is rough, then it is white. Bob isn\'t stated to be rough, so no issue.\n\n6. All big and nice things are rough. Bob is big, but unless he is nice, this doesn\'t apply. Since he isn\'t stated as nice, okay.\n\n7. All white and blue things are cold. Bob is white, blue, and cold, so that\'s good.\n\nSo all of Bob\'s attributes are consistent with the rules. Therefore, the direct statement that Bob is blue is correct. So the question is asking if "Bob is not blue" is true, which would be the opposite, so it\'s false. Therefore, the answer should be B) False.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Is there any possibility that the rules could override the direct statement? For example, if a rule requires that being blue must also satisfy another condition that Bob doesn\'t meet?\n\nLooking at rule 7 again: All white and blue things are cold. Bob is white, blue, and cold, so that\'s okay. So no problem there.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a rule that says if something is blue and something else, then it must be something else, but Bob doesn\'t satisfy that? Let me see.\n\nThe rules don\'t seem to have any that would negate Bob\'s blueness. Since the context directly states Bob is blue, unless there\'s a contradiction, the answer is B.\n\nHmm, maybe I\'m missing something? Let me check the other individuals for any possible implications.\n\nLooking at Dave: cold, kind, nice. Since Dave is kind, by rule 4, he must be nice, which he is. Also, since he\'s big and kind? Wait, is he big? The context says Dave is cold, kind, nice. It doesn\'t say he\'s big. Wait, the second rule says all big, kind things are nice. Since Dave is kind and nice, does that mean he must be big? Wait, no. The second rule says if something is big and kind, then it\'s n