le sees the cat.\n\n5. Therefore, by statement 13, since the bald eagle (something) sees the cat, then the cat must be big.\n\nSo the cat is big. Okay, that\'s an important inference.\n\nNow, going back to statement 18: the tiger is big, so it must chase the tiger (itself). So that would mean the tiger chases itself, which would make the statement "The tiger chases the tiger" true. So option A?\n\nBut wait, let\'s check another angle. Statement 17 says: "If something chases the tiger and it sees the cat then the tiger is big." Since the tiger is big, this is a true statement regardless. But if the tiger chases itself and also sees the cat, then that\'s okay because the tiger is big. But does the tiger see the cat?\n\nThe tiger\'s seeing the cat: The tiger sees the squirrel (statement 10). Does the tiger see the cat? Let\'s see.\n\nIf the tiger is big (which it is), then by statement 14, it must see the bald eagle. Then, by statement 19, if it sees the bald eagle, then it must see the cat. Therefore, the tiger sees the cat.\n\nWait, yes. Let me see:\n\n1. Tiger is big (statement 8).\n\n2. Therefore, by statement 14, the tiger sees the bald eagle.\n\n3. Therefore, by statement 19, since the tiger sees the bald eagle, it must see the cat.\n\nTherefore, the tiger sees the cat. So that\'s established.\n\nTherefore, if the tiger chases the tiger (itself) and sees the cat (which it does), then according to statement 17, the tiger is big, which is true. So no problem there.\n\nAnother check: Statement 12 says if something likes the tiger and it does not chase the cat, then the cat is not big. But since we just concluded the cat is big, that means that any such "something" that likes the tiger must chase the cat. But do we know if anything likes the tiger?\n\nLooking at the given statements:\n\n- The squirrel does not like the tiger (statement 7).\n\n- The bald eagle likes the cat (statement 2).\n\n- The squirrel likes the cat (statement 6).\n\nSo, does anyone like the tiger