he first part, i.e., the chaser. So "if X chases the dog, then X is round." So following that pattern, in statement 13, "If something chases the mouse and it is round then the mouse is kind" would mean: If X chases the mouse and X is round, then the mouse is kind. So the "it" refers to the chaser. That makes sense with the structure of statement 18. So that\'s probably the correct interpretation.\n\nTherefore, statement 13: If X chases the mouse and X is round, then the mouse is kind.\n\nNow, from statement 4, the lion chases the mouse. And from earlier, we inferred that the lion is round (because the lion chases the dog, so by statement 18, the lion is round). Therefore, substituting X with the lion, since the lion chases the mouse and the lion is round, then according to statement 13, the mouse is kind.\n\nSo, mouse is kind. Now, statement 20 says: "If something is kind then it chases the dog." So if the mouse is kind, then the mouse must chase the dog. But do we have any information that the mouse chases the dog?\n\nLooking back, the given context doesn\'t mention the mouse chasing the dog. The only chasing involving the mouse is the lion chasing the mouse (statement 4). The mouse sees the tiger (statement 8), but not chasing. Therefore, if the mouse is kind, then it must chase the dog. But there\'s no statement that says the mouse chases the dog. Therefore, this would be a contradiction unless the mouse isn\'t kind, or unless the mouse does chase the dog.\n\nWait, but according to our deduction, the mouse is kind (from statements 4, 18, 13). Therefore, the mouse must chase the dog (statement 20). But is there any contradiction here?\n\nThe problem is, the original statements don\'t say that the mouse doesn\'t chase the dog. So maybe it\'s possible that the mouse does chase the dog, but it\'s not mentioned. However, the question is about the mouse\'s roundness, not about chasing.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there\'s a way for the mouse not to be kind. Wait, but acc