ust be green. So T is green.\n\nThen, rule 18 says green things are young. So T must be young. But wait, is the tiger young? The given statements don't say that. The squirrel is young (statement7), but the tiger's age isn't mentioned. Wait, the only mention of age is the squirrel being young, so the tiger's age is unknown unless inferred.\n\nWait, but according to rule18: if something is green, it is young. Since T is green (from needing the squirrel, which requires being green), then T must be young. So the tiger is young. \n\nNow, rule17 says if something is young, then it needs the tiger. So since T is young (from being green and rule18), then T must need the tiger. Wait, that's a loop here. Let me clarify:\n\n1. T is big (because it visited bald eagle)\n2. So T needs S (rule14)\n3. Therefore T is green (rule16)\n4. Green implies young (rule18), so T is young\n5. Since T is young, then by rule17, T must need the tiger (i.e., T needs T)\n\nWait, that's a contradiction? Wait, let me check again:\n\nStep 5: If something is young, it needs the tiger. Since T is now determined to be young (from being green), then T must need the tiger. The \"tiger\" here is the same as T. So that would mean T needs T. But the question is asking if \"The tiger does NOT need the tiger\" is true. So if according to this reasoning, T must need itself, then the statement \"T does not need T\" would be false. Hence, the answer would be B) False.\n\nBut hold on, is this reasoning valid? Let me go through each step again carefully to check for errors.\n\nStarting over:\n\n- Tiger visits bald eagle (statement12). By rule13: if visits bald eagle \u2192 big. So T is big.\n\n- Since T is big, rule14: big \u2192 needs squirrel. So T needs S.\n\n- Rule16: if needs squirrel \u2192 green. So T is green.\n\n- Rule18: green things are young \u2192 T is young.\n\n- Rule17: if something is young \u2192 needs the tiger. Since T is young, T must need the tiger (i.e., T needs T).\n\nThus, according to these