If something needs the squirrel, then it is green.\n17. If something is young, then it needs the tiger.\n18. Green things are young.\n19. If something is big and it visits the lion, then the lion needs the squirrel.\n20. If the squirrel is big and the squirrel does not like the tiger, then the tiger is not big.\n\nThe question is about whether "The tiger does not need the tiger" is true, false, or unknown. Let\'s denote the tiger as T for simplicity. So the statement is "¬(T needs T)".\n\nFirst, I need to see if any of the given statements directly or indirectly address whether the tiger needs itself. Since the question is about the tiger\'s need towards itself, maybe there\'s a rule that could lead to that conclusion or negate it.\n\nLet me go through the statements one by one and see what can be inferred about the tiger\'s needs.\n\nStarting with the basic facts about the tiger:\n\nFrom statement 11: The tiger is cold.\nStatement 12: The tiger visits the bald eagle.\n\nSo, since the tiger visits the bald eagle (statement 12), applying rule 13 (If something visits the bald eagle, then it is big), so the tiger is big. Therefore, T is big.\n\nNow, using rule 14: If something is big, then it needs the squirrel. Since T is big (from above), that means the tiger needs the squirrel. So "T needs Squirrel".\n\nBut the question is about whether the tiger needs itself, not the squirrel. Hmm, so that\'s a different point. Let\'s see if there\'s another rule that could lead to the tiger needing itself.\n\nLooking at other rules:\n\nRule 17: If something is young, then it needs the tiger. The squirrel is young (statement 7), so by rule 17, the squirrel needs the tiger. So "Squirrel needs T".\n\nRule 19: If something is big and visits the lion, then the lion needs the squirrel. The tiger is big (from earlier) and does the tiger visit the lion? Wait, the lion visits the tiger (statement 5), but does the tiger visit the lion? Not directly stated. The tiger\'s visits are mentioned in statements 12 (to bald eagle) and maybe others? Let me check. The given visits are:\n\n- Bald eagle visits lion (2)\n- Lion visits tiger (5)\n- Squirrel visits bald eagle (10)\n- Tiger visits bald eagle (12)\n\nSo the tiger\'s visits are only to the bald eagle. Therefore, the tiger does not visit the lion. So the first part of rule 19 requires "something is big and visits the lion". The tiger is big but doesn\'t visit the lion, so that part doesn\'t apply. Therefore, rule 19 doesn\'t affect the tiger needing itself.\n\nLooking at rule 20: If the squirrel is big and the squirrel does not like the tiger, then the tiger is not big. The squirrel\'s age is young, but is it big? The squirrel\'s visits: it visits the bald eagle (statement 10). Since visiting the bald eagle makes it big (rule 13), the squirrel must be big. Wait, because the squirrel visits the bald eagle, so by rule 13, the squirrel is big. Wait, the squirrel\'s visit to the bald eagle (statement 10) would trigger rule 13, so the squirrel is big. Therefore, squirrel is big.\n\nBut the squirrel is also young (statement 7). So, squirrel is both young and big. Now, rule 17 says if something is young, then it needs the tiger. So the squirrel needs the tiger (as mentioned before). \n\nNow, back to rule 20. Since the squirrel is big (from visiting the bald eagle), and the squirrel does not like the tiger? Wait, statement 8 says the squirrel likes the tiger. So the second part of rule 20\'s condition is "the squirrel does not like the tiger", but the squirrel actually does like the tiger (statement 8). Therefore, the antecedent of rule 20 is false (since both parts of the "if" must be true: squirrel is big AND squirrel does not like tiger). Since the squirrel does like the tiger, the condition is false, so rule 20 doesn\'t apply here. Therefore, that doesn\'t affect anything.\n\nNow, moving back to the question: does the tiger need itself?\n\nWe know that the tiger is big (from visiting the bald eagle), so rule