ig, but does being furry imply anything else?\n\nWait, let's retrace. Let's suppose Erin is furry. Then:\n\n- Furry \u2192 Green (statement 11)\n- Green \u2192 Big (statement 14)\nSo Erin would be big and green.\n\nNow, is there any requirement for Erin to be quiet? Not directly, unless she's round. Let's see. To be round, from statement 8 and 9, round people must be quiet and green. So if Erin were green (as she would be if furry), she could be round only if she's also quiet. But if she's round, then she must be quiet. But if she is round and quiet, then since she's big (from being green), she would be a big, quiet person. Statement 12 says big quiet people are not smart. But Erin is smart. Therefore, if Erin were furry, she would be big and green, but if she were also quiet, she can't be smart. Since she is smart, she can't be both quiet and big. But she is big (if furry), so she must not be quiet. So she can't be round because to be round she needs to be quiet and green. Since she can't be quiet, she can't be round. Therefore, being furry would not require her to be round, but she can still be big and green, but not quiet.\n\nHowever, there's another angle: statement 10 says \"All quiet, furry people are green.\" But if someone is quiet and furry, they must be green. But that's already covered by statement 11, which says all furry are green. So maybe that's redundant.\n\nBut back to Erin. If Erin were furry, then she's green and big. But there's no direct contradiction here except that if she were quiet, then she would be round (due to statement 16: quiet and smart \u2192 round). Wait, Erin is smart. So if she were quiet, then statement 16 says she must be round. But being round requires being quiet and green (statements 8 and 9). So if she were quiet and smart (she is smart), then she is round. But being round makes her quiet (already is) and green (already is). But being round and big (since green \u2192 big), so she would be big, quiet, and smart. But statement 12 says big quiet people are not smart. That's a contradiction because she's supposed to be smart. Therefore, if Erin were furry, then she would be green and big, but if she is quiet, that leads to a contradiction. But she might not be quiet. \n\nSo, if Erin is furry, then she must be big and green, but she could be not quiet. Then, being not quiet, there's no problem. Because statement 12 only applies to big and quiet people. So if she's big (from being green) but not quiet, then statement 12 doesn't apply. So she can still be smart. So being furry would not contradict her smartness unless she's also quiet. Since she's smart, she can only be quiet if she's round. But if she's not quiet, then she can be furry and smart without contradiction. Therefore, is there any other rule that would prevent Erin from being furry?\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another angle. Let's check all statements again to see if there's any restriction on being furry and smart. The statements given don't directly link smartness to fur, except through other attributes. \n\nStatement 13 says smart people are nice. Since Erin is smart, she must be nice. But the problem doesn't state that Erin is nice, but according to the rules, that must be true. However, the question is about fur, not niceness. \n\nIs there any rule that would conflict if Erin is both smart and furry? Let's see:\n\nIf Erin is furry, then she is green (statement 11). Green means big (statement 14). So big and green. \n\nAdditionally, since she is smart, and smart people are nice (statement 13), so she is nice. The problem didn't mention her being nice, but that's inferred. \n\nNow, is there any other constraint? Let's see statement 16: If someone is quiet and smart, then they are round. Since Erin is smart, if she were quiet, she would have to be round. But being round requires being quiet and green (statements 8 and 9). So again, being round would mean being big (because green is big), so big quiet people can't be smart (