ne-way, so being smart implies white, but white doesn't imply smart. So Fiona could be white without being smart.\n\nRule 7: If something is rough and not green then it is white. Gary is rough and white, and Gary is also white. Since Gary is rough and white, but he is green? Wait, no. Wait, the individual facts say Gary is rough, round, white. So Gary is rough, white, and round. But is Gary green? The individual facts don't say. Wait, Anne and Bob are green, Fiona is white, Gary's color? The individual facts state Gary is white, so he can't be green, unless the problem allows multiple colors. Wait, the problem states Fiona is white, Gary is white, but maybe they can have multiple attributes. Wait, the problem says \"Fiona is white\" and \"Gary is white\", so their color is white. So Gary is white, so he's not green. So for Gary, since he is rough and not green (because he's white, so not green?), then rule 7 applies: if rough and not green, then white. Which he is, so that's consistent.\n\nBut back to Fiona. Let's think about possible rules that can connect her to being young or not.\n\nRule 8 says rough things are young. Since Fiona is not rough (since only Gary and Bob are rough, but Bob is rough as per rule 4), so Fiona isn't rough, so that rule doesn't apply.\n\nAnother rule: If something is white then it's round (rule 2). Fiona is white, so round. But how does that connect to being young?\n\nLooking at rule 3: If something is young and not nice, then it is round. But this is a one-way implication. So if we know something is young and not nice, then it must be round. But Fiona is round. However, this doesn't necessarily mean that Fiona is young and not nice. It could be that she's round for another reason (like being white, which is rule 2). So the fact that she's round doesn't give us info about her being young unless we can link it through another rule.\n\nRule 5: If something is young and green then it is smart. Fiona isn't green, so that doesn't apply to her