Fiona is not young. Is that possible? Let's see:\n\nIf Fiona is not young, then she could still be white and round. There's no rule that says non-young things can't be white or round. The rules about young are more about when they must be round or smart, but Fiona doesn't have any conflicting attributes.\n\nAlternatively, is there a rule that requires Fiona to be young? Let's see:\n\nNo, unless being white and round requires something else. But the only rules about being white are that they are round. So that's satisfied. So there's no rule that would force Fiona to be young.\n\nTherefore, Fiona's age is not determined by the given information, so the statement \"Fiona is not young\" would be unknown, because we can't confirm it's true or false. Wait, but let me check again.\n\nWait, maybe there's a way to infer something else. Let's see if Fiona is smart. The problem states that smart things are white (rule 6), so if Fiona is smart, then she must be white, which she is. But the converse isn't necessarily true. However, Anne is smart and green. Wait, but rule 6 says smart implies white, but Anne is green. Wait, that seems conflicting. Wait, Anne is green and smart. But rule 6 says smart things are white. That would mean Anne must be white, but the problem states Anne is green. That's a contradiction. Wait, that's a problem. Did I misread?\n\nWait, let me check the problem again:\n\nThe individual facts for Anne: \"Anne is green. Anne is nice. Anne is not rough. Anne is smart.\" \n\nAnd rule 6 says \"Smart things are white.\" So if Anne is smart, she must be white. But the problem says she's green. That's a contradiction. Wait, that must be an error in the problem setup, but maybe I made a mistake.\n\nWait, maybe the problem says \"Smart things are white\" so Anne can't be both smart and green. That's a contradiction. Therefore, perhaps there's a mistake here, but maybe I misread the problem.\n\nWait, let me check again. The problem says:\n\n\"Anne is green. Anne is 