ule 2 because cold and furry), then by rule 3, he must be round. Therefore, Charlie is round. Now, since Charlie is round and furry and cold, that's all good.\n\nNow, since Charlie is round and nice? Wait, if Charlie is round and furry, then by rule 4, he must be nice. Wait, we just determined Charlie is round and furry, so rule 4 applies. Therefore, Charlie must be nice. So Charlie has to be nice as well. But the original context didn't mention Charlie being nice. But according to the rules, since he is round and furry, he must be nice. Therefore, Charlie is nice.\n\nSo now, Charlie has attributes: big, cold, furry, rough, round, nice. Wait, but the original context only listed him as big and cold. The rest are inferred through the rules.\n\nOkay, going back to Harry. Let's see if there's a way to determine Harry's roundness. Harry is nice, young, furry. Let's see if any rules apply here.\n\nRule 8 says that if something is nice and round, then it's young. Harry is young and nice, so if he were round, that's okay. But that doesn't force him to be round. \n\nAlternatively, let's check if there's a path to make Harry round. Suppose Harry is round. Then he must be cold (rule 1). If he's cold and furry, then he must be rough (rule 2). Also, since he's round and nice (he's nice), then by rule 5, he must be rough. So both paths would require him to be rough. But is there any rule that would prevent him from being round? Not that I can see. However, the problem is asking whether \"Harry is not rough\" is true, so if Harry could be round (and thus rough), then the statement would be false. But if he can't be round, then maybe he's not rough, making the statement true. Or if it's possible for him to be either, then it's unknown.\n\nWait, but can Harry be round? Let's see. The only way to be round is through the rules. Let me think.\n\nHarry is furry and nice. Suppose he is round. Then he must be cold (rule 1). But since he's cold and furry, he must be rough (rule 2). Additionally, being round and nice (he's nice), rule 5 says he must be rough. So that's consistent. But does being round introduce any contradictions?\n\nHarry's known attributes are: furry, nice, young. If he is round, then cold (so new attribute cold). But that doesn't conflict with any existing attributes. The only thing is, if he is round and rough, then according to rule 3 (if furry and rough, then round), that's okay because he would be round. So no contradiction there.\n\nAlternatively, can Harry be round? There's no rule that says he can't be. The problem states his attributes as given, but through the rules, he could have additional attributes. Therefore, it's possible that Harry is round (and thus rough) or not round (and thus not rough). Therefore, we can't be sure. Wait, but maybe there's a way to determine it.\n\nWait, another angle: Rule 8 says \"Nice, round things are young.\" Harry is young and nice. If he were round, then that rule is satisfied because he would be young. But if he's not round, then the rule doesn't apply to him. There's no rule that requires him to be round based on his current attributes. So he could be round or not.\n\nWait, but maybe there's another rule. Let's see all the rules again.\n\nLooking at rule 5: All round, nice things are rough. So if Harry were round and nice, he must be rough. But that doesn't require him to be round. It just imposes a condition if he is.\n\nIs there any other rule that could require Harry to be round? Let me think.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the fact that Harry is young. The last rule (rule 8) says nice and round things are young. But Harry is young and nice. If he were not round, then the rule doesn't require anything. But if he were round, then he must be young, which he is. So no conflict.\n\nHmm, so without any other rules, Harry's roundness is uncertain. Therefore, whether he's rough or not depends on whether he's round. Since we can't determine that from the given info, then the statement \"Harry is