      "text": ":\nOkay, let's see. I need to figure out if the statement \"The bald eagle sees the bald eagle\" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context. The options are A, B, or C.\n\nFirst, I'll try to parse all the given information step by step. Let me list out all the statements provided in the context to make it clearer.\n\n1. The bald eagle eats the tiger.\n2. The bald eagle is red.\n3. The bald eagle likes the rabbit.\n4. The bear eats the bald eagle.\n5. The bear is rough.\n6. The rabbit eats the tiger.\n7. The tiger eats the bald eagle.\n8. The tiger eats the rabbit.\n9. The tiger is kind.\n10. The tiger likes the bear.\n11. If someone is cold then they see the rabbit. (Conditional: Cold \u2192 See rabbit)\n12. If someone sees the tiger and the tiger is kind then they see the bald eagle. (Conditional: (See tiger \u2227 tiger kind) \u2192 See bald eagle)\n13. If the rabbit is red and the rabbit is rough then the rabbit likes the bear. (Conditional: (Rabbit red \u2227 rabbit rough) \u2192 Rabbit likes bear)\n14. If someone sees the rabbit then the rabbit eats the bald eagle. (Conditional: See rabbit \u2192 Rabbit eats bald eagle)\n15. If someone eats the bald eagle then they are cold. (Conditional: Eats bald eagle \u2192 Cold)\n16. If someone sees the bald eagle and they eat the bear then they like the bald eagle. (Conditional: (See bald eagle \u2227 eats bear) \u2192 Like bald eagle)\n\nThe question is about whether the bald eagle sees itself. So, we need to determine if \"The bald eagle sees the bald eagle\" is true, false, or unknown based on the given rules.\n\nLet me start by considering what we know about the bald eagle. From the statements:\n\n- The bald eagle eats the tiger (statement 1).\n- It is red (statement 2).\n- It likes the rabbit (statement 3).\n- The bear eats the bald eagle (statement 4).\n- The tiger also eats the bald eagle (statement 7).\n\nNow, looking at the conditional statements, let's see if any of them can lead us to 