"they\" refers to the someone who eats the bald eagle. Therefore, the eater (bear and tiger) would be cold if they eat the bald eagle. But that doesn't tell us anything about the bald eagle's own temperature. So unless the bald eagle is eating someone else that would make it cold, but the bald eagle eats the tiger (statement 1). But eating the tiger doesn't trigger any of the conditions related to being cold. So maybe the bald eagle isn't cold. Therefore, statement 11 wouldn't apply to the bald eagle unless it's cold, which we can't confirm yet.\n\nNext, statement 12: If someone sees the tiger and the tiger is kind, then they see the bald eagle. Since the tiger is kind (statement 9), then if the bald eagle sees the tiger, then the bald eagle would see the bald eagle? Wait, no, the conclusion is \"they see the bald eagle.\" So if the bald eagle sees the tiger (since the tiger is kind), then the bald eagle would see the bald eagle. But does the bald eagle see the tiger? Let's see.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the bald eagle does see the tiger. Wait, but how do we know? The problem is that the statements don't explicitly say that the bald eagle sees any of the others unless through the conditionals. Let's see if there's any way to derive that the bald eagle sees the tiger, which would then via statement 12 make it see the bald eagle.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the bald eagle's own actions lead to some conditions. Let's see statement 14: If someone sees the rabbit, then the rabbit eats the bald eagle. The rabbit does eat the bald eagle? Wait, statement 6 says the rabbit eats the tiger, but statement 14 is a conditional. Wait, statement 6 says \"The rabbit eats the tiger.\" So the rabbit's eating is already established. But statement 14 says if someone sees the rabbit, then the rabbit eats the bald eagle. But the rabbit already eats the tiger, but according to the conditional, if someone sees the rabbit, then the rabbit also eats the bald eagle. Wait, but that would mean that 