      "text": ":\nOkay, let me try to work through this problem step by step. So, the question is whether the statement \"The squirrel does not see the rabbit\" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context. The options are A, B, or C. \n\nFirst, I need to go through all the provided statements and see what information we have about each animal's actions and attributes. Let me list out all the given facts to make it clearer.\n\nThe context says:\n\n1. The cow chases the squirrel.\n2. The cow is young.\n3. The cow sees the squirrel.\n4. The dog is round.\n5. The dog likes the rabbit.\n6. The dog likes the squirrel.\n7. The dog sees the rabbit.\n8. The rabbit chases the cow.\n9. The rabbit does not chase the dog.\n10. The squirrel chases the rabbit.\n11. The squirrel is young.\n12. The squirrel likes the cow.\n\nThen there are several conditional statements:\n\n13. If someone chases the cow then the cow is big.\n14. If someone likes the rabbit and they do not see the squirrel then they are big.\n15. If someone is big then they like the rabbit.\n16. If someone is young then they do not see the dog.\n17. If someone is blue then they chase the cow.\n18. If someone likes the rabbit then they like the cow.\n19. If someone likes the cow then they see the cow.\n20. If someone sees the cow and they chase the squirrel then they see the rabbit.\n\nThe question is about the squirrel not seeing the rabbit. Let's see what we know about the squirrel.\n\nLooking at the direct statements first (facts 1-12):\n\nFrom fact 3: The cow sees the squirrel. So, the squirrel is being seen by the cow, but that doesn't necessarily tell us if the squirrel sees the cow or anything else. \n\nFact 10 says the squirrel chases the rabbit. So, the squirrel is chasing the rabbit. If someone chases another, do they need to see them? Maybe, but that's an assumption. The problem might require us to use only the given statements, not real-world knowledge. \n\nFact 11: The squirrel is young. So, acc