ng the rabbit. So maybe not. The dog likes the rabbit (fact5). \n\nAlternatively, let\'s see if the squirrel\'s seeing the rabbit can be derived from another condition.\n\nSuppose we consider the squirrel\'s actions. The squirrel chases the rabbit (fact10). If chasing implies seeing, then the squirrel must see the rabbit. But the problem doesn\'t state that chasing requires seeing, so we can\'t assume that. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a chain of implications. Let me think again.\n\nWait, the squirrel likes the cow (fact12). Therefore, by statement18 (If someone likes the rabbit, then they like the cow). Wait, that\'s the other way around. Statement18 says if someone likes the rabbit, then they must like the cow. But the squirrel doesn\'t like the rabbit, so that doesn\'t apply here. \n\nAlternatively, since the squirrel likes the cow, does that trigger any other conditionals? \n\nStatement19: If someone likes the cow, they see the cow. Which we\'ve already used to conclude the squirrel sees the cow. \n\nStatement18: If someone likes the rabbit, then they like the cow. So that\'s a different direction. \n\nWhat about statement17: If someone is blue then they chase the cow. We don\'t know if the squirrel is blue, so that\'s not helpful here. \n\nStatement16: squirrel is young, so doesn\'t see the dog. \n\nStatement15: If someone is big, they like the rabbit. \n\nStatement14: If someone likes the rabbit and doesn\'t see the squirrel, then they are big. \n\nWait, the squirrel is chasing the rabbit. The dog likes the rabbit and the squirrel. Let\'s see for the dog. The dog likes the rabbit (fact5) and the squirrel (fact6). \n\nDoes the dog see the squirrel? The dog sees the rabbit (fact7), but there\'s no info on seeing the squirrel. \n\nWait, if the dog likes the squirrel (fact6), then by statement18, does that require anything? Statement18 is about liking the rabbit leading to liking the cow. So since the dog likes the squirrel, that doesn\'t trigger any conditional. \n\nAlternatively, if the dog likes the squirrel, but that\'s not covered by the conditionals except maybe others. \n\nWait, let\'s check if the dog is big. Suppose someone likes the rabbit and doesn\'t see the squirrel, then they are big (statement14). The dog likes the rabbit (fact5). Does the dog see the squirrel? Not stated. If the dog doesn\'t see the squirrel, then the dog would be big. But we don\'t know if the dog sees the squirrel, so that\'s uncertain. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the squirrel\'s seeing the rabbit can be inferred from another path. Let\'s think again.\n\nThe squirrel chases the rabbit (fact10). Suppose that chasing requires seeing, so the squirrel must see the rabbit. But the problem doesn\'t state that. Unless there\'s a rule like "if someone chases X, then they see X". But the problem\'s given conditionals don\'t include that. So we can\'t assume that. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a conditional that can lead to that. Let me check statement20 again. \n\nStatement20: If someone sees the cow and chases the squirrel, then they see the rabbit. \n\nThe only one who chases the squirrel is the cow. The cow sees the squirrel (fact3), but does the cow see the cow? If the cow sees the cow (maybe the cow sees itself?), then the antecedent would be true for the cow, leading to the cow seeing the rabbit. \n\nWait, but the cow is chasing the squirrel. If the cow sees the cow (assuming that), then the antecedent is met. Then the cow would see the rabbit. But the problem doesn\'t say the cow sees the rabbit. However, if that were the case, then the cow would see the rabbit. But since we don\'t know if the cow sees itself, that\'s not certain. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the squirrel is the one who sees the cow (from fact12 and statement19), so the squirrel sees the cow. Now, if the squirrel also chases the squirrel? No, the squirrel chases the rabbit. So the squirrel can\'t satisfy the antecedent of statement20, since they don\'t chase the squirrel. \