, the consequent must be true: the cow sees the rabbit. \n\nSo, the cow sees the rabbit. \n\nNow, does that help us with the squirrel seeing the rabbit? \n\nHmm, not directly, but maybe there\'s another path. \n\nThe squirrel chases the rabbit (fact10). Let\'s see if the squirrel\'s actions can be connected via any conditional. \n\nThe squirrel is young (fact11), so by statement16, doesn\'t see the dog. \n\nThe squirrel likes the cow (fact12), so sees the cow (statement19). \n\nNow, if the squirrel sees the cow and chases the squirrel (but the squirrel doesn\'t chase itself), so that\'s not applicable. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the squirrel sees the cow and chases the rabbit. Wait, the squirrel chases the rabbit, but statement20 requires chasing the squirrel. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s another conditional. Let me check statement17: If someone is blue then they chase the cow. We don\'t know if the squirrel is blue, so that\'s not helpful. \n\nWhat about statement14: If someone likes the rabbit and doesn\'t see the squirrel, then they are big. \n\nThe squirrel likes the cow, not the rabbit. The dog likes the rabbit. So the dog is the one here. \n\nThe dog likes the rabbit (fact5). Does the dog see the squirrel? Not stated. If the dog does not see the squirrel, then the dog would be big (by statement14). But we don\'t know if the dog sees the squirrel, so that\'s unknown. \n\nAlternatively, the squirrel doesn\'t like the rabbit, so statement14 doesn\'t apply to them. \n\nWait, going back to the squirrel seeing the rabbit. Is there any way to link that through other facts?\n\nThe squirrel chases the rabbit (fact10). If chasing requires seeing, then yes, but that\'s an assumption. Alternatively, maybe through another conditional.\n\nWait, statement20\'s conclusion was that the cow sees the rabbit. So the cow sees the rabbit. The rabbit is being chased by the cow and the squirrel. \n\nBut the question is about the squirrel seeing the rabbit. \n\nAnother angle: The s