ue or false. If there\'s no information, then it\'s unknown. \n\nGiven that, let\'s see if any of the conditionals or facts lead us to that conclusion. \n\nThe only possible path is through statement20, but the conclusion there was that the cow sees the rabbit. The squirrel\'s seeing the rabbit isn\'t directly addressed. \n\nAlternatively, the squirrel\'s actions and attributes:\n\n- The squirrel is young (fact11) → doesn\'t see the dog (statement16). \n\n- The squirrel likes the cow (fact12) → sees the cow (statement19). \n\n- The squirrel chases the rabbit (fact10). \n\nNo other direct info. \n\nIs there any way that the squirrel seeing the rabbit can be inferred from other conditionals?\n\nSuppose we consider the squirrel\'s chasing the rabbit. Let\'s see if that can lead to any conditional. \n\nSuppose the squirrel likes the rabbit. But there\'s no fact stating that. The squirrel likes the cow. \n\nAlternatively, if the squirrel sees the rabbit, then does that trigger anything? \n\nWait, if the squirrel sees the rabbit, then maybe it can be part of some other condition. \n\nAlternatively, let\'s see if there\'s any contradiction if we assume the squirrel does or does not see the rabbit. \n\nSuppose the squirrel does see the rabbit. Is that possible without violating any rules?\n\nYes, because none of the conditionals forbid it. \n\nIf the squirrel does not see the rabbit, is that possible?\n\nAlso yes, unless there\'s a rule that says chasing requires seeing. \n\nSince there\'s no such rule, both possibilities are open. \n\nTherefore, the answer would be unknown (C). \n\nWait, but wait a minute! Let me check again. \n\nWait, the squirrel likes the cow, so by statement19, sees the cow. \n\nThe cow sees the rabbit (from earlier deduction via statement20). \n\nNow, if the squirrel sees the cow, and the cow sees the rabbit, does that imply anything about the squirrel seeing the rabbit?\n\nProbably not directly. Unless there\'s a transitive relation, which isn\'t sta