ditionally, point 20 says "Green people are nice," which aligns with Harry being nice (from point 19), so that\'s okay.\n\nNow, let\'s check if there\'s any contradiction. Since Harry is green (from being rough), he must be blue (point 17). But Harry\'s color is given as white (point 12). However, as per Gary\'s case, being both white and blue is possible. So Harry can be both white and blue. Therefore, no contradiction here.\n\nWait, but the problem is asking whether "Harry is not green" is true. If our deductions show that Harry must be green, then the statement "Harry is not green" would be false, so the answer would be B) False.\n\nBut let me double-check all steps to make sure I didn\'t skip anything.\n\nStarting again:\n\nHarry is white (12).\n\nWhite people are nice (19) → Harry is nice.\n\nAll nice people are big (16) → Harry is big.\n\nAll big people are rough (13) → Harry is rough.\n\nIf someone is rough, they are green (15) → Harry is green.\n\nThus, Harry is green. Therefore, the statement "Harry is not green" is false. So the answer would be B) False.\n\nWait a second, but let me check if there\'s any rule that could prevent Harry from being green. For example, are there any conditions on being green besides being rough?\n\nPoint 17 says green people are blue, so if someone is green, they must be blue. Since Harry is white and green, he must also be blue. But the problem allows multiple colors as per Gary. So that\'s okay.\n\nIs there a rule that says if someone is white and rough, something else happens? Let me check point 18 again: "Rough, white people are nice." Since Harry is both rough and white, that would make him nice, which he already is, so no conflict.\n\nAnother point: Green people are nice (20). Since Harry is green, that\'s consistent with him being nice.\n\nIs there any rule that would say that a person cannot be both white and green? The only color-related rules are that green people must be blue, but being white and blue is allowed, so 