ut she\'s green. So unless being nice allows her to be both, but maybe that\'s a contradiction. Let me think again.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps an entity can have multiple colors? The problem didn\'t state that colors are exclusive, so maybe she could be both green and blue. However, if that\'s allowed, then maybe that\'s possible. But the problem\'s statements might imply that color attributes are exclusive. For example, Fiona is rough, quiet, not nice, etc., but her color isn\'t mentioned. Wait, actually, looking back at the context, Fiona is described as rough, quiet, not nice, but her color isn\'t given. Hmm.\n\nWait, the problem lists attributes in the form "Anne is green" which is a color, "quiet" which is a trait, "round" as a shape, "nice" as another trait, etc. So perhaps each entity can have multiple attributes, including colors, but colors are separate from other traits. So an entity could be both green and blue? The problem didn\'t specify that colors are mutually exclusive, so maybe they can have multiple colors. But that complicates things.\n\nAlternatively, maybe each entity has only one color. Since Anne is stated as green, she can\'t be blue. Let me see if that\'s a possible interpretation. Since the problem didn\'t say that an entity can\'t have multiple colors, but in the given context, each entity is only listed with one color (e.g., Anne is green, Dave is round but his color isn\'t mentioned; Erin\'s color isn\'t mentioned, Fiona\'s color isn\'t mentioned except maybe through other attributes). Hmm, perhaps color is a separate attribute, so maybe they can have multiple colors, but in the given statements, each entity is only given one color. But that\'s not clear.\n\nAlternatively, maybe "is green" is the only color Anne has, so she\'s not blue. If that\'s the case, then if Anne were nice, she would have to be blue (by rule 7), but she\'s not, so that would make her not nice. Therefore, Anne cannot be nice, so the answer would be False (B).\n\nWai