      "text": ":\nOkay, let's see. I need to figure out whether the statement \"Dave is not blue\" is true, false, or unknown based on the given context. Alright, let's start by listing out all the given information and then see how they connect.\n\nFirst, the context has several statements. Let me parse each one step by step.\n\n1. Dave is big.\n2. Dave is furry.\n3. Erin is blue.\n4. Erin is cold.\n5. Erin is round.\n6. Fiona is quiet.\n7. Gary is rough.\n8. If something is rough and cold then it is furry. (Rough \u2227 Cold \u2192 Furry)\n9. Quiet, big things are not round. (Quiet \u2227 Big \u2192 \u00acRound)\n10. If Dave is blue then Dave is furry. (Dave Blue \u2192 Dave Furry)\n11. All quiet, blue things are big. (Quiet \u2227 Blue \u2192 Big)\n12. If Fiona is furry then Fiona is blue. (Fiona Furry \u2192 Fiona Blue)\n13. If something is quiet then it is cold. (Quiet \u2192 Cold)\n14. All big things are cold. (Big \u2192 Cold)\n15. All blue, round things are not quiet. (Blue \u2227 Round \u2192 \u00acQuiet)\n16. Cold things are rough. (Cold \u2192 Rough)\n\nThe question is whether \"Dave is not blue\" is true, false, or unknown. So I need to see if from the premises, we can derive that Dave is blue, or not blue, or if there's insufficient information.\n\nFirst, let's note down the properties of each entity mentioned:\n\n- Dave: Big, Furry (from 1 and 2)\n- Erin: Blue, Cold, Round (from 3,4,5)\n- Fiona: Quiet (from 6)\n- Gary: Rough (from7)\n\nNow, let's see if any of the conditionals can give us more info about Dave.\n\nStarting with Dave's properties. We know Dave is big and furry. Let's see if any rules apply here.\n\nRule 14: All big things are cold. Since Dave is big, he must be cold. So Dave is cold. (Big \u2192 Cold, so Dave cold.)\n\nRule 16: Cold things are rough. Since Dave is cold, he must be rough. So Dave is rough. (Cold \u2192 Rough, so Dave rough.)\n\nNow, looking at rule 8: If something is rough and cold, then it is furry. Dave is both rough and cold, so according to rule 8, he must be furry. Which he already is, so that's consistent. No problem here.\n\nNow, the key question is whether Dave is blue. Let's see if there's any rule that can link Dave's color.\n\nFirst, let's look at rule 10: If Dave is blue, then Dave is furry. Since Dave is already furry, this statement doesn't give us new info. It's a conditional that holds, but it doesn't tell us anything about Dave being blue. Because even if Dave were not blue, the implication would still be true (since the antecedent is false). So rule 10 doesn't help us here.\n\nAnother possible path is looking at other rules that involve blue. Let's see:\n\nRule 11: Quiet and blue things are big. Fiona is quiet, but we don't know if she's blue. Rule 12 says if Fiona is furry, then she's blue. But Fiona's furrieness isn't stated. So maybe not directly helpful for Dave.\n\nRule 15: Blue and round things are not quiet. Erin is blue and round, so according to rule 15, she can't be quiet. But Erin's quietness isn't mentioned except she is cold. Wait, Erin is cold, and rule 13 says quiet \u2192 cold, but Erin being cold doesn't mean she's quiet. So Erin's quietness is not stated except in her own properties? Wait no, Erin's properties are blue, cold, round. So she's not necessarily quiet unless stated. Wait, the original context lists Erin as blue, cold, round. So she's not stated to be quiet. So rule 15 applies to her: since she is blue and round, she can't be quiet. Which is okay, because she isn't listed as quiet. So that's consistent.\n\nNow, let's think about Dave again. Suppose we try to see if Dave could be blue. Let's assume Dave is blue, and see if that leads to any contradictions.\n\nAssume Dave is blue. Then, since he's blue and big (from his properties), let's see if that triggers any rules. Let's see:\n\nRule 11 says that quiet and blue things must be big. But Dave isn't quiet (unless... wait, is he? Let's see. Dave's properties: big, furry, cold, rough. Not mentioned quiet. So he isn't quiet. So that rule doesn't apply to him.\n\nRule 15: If something is blue and round, then it's not quiet. But Dave's roundness isn't stated. Wait, the context only says Dave is big and furry. So Dave's roundness is unknown. Hmm, so if he were blue and round, then he can't be quiet, but since he's not quiet anyway, that's okay.\n\nBut maybe another angle: Rule 14 says all big things are cold, so Dave is cold. Since he's cold, rule 16 says he's rough. Which we already have. Also, rule 8 applies to him, which is okay.\n\nNow, if Dave is blue, then let's see if any rules would conflict. Let's see if there's a rule that says something about blue and big things.\n\nWait, rule 11: Quiet and blue \u2192 big. But Dave is not quiet, so that's okay.\n\nAnother thought: Rule 12 says if Fiona is furry, then Fiona is blue. But Fiona's furrieness isn't known. Not sure how that helps.\n\nAlternatively, let's see if there's a chain of reasoning. Since Dave is big and cold (from 14), and cold implies rough (from 16). So Dave is rough and cold, so by rule 8, he must be furry, which he is. That's all consistent.\n\nWait, but what about the contrapositive of some rules? Let's see.\n\nSuppose we want to see if Dave can be blue. Let's see if there's any rule that would make him not blue. Suppose Dave is blue. Let's see:\n\nIs there any rule that would require him to be something that contradicts his known attributes?\n\nAlternatively, maybe if he were blue, then some other condition would be triggered. Let's see.\n\nLooking at the rules again:\n\nRule 10: If Dave is blue, then he must be furry. Which he is, so that's okay.\n\nRule 15: Blue and round \u2192 not quiet. But Dave's roundness is unknown. So unless we can find that Dave is round, then even if he were blue, that rule wouldn't apply. Since he's not stated to be round, that's okay.\n\nWait, but is there any rule that would require Dave to be round or not?\n\nLooking at the context, Dave's properties are only big, furry, cold, rough. Roundness isn't mentioned. So Dave could be round or not, unless inferred.\n\nWait, rule 9: Quiet and big \u2192 not round. Dave is not quiet, so that doesn't apply. So Dave's roundness is unknown.\n\nHmm. So assuming Dave is blue doesn't lead to any contradictions based on the given rules. Therefore, is there any rule that would prevent Dave from being blue?\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can find that Dave must not be blue. Let's see.\n\nWait, rule 14 says all big things are cold. Dave is big and cold. Rule 16 says cold things are rough. So Dave is rough. Now, if something is rough and cold (which Dave is), then by rule 8, it must be furry, which he is. So that's okay.\n\nNow, let's consider another angle. Let's see if there are any other rules that involve blue and other attributes.\n\nRule 3 says Erin is blue. So Erin is blue, and she's also cold and round. From rule 15, blue and round things are not quiet. Since Erin is blue and round, she can't be quiet, which is consistent because she isn't listed as quiet. So that's okay.\n\nAnother thing: Rule 13 says that quiet things are cold. Fiona is quiet (from 6), so Fiona must be cold. Fiona's coldness isn't stated, but according to rule 13, she must be cold. So Fiona is cold.\n\nAlso, Fiona is quiet. Rule 11 says quiet and blue things must be big. So if Fiona is blue, then she must be big. But Fiona's bigness isn't stated. So unless Fiona is blue, her bigness is unknown. But Fiona's furrieness is also a factor here. Rule 12: If Fiona is furry, then she is blue. So if Fiona were furry, she would have to be blue, but if she's not furry, that doesn't say anything.\n\nBut Fiona's properties are only that she's quiet. So she must be cold (from rule 13). So Fiona is quiet, cold, and maybe other attributes.\n\nBack to Dave. Let's see if there's any other constraints. Suppose Dave is blue. Then, since he's big (given), blue, and big. Let's see if there's a rule that links blue and big with somet