 Cold)\n15. All blue, round things are not quiet. (Blue ∧ Round → ¬Quiet)\n16. Cold things are rough. (Cold → Rough)\n\nThe question is whether "Dave is not blue" is true, false, or unknown. So I need to see if from the premises, we can derive that Dave is blue, or not blue, or if there\'s insufficient information.\n\nFirst, let\'s note down the properties of each entity mentioned:\n\n- Dave: Big, Furry (from 1 and 2)\n- Erin: Blue, Cold, Round (from 3,4,5)\n- Fiona: Quiet (from 6)\n- Gary: Rough (from7)\n\nNow, let\'s see if any of the conditionals can give us more info about Dave.\n\nStarting with Dave\'s properties. We know Dave is big and furry. Let\'s see if any rules apply here.\n\nRule 14: All big things are cold. Since Dave is big, he must be cold. So Dave is cold. (Big → Cold, so Dave cold.)\n\nRule 16: Cold things are rough. Since Dave is cold, he must be rough. So Dave is rough. (Cold → Rough, so Dave rough.)\n\nNow, looking at rule 8: If something is rough and cold, then it is furry. Dave is both rough and cold, so according to rule 8, he must be furry. Which he already is, so that\'s consistent. No problem here.\n\nNow, the key question is whether Dave is blue. Let\'s see if there\'s any rule that can link Dave\'s color.\n\nFirst, let\'s look at rule 10: If Dave is blue, then Dave is furry. Since Dave is already furry, this statement doesn\'t give us new info. It\'s a conditional that holds, but it doesn\'t tell us anything about Dave being blue. Because even if Dave were not blue, the implication would still be true (since the antecedent is false). So rule 10 doesn\'t help us here.\n\nAnother possible path is looking at other rules that involve blue. Let\'s see:\n\nRule 11: Quiet and blue things are big. Fiona is quiet, but we don\'t know if she\'s blue. Rule 12 says if Fiona is furry, then she\'s blue. But Fiona\'s furrieness isn\'t stated. So maybe not directly helpful for Dave.\n\nRule 15: Blue and round things are not quiet. Erin is blue and round,