s about seeing are in points 6 and 7 (cat sees bear and dog), and point 20 which is a conditional.\n\nLooking at the bear\'s attributes and relationships:\n\n- The bear is green (point 1).\n- The bear likes the cat and dog (points 2,3).\n- The bear visits the dog (point 4).\n- There\'s no direct statement that the bear sees the mouse. The question is asking if that\'s true or not.\n\nNow, let\'s check if any of the conditional statements can lead us to conclude that the bear sees the mouse.\n\nFirst, let\'s look at the conditional statements that might relate to seeing:\n\n- Point 20: "If something is green then it sees the dog." Since the bear is green, this would mean the bear sees the dog. That\'s confirmed in point 4 where the bear visits the dog, but the seeing is from the bear\'s perspective. Wait, but the conditional here says that if something is green, it sees the dog. So the bear being green would mean it sees the dog. However, does that mean the bear sees the dog, but what about the mouse?\n\nWait, the question is about the bear seeing the mouse. So maybe there\'s another conditional that could imply that?\n\nLet me check all the conditionals again:\n\n17. If something visits the mouse and the mouse visits the dog then it is cold.\n    - The mouse does visit the dog? Wait, the dog visits the mouse (point 13), but the mouse visiting the dog isn\'t stated. Wait, the mouse\'s actions: the cat visits the mouse (point9), the dog visits the mouse (point13). The mouse\'s own actions aren\'t stated except being visited. So the first part of this conditional is about "something" that visits the mouse, and the mouse also visits the dog. Since the mouse doesn\'t visit the dog (as per the given info), maybe this condition isn\'t triggered here. Not sure yet, but maybe not relevant for the bear\'s seeing.\n\n18. If something likes the cat then it visits the dog.\n    - The bear likes the cat (point2), so according to this, the bear must visit the dog. Which it does (point4). So that\'s consistent.\n\n19. If something is cold then it likes the cat.\n    - The mouse is cold (point15), so the mouse must like the cat. But is that stated? The mouse\'s likes aren\'t mentioned except in point12 (dog likes mouse). So this would mean the mouse likes the cat, but the original info doesn\'t say that. Wait, but according to the conditional, since the mouse is cold, it must like the cat. So that\'s an inference. So the mouse likes the cat, even though it\'s not explicitly stated.\n\n20. If something is green then it sees the dog. → As before, bear sees dog.\n\n21. If something likes the mouse then it sees the cat.\n    - The dog likes the mouse (point12), so the dog must see the cat. Is that stated? The dog\'s seeing isn\'t mentioned except in the conditionals. So the dog must see the cat because of this. But the original info doesn\'t say the dog sees the cat. So that\'s an inference.\n\n22. If something is green and cold then it likes the cat.\n    - The bear is green but not cold (the mouse is cold, not the bear). So this doesn\'t apply to the bear. The mouse is cold but not green (unless the mouse is green? Wait, no, the bear is green. The mouse\'s color isn\'t stated, so we don\'t know. Wait, the first statement says the bear is green. The mouse\'s color isn\'t mentioned, so unless inferred otherwise, it\'s unknown. So the mouse is cold, but unless it\'s also green, which we can\'t assume, so this condition doesn\'t apply to the mouse. Maybe to another entity?\n\n23. If something is big and it visits the bear then the bear is green.\n    - The mouse is big (point14). Does the mouse visit the bear? The cat visits the mouse (point9), and the cat visits the bear (point8), but the mouse\'s visits aren\'t stated except being visited by the dog and cat. The mouse doesn\'t visit the bear, so this condition isn\'t triggered. The bear is already green, so that\'s consistent.\n\nNow, back to the question: does the bear see the mouse?\n\nLooking a