point19 (since it\'s cold), the mouse must like the cat. So mouse likes cat.\n\nAdditionally, the dog, since it likes the mouse (point12), must see the cat (point21). So the dog sees the cat.\n\nNow, the question is about the bear seeing the mouse. The only seeing related to the bear is that the bear, being green, sees the dog (point20). So the bear sees the dog. But nothing about the mouse.\n\nIs there a way that the bear could see the mouse through another path?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps if the bear visited the mouse, then there might be a seeing relation. But the bear doesn\'t visit the mouse. The bear only visits the dog (point4).\n\nAlternatively, does the mouse visit the bear? The mouse is visited by the cat and dog, but the mouse\'s own visits aren\'t stated except in point9 (cat visits mouse). So the mouse doesn\'t visit the bear, so point23 isn\'t triggered.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the fact that the cat sees the bear (point6) and the cat visits the mouse (point9) implies that the bear can see the mouse through the cat\'s actions? But that\'s not a logical connection.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps if the mouse is cold (point15), then from point19, the mouse likes the cat. But that doesn\'t help with the bear seeing the mouse.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the bear\'s greenness and coldness? Wait, the bear is green but not cold. The mouse is cold. So point22 requires both green and cold, which the bear isn\'t, so that\'s irrelevant.\n\nHmm. So after going through all the given info and conditionals, I can\'t find any direct or indirect statements that would indicate whether the bear sees the mouse. The only seeing related to the bear is that it sees the dog because it\'s green. The mouse\'s presence isn\'t linked to the bear\'s seeing through any of the given conditionals.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be unknown (option C). But wait, let me check again for any possible oversight.\n\nWait, the question is "The bear sees the mouse". To be true, there must be a fact or