ntext\'s rules:\n\n1. If something is blue and furry then it is quiet. (OK)\n\n2. If Dave is furry then Dave is nice. (Yes)\n\n3. If something is furry then it is blue. (Ah, this is a general rule: all furry things are blue)\n\n4. Quiet things are red. (Yes)\n\n5. All furry, green things are quiet. (Yes)\n\n6. If something is red then it is green. (Yes)\n\n7. All nice things are furry. (Yes)\n\n8. All smart things are nice. (Yes)\n\n9. Red things are quiet. (Yes)\n\nSo, according to rule 3, all furry things are blue. Dave is furry, so he must be blue. However, in the facts, Dave\'s attributes are listed as green, quiet, red. There\'s no mention of blue. So this is a problem. Either the facts are incomplete, or the rules are not being followed. Since in logical reasoning problems, we usually assume that all the given premises are true and consistent, perhaps there\'s an error here?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the attributes listed are all the attributes each entity has, but the rules might add more. But if Dave must be blue according to rule 3, but isn\'t listed as blue, then that\'s a contradiction. But maybe the problem expects us to ignore that and proceed with the other rules?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made a mistake in interpreting the rules. Let me re-express the rules symbolically to see.\n\nLet me try to approach this step by step, starting with Dave\'s attributes and applying the rules.\n\nFirst, Dave\'s explicit attributes:\n\n- Furry (F)\n- Green (G)\n- Quiet (Q)\n- Red (R)\n\nFrom the rules:\n\nRule 3: Furry → Blue (B). So since Dave is Furry, he must also be Blue. But the facts don\'t say he\'s Blue. This is a problem. Unless the problem allows for some attributes to be added through the rules. Maybe the problem expects that we have to consider that Dave is Blue because of rule 3, even though it\'s not listed?\n\nWait, but in the problem statement, the facts are presented as "Dave is furry. Dave is green..." etc., so maybe those are all the attributes that