g else? Not directly related to liking the rabbit. \n\nWhat about statement16: If someone sees the cow and is not big, then they don\'t like the lion. \n\nThe cow sees the dog (statement2), but that\'s not seeing the cow. The lion seeing the rabbit (from earlier) doesn\'t involve the cow. \n\nAnother angle: Since the lion is green (from earlier), and if someone is green and likes the cow, then the cow needs the rabbit (statement18). \n\nDoes the lion like the cow? We don\'t know. The cow likes the lion (statement1), but that\'s a different direction. If the lion liked the cow, then according to statement13 (if someone likes the cow, they are big), the lion would have to be big. But the dog is big (statement3). The lion\'s size isn\'t mentioned. \n\nWait, the lion is rough (statement7), but that\'s a different attribute. \n\nSo, if the lion were to like the cow, then the lion would have to be big (from statement13). But we don\'t know if the lion likes the cow. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the lion\'s status as green (established earlier) could lead to other things, but not directly related to liking the rabbit. \n\nSo, putting it all together:\n\nWe know the rabbit likes the lion because the lion is green (so sees the rabbit, triggering statement19). But the question is about the lion\'s feelings towards the rabbit. \n\nIs there any rule that would require the lion to like the rabbit? \n\nLooking at all the statements again, I don\'t see any. The only rules that mention liking the lion or rabbit are the ones we already considered. \n\nTherefore, there\'s no direct or indirect information that the lion likes the rabbit. Also, there\'s no rule that would prevent the lion from liking the rabbit. So the statement "The lion does not like the rabbit" could be either true or false, but we don\'t have enough information to determine it. \n\nWait, but hold on. Let me check again if there\'s any possibility that the lion must like the rabbit. \n\nSuppose someone (like the rabbit)