r\'s self-liking?\n\nLooking at conditional 14 again: If something chases the dog, then it likes the tiger. \n\nIf the tiger were to chase the dog, then it would have to like the tiger (itself). But the problem doesn\'t say that the tiger chases the dog. So unless there\'s another condition that requires the tiger to chase the dog, we can\'t assume that. \n\nAlternatively, suppose someone else chases the dog, but that\'s already covered by the cow and the bear. The cow chases the dog (from statement 16\'s implication), and the bear now also chases the dog (from conditional 18). So the tiger isn\'t involved in chasing the dog unless there\'s another condition. \n\nAnother conditional: 18 says if something chases the bear and the bear is young, then the bear chases the dog. We already used that. \n\nIs there a condition where the tiger\'s self-liking is required? \n\nAlternatively, maybe if the tiger likes the dog, then it would have to be round (from conditional 11). But that\'s not about self-liking. \n\nAlternatively, if the tiger sees the cow (which it does, statement 10), then by conditional 12, it is round. So the tiger is round. But that\'s a physical attribute, not related to liking. \n\nSo putting it all together, there\'s no information given or inferred that the tiger likes itself, nor any rule that would make that necessary or impossible. \n\nTherefore, the answer should be C) Unknown. \n\nWait, but let me check again for any oversight. Let me list all the facts we have:\n\n- The bear likes the cow (given).\n- Bear doesn\'t see the cow (given).\n- Cow chases bear (given).\n- Cow is cold (given).\n- Cow sees dog (given).\n- Dog likes bear (given).\n- Dog doesn\'t like tiger (given).\n- Tiger not big (given).\n- Tiger doesn\'t see bear (given).\n- Tiger sees cow (given).\n- From 16: Since bear likes cow, cow chases dog. So cow chases dog.\n- From 14: Since cow chases dog, cow likes tiger.\n- From 15: Cow chases dog and is cold → cow is young.\n- From 13: Cow