ead to contradictions. But in logical reasoning, contradictions can lead to any conclusion (principle of explosion). However, in typical logic problems like this, perhaps we are supposed to work with the given facts and see if the conclusion can be derived without contradiction, but maybe the problem expects us to ignore contradictions or treat them as unknown?\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake in applying rule 20. Let me check again. Rule 20 says: If something is green and it sees the dog, then it is blue. The squirrel is green (fact 8) and sees the dog (fact 10). Therefore, the conclusion is that the squirrel must be blue. But the fact says it\'s green, so that\'s a contradiction. \n\nHmm, so this is a problem. The premises are inconsistent. Therefore, any statement could be inferred (or not), but in such cases, perhaps the answer is unknown because of the contradiction? Or maybe the problem expects us to proceed despite the contradiction, focusing only on the question at hand?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem\'s author intended that rule 20 is an implication, so even if the squirrel is green and sees the dog, it must be blue, but since it\'s stated to be green, maybe that overrides the implication? Or maybe the rules take precedence over the facts?\n\nWait, in logic, the premises are all the given facts and the conditional statements. So all the given statements (facts and rules) are considered true. But if they are contradictory, then the system is inconsistent, and therefore any statement can be proven. However, in such cases, perhaps the question is still answerable based on the given info, ignoring the contradiction?\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made an error in applying the rules. Let me re-express the rules in formal terms to see.\n\nLet me denote the entities: D (dog), M (mouse), R (rabbit), S (squirrel).\n\nGiven facts:\n\n1. D is blue.\n2. D sees M.\n3. M is not round.\n4. M sees D.\n5. R is cold.\n6. R is red.\n7. R sees M.\n8. S is green.\n9. 