oung. Now, let\'s see if we can link this to being big.\n\nLooking at statement 11: Young things are nice. Since Gary is young, he must be nice (from 11). So Gary is nice and young. \n\nNow, statement 13 says all nice things are quiet. Since Gary is nice, he must also be quiet. So Gary is quiet. \n\nNow, statement 14 states that all quiet things are big. Wait, so if something is quiet, then it\'s big. Since Gary is quiet, does that mean he\'s big? That would make the statement "Gary is big" true, so the answer would be A) True. But let me check all the steps again to make sure I didn\'t miss anything.\n\nHold on, there\'s a possible contradiction here. Let me retrace:\n\n- Gary is young (7).\n- Young → nice (11), so Gary is nice.\n- Nice → quiet (13), so Gary is quiet.\n- Quiet → big (14), so Gary is big. \n\nThat seems straightforward, so according to these steps, Gary must be big. But let me check if there\'s any rule that might conflict with that conclusion.\n\nWait, let\'s look at statement 15: Young, quiet things are red. So if Gary is young and quiet, then he must be red. But does that affect anything? Well, the question is about being big, so maybe not directly. But let\'s see if there\'s any other constraints.\n\nAlso, Erin is big and red (statements 5 and 6). And big, red things are young (statement 12). Wait, so Erin is big and red, so according to statement 12, big and red → young. But Erin\'s age isn\'t mentioned. Wait, the context says "Erin is big. Erin is red." So according to statement 12, big and red → young. So Erin must be young? But the context doesn\'t state Erin\'s age. Hmm, that\'s an inconsistency unless the context allows that. Wait, maybe I made a mistake here. Let me check again.\n\nStatement 12: "Big, red things are young." So if something is both big and red, it must be young. Since Erin is big and red (statements 5 and 6), then Erin must be young. But the context doesn\'t mention Erin\'s age. That\'s okay because the given info only tells us what\'s stated. So Erin is young, but that\'s not mentioned in the context. Wait, but the context only lists Erin as big and red, so perhaps that\'s an implication. But that\'s okay, the problem is about Gary.\n\nBack to Gary. Following the chain: Young → nice → quiet → big. So according to the rules, Gary must be big. Therefore, the answer should be A) True. But let me check if there\'s any other constraints that might say something else.\n\nWait, the problem mentions "All quiet things are big" (statement 14). So if Gary is quiet, he must be big. Therefore, unless there\'s a way for Gary to not be quiet, but according to the previous steps, he must be quiet. Let me see:\n\nGary is young → nice (statement 11). Then, all nice things are quiet (statement 13), so yes, he\'s quiet. So unless there\'s a way he can\'t be quiet, but there\'s no contradiction here. So he must be big. \n\nWait another thought: The problem states "Young, quiet things are red" (statement 15). Since Gary is young and quiet, he must be red. But there\'s no mention of Gary\'s color in the context. But the question is about being big, so even if he has to be red, that doesn\'t conflict with being big. So that\'s okay. So the conclusion holds.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a loop here. Let me check all the rules again to ensure no contradictions in the logic.\n\nStarting with Gary\'s attributes:\n\n1. Gary is young (given).\n2. Young → nice (statement 11). So nice.\n3. Nice → quiet (statement 13). Quiet.\n4. Quiet → big (statement 14). Therefore big.\n5. Young and quiet → red (statement 15). So Gary must be red. But the original context doesn\'t say Gary\'s color, but that\'s okay because the question is about being big, not red. \n\nTherefore, based on the given rules, Gary must be big. So the answer should be True (A).\n\nWait a second. Let me check if there\'s any other rule that could affect this. For instance, statement 9: All nice, big things are white. If Gary is both nice and big (