 furry and smart, then she must be big. But since Fiona is young, from statement 14, she\'s not red. But how does that help? Let\'s see.\n\nIf Fiona were smart, then according to statement 15, she would have to be big. But is there any contradiction in that? Let\'s see. If Fiona is big, then statement 8 says that big and smart things are kind. So if Fiona were smart and big, then she would have to be kind. But then, if she is kind, from statement 13, she must be young. Well, she is already young. So that\'s okay. But does she have to be kind?\n\nWait, if Fiona were smart and big, then she must be kind (from 8). But being kind would require her to be young (from 13), which she is. So that\'s okay. However, is there any other constraints?\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can look at other rules. Let me check if there are any other implications.\n\nStatement 9: If something is red and not smart, then it\'s kind. Fiona isn\'t red, so that doesn\'t apply.\n\nStatement 12 is about Charlie, so maybe not relevant here.\n\nLet me think of possible scenarios. Let\'s suppose Fiona is smart. Then:\n\n- Fiona is smart → she must be furry (statement 10), which she is.\n\n- She is furry and smart → she must be big (statement 15). So Fiona would be big.\n\n- Since she is big and smart, then by statement 8, she must be kind.\n\n- If she\'s kind, then by statement 13, she must be young, which she is. So that\'s consistent.\n\nSo if Fiona were smart, she would have to be big and kind. But the problem doesn\'t state whether Fiona is big or kind. The original context only says Fiona is furry and young. So can she be big? The context doesn\'t say she\'s not big. So maybe she could be big. But the question is, do we have enough information to confirm or deny her being smart?\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a contradiction if she is smart, which would mean she must be big, but if she\'s big and smart, then she must be kind. But if she is kind, then she must be young (which she is), but there\'s