t be quiet (rule 17). Since Gary is smart and round, that requires him to be quiet. So that actually supports the fact that he is quiet. \n\nTherefore, there's no contradiction here. The given fact that Gary is quiet is consistent with all the rules. Therefore, the statement \"Gary is not quiet\" is false, so the correct answer should be B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe problem requires determining if the statement \"Gary is not quiet\" is true, false, or unknown based on the given premises. Let's analyze the information systematically:\n\n### **Known Facts About Gary:**\n1. **Gary is green** (Fact 9).\n2. **Gary is quiet** (Fact 10).\n\n### **Inferences from Premises:**\n- **Quiet \u2192 Smart** (Fact 14): Since Gary is quiet, he must be **smart**.\n- **Smart \u2192 Big** (Fact 15): Gary being smart means he is **big**.\n- **Big and Green \u2192 Furry** (Fact 16): Gary is big (from above) and green (given), so he must be **furry**.\n- **Furry \u2192 White** (Fact 11): Gary\u2019s furrieness implies he is **white**.\n- **White and Big \u2192 Round** (Fact 13): Gary is white and big, so he is **round**.\n- **Smart and Round \u2192 Quiet** (Fact 17): Gary\u2019s smartness and roundness confirm he must be **quiet**, which aligns with Fact 10.\n\n### **Contradictions/Consistency Check:**\n- All inferences (smart, big, furry, white, round) are consistent with the original facts and rules.\n- The premise explicitly states Gary is quiet (Fact 10), and no rule contradicts this.\n\n### **Conclusion:**\nThe statement \"Gary is not quiet\" directly contradicts Fact 10, which asserts Gary is quiet. All inferences validate his quietness. Thus, the statement is **false**.\n\n**Answer:** B) False",
