ite, big people are round.\n14. If someone is quiet then they are smart.\n15. Smart people are big.\n16. All big, green people are furry.\n17. If someone is smart and round then they are quiet.\n18. Green people are smart.\n19. All smart, furry people are big.\n\nNow, the question is about Gary. Let\'s focus on what we know about Gary directly first. From the context:\n\n- Gary is green (fact 9).\n- Gary is quiet (fact 10).\n\nThe question is whether "Gary is not quiet" is true, which would mean the statement is asserting that Gary is not quiet. But according to fact 10, Gary is quiet. So at first glance, the statement "Gary is not quiet" should be false, which would make the answer B) False. However, maybe there\'s a contradiction or some inference that could affect this conclusion?\n\nWait, but let me check if there are any rules that might conflict with Gary being quiet. Let\'s go through the rules step by step.\n\nStarting with Gary\'s attributes:\n\nGary is green (fact 9) and quiet (fact 10). \n\nLooking at rule 14: If someone is quiet then they are smart. Since Gary is quiet, that means he must be smart. So Gary is smart.\n\nRule 15 says smart people are big. Therefore, since Gary is smart, he must be big. So now we know Gary is big, green, quiet, smart.\n\nWait, but let\'s check other rules. Since he is green (fact 9) and big (from being smart), rule 16 says all big, green people are furry. Therefore, Gary must also be furry. \n\nSo now Gary\'s attributes are: green, quiet, smart, big, furry. \n\nNow, rule 11 says all furry people are white. So since Gary is furry, he must be white. \n\nSo now adding white to his attributes: green, quiet, smart, big, furry, white.\n\nNow check rule 13: All white, big people are round. Since Gary is both white and big, he must be round. \n\nSo adding round to his attributes: green, quiet, smart, big, furry, white, round.\n\nNow, rule 17: If someone is smart and round then they are quiet. Well, Gary is smart, round, and already