t would indicate whether the rabbit eats the squirrel?\n\nLooking through the premises again. Let\'s see:\n\nThe only direct mention of "eats" involving the rabbit is that the squirrel eats the rabbit (statement 6). There\'s nothing in the premises that says the rabbit eats the squirrel. So unless there\'s a conditional statement that could lead to that, the default would be that it\'s unknown? But maybe there\'s a chain of reasoning here.\n\nLet me check the other conditional statements. For example, premise 9: If the squirrel is green and likes the rabbit, then the rabbit eats the mouse. But since the squirrel is red (statement 7), it can\'t be green, so this condition is false, so the conclusion (rabbit eats mouse) is irrelevant here. So that doesn\'t affect the rabbit\'s relation to the squirrel.\n\nPremise 10: If someone sees the cat and likes the squirrel, then the squirrel sees the cat. Not sure if that\'s relevant here.\n\nPremise 11: If someone is young, then they see the mouse. Since the squirrel is red (statement7), and all red people are young (statement8), so the squirrel is young. Therefore, by premise11, the squirrel sees the mouse. Wait, but that\'s a conclusion. Let me note that: squirrel is red → young → must see the mouse. So squirrel sees the mouse.\n\nPremise12: If someone eats the rabbit and they see the mouse, then the mouse is red. The squirrel eats the rabbit (statement6), and we just concluded that the squirrel sees the mouse (from premises7,8,11). So squirrel eats the rabbit and sees the mouse. Therefore, by premise12, the mouse must be red. Wait, but the problem didn\'t state the mouse\'s color. So according to this, the mouse is red. So that\'s a conclusion we can make.\n\nPremise13: If someone is cold, they eat the squirrel. Not sure if anyone is cold, so maybe not relevant.\n\nPremise14: If someone sees the rabbit and the rabbit is green, then they are young. But the rabbit\'s color isn\'t mentioned. Unless the squirrel\'s color is red