 Hence, the rabbit does not visit the mouse. \n\nSo, the rabbit\'s visiting the BE is okay because they don\'t visit the mouse. That\'s consistent. \n\nNow, let\'s check other implications. Let\'s see if the rabbit\'s visit to the BE causes any other conditions.\n\nFrom premise 10, we already have that BE likes rabbit because rabbit needs BE.\n\nFrom premise 11: If someone needs BE, then BE likes mouse. Since rabbit needs BE (6), then BE must like the mouse. So BE likes the mouse as well.\n\nSo BE likes both rabbit and mouse.\n\nGoing back to premise 16, which we already considered. \n\nNow, let\'s see if there are any other conditions triggered by the rabbit\'s actions.\n\nLooking at premise 8: If someone visits the mouse, they like the cow. Since the rabbit does not visit the mouse (as we concluded earlier), this doesn\'t apply to the rabbit.\n\nWhat about the cow? The cow is round (premise 3). Premise 15 says if someone is round and needs BE, then BE is round. The cow is round, and does the cow need BE? The first premises say BE needs the cow and the mouse (premises 1 and 2). But does the cow need BE? Not stated unless inferred. Since the problem states "The bald eagle needs the cow," which is a separate statement. So the cow might not need BE. Therefore, premise 15\'s antecedent (someone round needing BE) might not apply to the cow. Unless another entity is round. The rabbit is green (5), so not round. The mouse? Not mentioned. So maybe the cow is the only round one. Thus, unless the cow needs BE, which isn\'t stated, premise 15\'s condition isn\'t triggered.\n\nMoving on to other conditions. Let\'s check premise 12: If someone likes the cow, they are blue. \n\nDoes anyone like the cow? Let\'s see. The BE needs the cow (premise 1), but needing doesn\'t necessarily mean liking. Wait, but premise 10 and 11: When someone needs BE, then BE likes rabbit and mouse. But does that imply anything about BE\'s feelings towards the cow? Not directly. \n\nAlternatively, does