      "text": ":\nOkay, let's see. I need to figure out if Gary is not white, so the statement is \"Gary is not white.\" The options are True, False, or Unknown. Let me go through the given information step by step.\n\nFirst, let me list out all the given premises to keep track:\n\n1. Bob is rough.\n2. Charlie is nice.\n3. Charlie is rough.\n4. Charlie is white.\n5. Dave is not white.\n6. Gary is cold.\n7. Gary is quiet.\n8. Rough, white things are quiet. (So if something is both rough and white, then it's quiet.)\n9. If Charlie is round and Charlie is quiet, then Charlie is rough. (But we already know Charlie is rough from point 3, so maybe that's redundant?)\n10. All young things are round. (So young \u2192 round)\n11. Nice things are white. (So if something is nice, then it's white. Since Charlie is nice, that aligns with point 4 where Charlie is white.)\n12. Rough, nice things are cold. (If something is both rough and nice, then it's cold.)\n13. If something is round and rough then it is nice. (round \u2227 rough \u2192 nice)\n14. If something is rough then it is young. (rough \u2192 young)\n\nThe question is about Gary's whiteness. Let's look at what we know about Gary: points 6 and 7 say Gary is cold and quiet. Also, any other info?\n\nLooking at premise 12: Rough, nice things are cold. But Gary is cold (point6). Wait, but does Gary have to be rough and nice to be cold? Because premise 12 says that if something is rough and nice, then it is cold. But it doesn't say that only those things are cold. So maybe Gary could be cold for another reason. Not sure yet.\n\nWe need to see if Gary is white or not. The options are whether \"Gary is not white\" is true, false, or unknown.\n\nFirst, let's see if any premises directly mention Gary's color. The premises about Gary are that he is cold and quiet. The other premises that might relate to color: \n\nPremise 8 says rough and white things are quiet. So if something is both rough and white, then it must be quiet. Gary is quiet, but that doesn't necessarily mean he is rough and white, because quietness could come from other reasons. However, if Gary is rough and white, then he must be quiet, but since he is already quiet, that's okay. But we don't know if he's rough or not.\n\nWait, but Gary's properties: we know he's cold and quiet. Let's see other possible connections.\n\nPremise 12 says that if something is rough and nice, then it is cold. So if Gary were both rough and nice, then he would be cold. But Gary is cold, but does that mean he must be rough and nice? Not necessarily, because premise 12 is a one-way implication. The coldness could be from another source. So maybe Gary is cold for another reason, so we can't assume he's rough and nice unless more info is given.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another way to connect.\n\nLooking at the other premises, maybe we can find something about Gary's roughness or niceness. Let's see.\n\nGary's attributes so far: cold, quiet. Need to see if he's rough or nice.\n\nWait, premise 8: rough and white things are quiet. Gary is quiet, so maybe he's a rough and white thing. But then he would have to be rough and white, but we don't know if he's white. Alternatively, maybe he's not rough but still quiet for another reason. Since the premise only states that rough and white things are quiet, but doesn't say that all quiet things are rough and white. So quietness is a necessary condition for being rough and white, but not a sufficient one. So quietness alone doesn't imply rough and white.\n\nHmm. Let's think of other rules. Let's see if there's any way to connect Gary's coldness to his color. Premise 12: rough and nice \u2192 cold. If Gary were cold and also nice, then he would have to be rough (since if something is nice and rough, then cold). But Gary is cold, so if he's nice, then he must be rough. But we don't know if Gary is nice or not.\n\nAlternatively, maybe Gary's coldness comes from another rule? The problem doesn't state any other rules about coldness except premise 12 and maybe others?\n\nLooking at the premises again:\n\nPremise 6: Gary is cold.\n\nPremise 12: rough and nice \u2192 cold.\n\nIs there any other rule about coldness? I think that's the only one. So coldness is only mentioned in those two places.\n\nSo, if Gary is cold, perhaps he is either (a) rough and nice, or (b) cold for some other reason not covered by the premises. Since the premises don't state that only rough and nice things are cold, then (b) is possible. Therefore, we can't conclude that Gary must be rough and nice, so we can't know for sure.\n\nBut let's see if there are other premises that might affect Gary's attributes.\n\nLooking at the other premises, maybe the ones about being young, round, etc.\n\nPremise 10: All young things are round.\n\nPremise 13: If something is round and rough, then it is nice. (round \u2227 rough \u2192 nice)\n\nPremise 14: If something is rough, then it is young. (rough \u2192 young)\n\nSo, if something is rough, it must be young, and then being young makes it round. So a rough thing must be round (because rough \u2192 young \u2192 round). Then, if it's round and rough, premise 13 says it must be nice. So, putting that together:\n\nIf something is rough \u2192 young \u2192 round. Then, since it's rough and round (because round from young), then by premise 13, it must be nice. Therefore, any rough thing must also be nice.\n\nWait a second. Let me check that chain again:\n\nSuppose X is rough.\n\nThen by premise14, X is young.\n\nBy premise10, X is round (since young \u2192 round).\n\nThus, X is rough and round. Then by premise13, X must be nice.\n\nTherefore, any rough thing must be nice.\n\nTherefore, all rough things are nice.\n\nSo, that's a conclusion we can draw from the premises. So if something is rough, then it's nice. So combining that with premise12: rough and nice \u2192 cold. But since all rough things are nice, then any rough thing is also cold? Wait, because if something is