 if any of the conditional statements could affect this. The squirrel is stated to be not cold (so that\'s a direct negation of being cold). However, maybe there\'s a hidden contradiction? Let me see.\n\nLooking at the other statements, the squirrel is also said to be nice (statement 11). Then, statement 20 says if something is nice, then it is blue. So since the squirrel is nice, it must be blue. That\'s okay. But does that affect its temperature? Not directly. The coldness is addressed in statement 10.\n\nWait, perhaps the "not cold" is an explicit statement, so unless there\'s a rule that overrides it, the answer should be that it\'s not cold, so the statement "the squirrel is cold" would be false. But let me check the other rules to see if any of them could imply that the squirrel is cold, which would contradict statement 10.\n\nLooking at the conditionals, statement 10 is a direct assertion. Unless there\'s a contradiction in the premises, which would make the answer unknown. But do the premises contradict each other?\n\nLet me see. The squirrel is nice (statement 11). Statement 20 says that if something is nice, it is blue. So squirrel is blue. That\'s okay. Now, any other implications?\n\nLooking at statement 14: If something is green and blue, then it visits the bear. Since the squirrel is blue, but we don\'t know if it\'s green. So unless the squirrel is green and blue, which we don\'t know, that rule doesn\'t apply here.\n\nStatement 13: If something visits the bear and it is green, then it is nice. But again, unless the squirrel is visiting the bear and green, which we don\'t know. The squirrel is visited by the lion (statement 5), but does the squirrel visit the bear? Not stated. The mouse visits the squirrel (statement 9), and the lion visits the squirrel (statement 5). The lion also visits the mouse (statement 4). The bear\'s actions are that it likes the lion (statements 2, 17, 18). \n\nWait, statement 17 says if the bear is not green, then it likes the lion. But we know the bear does like the lion (statement 2), so that part holds. \n\nIs there any rule that could make the squirrel cold? Let me see. The only direct statement about the squirrel\'s temperature is that it is not cold (statement 10). There\'s no other statement that mentions coldness except in that one. The other statements talk about being nice, blue, etc., but not coldness. \n\nTherefore, the squirrel is explicitly stated as not cold. So the question is asking if "the squirrel is cold" is true or false. Since it\'s stated that it\'s not cold, the answer should be false. Therefore, the correct option is B) False.\n\nWait a second, but let me make sure there isn\'t any hidden implication. For instance, if being nice implies something else that could affect being cold? Let me check. The squirrel is nice (statement 11), and nice things are blue (statement 20). So squirrel is blue. But does being blue have any relation to coldness? Not directly stated. So unless there\'s a rule that connects color to temperature, which there isn\'t. So the only direct info is that it\'s not cold. \n\nAnother angle: Could there be a contradiction in the premises that makes the squirrel\'s status unknown? For example, if another rule implied it is cold, conflicting with statement 10. Let\'s see. \n\nLooking at all the conditionals, the only one that could possibly involve coldness is if there\'s a rule that says if something is X then it is cold, but I don\'t see any such rule. The only mention of cold is in statement 10\'s negation. So no, there\'s no conflicting rule. Therefore, the answer is B) False.\n'] 54%|█████▍    | 326/600 [10:06:30<8:27:39, 111.17s/it] 
