ases bald eagle)\n\nThe question is about whether the tiger does not visit the bald eagle. So, we need to determine if \"tiger does not visit bald eagle\" is true, false, or unknown based on these premises.\n\nLet me start by noting all direct relationships first before getting into the implications from the conditional statements.\n\nFrom the given statements (facts):\n\n- Bald Eagle (BE) visits squirrel (S). (Premise 4)\n- Squirrel (S) chases BE (premise 8) and chases tiger (T) (premise 9). Also, S visits T (premise 10).\n- Lion (L) is cold (5), kind (6), and needs T (7).\n- Tiger (T) is red (11).\n- BE needs T (3).\n- BE is cold and rough (1,2).\n\nNow, let's look at the conditional statements and see if any of them can lead us to conclusions about the tiger visiting the bald eagle.\n\nFirst, let's see if any premises directly mention the tiger visiting the bald eagle. Scanning through the given premises, I don't see any direct statement about T visiting BE. The only direct visit mentions are:\n\n- BE visits S (4)\n- S visits T (10)\n- The last premise (20) says if someone chases the tiger, then the tiger chases the bald eagle. But that's about chasing, not visiting.\n\nSo, to determine if T visits BE, we need to see if any of the conditional statements can lead us to that conclusion or its negation.\n\nLet me go through each conditional statement and see what they imply, perhaps building a chain of reasoning.\n\nStarting with premise 12: If someone needs the squirrel, then they chase the tiger.\n\nWe know that BE needs T (premise 3), not the squirrel, so that might not apply here. The lion needs T (premise 7), so again not squirrel. The squirrel itself: does the squirrel need someone? Not mentioned. So maybe this premise isn't directly applicable yet.\n\nPremise 13: If someone is rough and they chase the squirrel, then the squirrel visits the lion.\n\nBE is rough (premise 2). Does BE chase the squirrel? Let's check. The squirrel chases BE (premise 8), but does BE chase the squirrel? Not directly stated. So unless some other condition makes BE chase the squirrel, this might not apply. Wait, premise 15 says if someone visits the bald eagle and the bald eagle visits the squirrel, then the bald eagle chases the squirrel. Let's see: BE visits squirrel (premise 4). So if someone (say, X) visits the bald eagle, then combining with BE visiting the squirrel, then BE chases the squirrel. Wait, the antecedent is \"if someone visits the bald eagle AND the bald eagle visits the squirrel\". Since BE does visit the squirrel, then if someone (any X) visits BE, then BE chases the squirrel. Hmm, but that might not be directly useful here.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the BE itself is the \"someone\" in premise 15? Wait, no. The premise says \"if someone visits the bald eagle and the bald eagle visits the squirrel...\", so if X is the someone who visits BE, then BE chases the squirrel. Since BE does visit the squirrel, then if X visits BE, then BE chases S. But does BE chase S? The squirrel chases BE (premise8), but BE chasing S isn't stated. Let me note that as a possible implication.\n\nBut maybe let's look elsewhere first.\n\nPremise 14: If someone visits the tiger and they visit the lion, then the lion needs the bald eagle.\n\nWe know that the squirrel visits the tiger (premise10). So S visits T. Does S visit the lion? Not stated yet. If S did visit the lion, then premise14 would trigger, leading to lion needing BE. But since L already needs T (premise7), but needing BE would be an additional fact. Not sure yet.\n\nPremise16: If someone is red, they chase the lion. The tiger is red (premise11). Therefore, the tiger (T) must chase the lion (L). So T chases L.\n\nSo from T being red, we can conclude T chases L (from premise16).\n\nNow, let's see what that leads to. If T chases L, then look at premise19: If someone chases the lion, then they visit the bald eagle. Since T chases L, then T must visit the bald eagle (BE). So T visits BE?\n\nWait,