nfo.\n\nLet me see if there's any way to determine Gary's whiteness. Let's look at the rules that can lead to someone being white.\n\nThe rules that can lead to someone being white:\n\n- If someone is quiet and cold, then they are white (statement 11). So quiet + cold \u2192 white.\n\n- Also, if someone is furry, they must be white (statement 10), but that's the converse.\n\nSo to get someone's whiteness, either:\n\n1. They are quiet and cold (then they are white), or\n\n2. They are furry (then they must be white, but that's not helpful unless we know they are furry).\n\nAlternatively, if someone is white and smart, then they are furry (statement 8). So if someone is both white and smart, they must be furry. So if someone is smart and white, they must be furry.\n\nNow, Gary is smart (from being big). To know if he's white, let's see:\n\nGary is quiet (given). Is he cold? Let's check if we can determine if Gary is cold.\n\nLooking at the given info, Fiona is cold (given). Bob's coldness is not stated. Gary's coldness is not stated. The only person stated as cold is Fiona. So unless there's a rule that makes Gary cold, we can't assume that.\n\nThe only rule that might lead to someone being cold is if they are quiet and cold \u2192 white, but that's a conditional. Wait, the rule is \"if someone is quiet and cold then they are white\", but that doesn't mean that being quiet and white requires coldness. So to get Gary's coldness, we need another path.\n\nAlternatively, maybe through Harry. Let me check Harry's info. Harry is big and rough. Since he's big, he's smart. Also, statement 13 says \"If Harry is big then Harry is cold.\" Wait, statement 13 is an if-then: If Harry is big, then Harry is cold. Since Harry is indeed big (given), then by modus ponens, Harry must be cold. Wait, that's a key point.\n\nSo Harry is big \u2192 therefore, Harry is cold (from statement 13). So Harry is cold. Also, Harry is rough, big, smart (from being big), and cold.\n\nWait, that's important. So Harry is cold. Let me note that. So Harry is cold. Now, Fiona is cold (given). So both Fiona and Harry are cold. Bob's coldness is unknown unless inferred.\n\nNow, going back to Gary. Let's see if we can find out if Gary is cold. There's no direct info. The only possible way is through some rule. The rules that mention coldness are the ones involving the quiet and cold leading to white.\n\nBut Gary is quiet. If he were also cold, then by statement 11, he would be white. But we don't know if he's cold.\n\nSo, to find if Gary is furry, we need to see if he is both white and smart (so that statement 8 would make him furry). He is smart, so the question is whether he is white.\n\nIs Gary white?\n\nHow can someone be white?\n\nEither:\n\n- They are quiet and cold (so, quiet + cold \u2192 white)\n\n- Or, they are furry (since all furry are white, but that's not helpful unless we know they are furry)\n\nAlternatively, maybe through some other rules. Let me see.\n\nAlternatively, if someone is white and smart, they are furry. But that's a conditional.\n\nAlternatively, maybe being big and smart?\n\nWait, big and smart people are quiet (statement 14). But that's already true for Gary (he is quiet), so that doesn't help with whiteness.\n\nAlternatively, let's see if Gary can be white. To be white via the quiet and cold path, he needs to be cold. If he's not cold, then that path is closed. If he is cold, then he would be white. But we don't know if he is cold. So unless there's a rule that makes him cold, we can't assume.\n\nAre there any rules that could make Gary cold?\n\nThe only coldness rule is statement 13, which applies only to Harry. So unless there's another rule, no. So Gary's coldness is unknown. Therefore, his whiteness is unknown unless another path exists.\n\nAlternatively, maybe through being furry. Wait, if Gary were furry, then he must be white (from statement 10). But we don't know if he is furry. To be furry, he needs to be white and smart. Since he is smart, if he were white, he would have to be furry. But we can't establish his whiteness, so this is circular.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another angle. Let me see if any of the other people's attributes can influence Gary.\n\nWait, let's check Fiona's status. Fiona is cold and rough. Since she is rough, she must be big (from statement 9). So Fiona is big. And since she's big, she must be smart (statement 15). So Fiona is big, cold, rough, smart.\n\nSo Fiona is quiet? Not mentioned. So she could be quiet or not. The problem only says Gary is quiet. Bob's quietness isn't mentioned.\n\nNow, for Fiona: since she is quiet and cold? Wait, Fiona is cold, but is she quiet? The problem only says Gary is quiet. Fiona's quietness isn't stated. So unless there's a rule that makes Fiona quiet, she might not be. So Fiona can't be used to get Gary's whiteness.\n\nBack to Gary. Let's see if there's any other way. Since he's big and smart, he is quiet (from statement 14). So he is quiet. But to get white, he needs to be cold. If he were cold, then quiet + cold \u2192 white. But we don't know his coldness. Since there's no rule that forces Gary to be cold, unless...\n\nWait, are there any other rules involving coldness? Let me check all statements again.\n\nThe given statements:\n\n8. If someone is white and smart then they are furry.\n\n9. All rough people are big.\n\n10. All furry people are white.\n\n11. If someone is quiet and cold then they are white.\n\n13. If Harry is big then Harry is cold. (Which we already used to conclude Harry is cold.)\n\n14. Big, smart people are quiet.\n\n15. If someone is big then they are smart.\n\nSo the only coldness rules are statement 11 (quiet + cold \u2192 white) and statement 13 (Harry is cold because he's big). There's no other rule that would make someone cold unless they're Harry.\n\nTherefore, Gary's coldness is unknown. So we can't assume he's cold. Therefore, we can't conclude he's white via the quiet and cold path. Therefore, his whiteness is unknown.\n\nThus, since we can't confirm he's white, we can't confirm he's furry (since that requires white and smart). So the statement \"Gary is furry\" is unknown? Or can we find another path?\n\nWait, maybe through the contrapositive. Let's see:\n\nSuppose Gary is furry. Then he must be white (from statement 10). To be furry, he must be white and smart (from statement 8). Since he is smart, if he were white, he would be furry. But we don't know if he's white. Alternatively, is there a way to prove he can't be furry?\n\nWait, if we can show that he's not white, then he can't be furry. But we can't prove he's not white either. Because maybe he is white through another path? Wait, the only paths to white are being quiet and cold, or being furry (but that requires being white already). So unless there's another way to be white, like being furry requires being white, but that's circular. So the only way to be white is either via quiet and cold or being furry (but that needs to be white already). So the only way is quiet and cold, or some other path?\n\nWait, maybe there's a mistake here. Let me think again.\n\nThe rules are:\n\n- If someone is white and smart \u2192 furry.\n\n- All furry are white.\n\n- If someone is quiet and cold \u2192 white.\n\n- All big are smart (statement 15).\n\n- Big and smart \u2192 quiet (statement 14).\n\nSo for someone to be white, they can be:\n\n1. Quiet and cold \u2192 white.\n\n2. Furry \u2192 white (but that requires being white and smart first, so circular).\n\nThus, the only non-circular way is quiet and cold.\n\nTherefore, unless someone is quiet and cold, they can't be white unless they are furry (but that requires being white already, so that's not helpful). So the only way to be white is quiet and cold, or being furry (but that needs being white, so no).\n\nTherefore, unless Gary is quiet and cold, he can't be white. Since he is quiet, but we don't know if he's cold, so his whiteness is unknown. Hence, whether 