g.\n\nSo the tiger is big. That\'s a new fact.\n\nNow, the tiger is big, so by rule1, the tiger must need the tiger? Wait, rule1 says if something is big, it needs the tiger. So the tiger is big, so it needs the tiger? That would mean the tiger needs itself. But the original statements didn\'t mention that, but according to the rule, yes. Is that possible?\n\nHmm, that might be a problem. Because if the tiger is big, then by rule1 it must need the tiger. But needing oneself might be a contradiction? Or perhaps it\'s allowed. The problem doesn\'t specify that needing is between different entities. But maybe it\'s okay. Let me note that as an inference: the tiger needs the tiger.\n\nWait, but the original statements say "the tiger needs the bald eagle" and "the tiger needs the rabbit". So adding that the tiger also needs itself. Not sure if that\'s an issue, but according to the rule, it must be so.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake here. Let me check again.\n\nWait, the tiger is big (from rule2\'s conclusion), so applying rule1: if something is big, it needs the tiger. So the tiger is big, so it needs the tiger. So the tiger must need the tiger. That\'s a bit odd, but maybe it\'s acceptable in the problem\'s context. Let me proceed.\n\nSo the tiger is big, needs the bald eagle, the rabbit, and itself.\n\nNow, back to the question. The bear\'s visiting the rabbit.\n\nIs there any rule that could lead to that?\n\nLooking at the rules again. Let\'s see:\n\nSuppose the bear visits the rabbit. Is there any rule that would contradict that?\n\nAlternatively, if the bear does visit the rabbit, then perhaps that would trigger some other rule.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the rabbit\'s visiting the tiger. The rabbit visits the tiger, so maybe that\'s related.\n\nAlternatively, let\'s see if the bear visiting the rabbit is prohibited or required by any rule.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the rabbit\'s needs. The rabbit needs the bear (given) and the bald eagle (given), and the tige