eone is blue and sees the tiger, then the tiger needs the squirrel. But we don\'t know if the squirrel is blue or sees the tiger. The mouse likes the tiger (statement 7), but that\'s a different relation. So maybe not applicable.\n\n15. If the mouse needs the bald eagle and the bald eagle likes the mouse, then the bald eagle is rough. The first part of the antecedent: mouse needs bald eagle. From statement 3, the bald eagle needs the mouse, but does the mouse need the bald eagle? The question is about the direction. Statement 3 says bald eagle needs mouse, but not necessarily the other way. So unless stated otherwise, the mouse might not need the bald eagle. However, statement 15\'s antecedent is "mouse needs the bald eagle and bald eagle likes the mouse". But we don\'t know if the bald eagle likes the mouse. The bald eagle is described as rough (statement 2) and nice (statement 1). The likes from the mouse are towards the tiger (statement 7). So maybe this condition isn\'t triggered. So maybe 15 isn\'t helpful here.\n\n16. If the mouse needs the squirrel, then the squirrel likes the mouse. Again, does the mouse need the squirrel? From statement 4, the bald eagle needs the squirrel, but the mouse\'s needs are not mentioned except needing the bald eagle? Wait, statement 3 says the bald eagle needs the mouse. The mouse\'s needs are not stated except in statement 7 (likes tiger) and 8 (sees bald eagle). So maybe the mouse doesn\'t need the squirrel, so the antecedent here isn\'t met, so 16\'s consequent (squirrel likes mouse) would be irrelevant here.\n\n17. If someone is rough, then they see the mouse. The bald eagle is rough (statement 2), so by 17, the bald eagle must see the mouse. Does the bald eagle see the mouse? The context says the mouse sees the bald eagle (statement 8), but that\'s the mouse\'s perspective. The bald eagle seeing the mouse isn\'t stated. But according to 17, since the bald eagle is rough, they must see the mouse. Wait, that\'s an important po