      "text": ":\nOkay, let's tackle this problem step by step. First, I need to parse all the given information and see how it applies to Bob. The question is whether \"Bob is not big\" is true, false, or unknown. The options are A, B, or C.\n\nStarting with the context provided:\n\nAnne is furry, smart. Bob is kind, nice. Charlie has several attributes: big, green, nice, young. Gary is furry, kind, nice, young. There are also several conditional statements. Let me list out the premises first to organize.\n\nFirst, the direct attributes:\n\n- Anne: furry, smart\n- Bob: kind, nice\n- Charlie: big, green, nice, young\n- Gary: furry, kind, nice, young\n\nNow the conditional statements:\n\n1. If something is young, then it is nice. (Y \u2192 N)\n2. If Anne is green, then Anne is big. (A green \u2192 A big)\n3. Smart things are nice. (S \u2192 N)\n4. All smart, kind things are green. (S \u2227 K \u2192 G)\n5. Young, big things are kind. (Y \u2227 B \u2192 K)\n6. Kind, smart things are furry. (K \u2227 S \u2192 F)\n7. If something is nice and big, then it is young. (N \u2227 B \u2192 Y)\n8. All nice things are kind. (N \u2192 K)\n9. If Anne is young and big, then Anne is nice. (A Y \u2227 A B \u2192 A N)\n\nNow, the question is about Bob not being big. Let's see what we can derive about Bob.\n\nFirst, Bob's known attributes: kind and nice. From premise 8, all nice things are kind, so that checks out for Bob. Since Bob is nice and kind, that's consistent.\n\nNow, do we have any information that could link Bob to being big or not?\n\nLooking at the conditional statements:\n\nLet me see if there's any rule that connects Bob's attributes (kind, nice) to big.\n\nPremise 5 says that if something is young and big, then it is kind. But that's Y \u2227 B \u2192 K. But Bob's kind, so if he were both young and big, that would fit, but we don't know if he is young. Wait, Bob's attributes don't mention young. However, premise 1 says if something is young, then it's nice. Since Bob is