es aren't stated, unless there's a rule that would require it, we can't assume that. \n\nWait, the rule says \"if someone likes the bear, then they chase the bear.\" So, if the bear were to like the bear, then the bear would chase the bear. But since we don't know if the bear likes itself, that path is uncertain. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another way the bear could be chasing itself. Let me check all the facts again. The bear chases the lion. The squirrel chases the bald eagle. The lion's actions: the lion likes the bear and visits the squirrel, but there's no mention of the lion chasing anyone except perhaps through rules. Wait, the rule about the lion: \"If the lion is big and green, then lion chases bald eagle.\" But the lion is not green (given fact), so that rule's antecedent is false, so the consequent doesn't apply. So the lion isn't chasing the bald eagle.\n\nIs there any other rule that could make the bear chase itself? Let's look at the other rules. The rule: \"If the lion visits the squirrel and the squirrel chases the bear, then the bear chases the bald eagle.\" Wait, but that's about the bear chasing the bald eagle, not itself. \n\nAnother rule: \"If someone visits the lion, then they like the bald eagle.\" But that's about visitors to the lion liking the bald eagle. \n\nHmm. Let me think again. The only direct fact about the bear's actions is that it chases the lion. There's no fact stating that the bear chases itself. \n\nThe only possible way the bear could be chasing itself is if the rule \"if someone likes the bear then they chase the bear\" applies when the someone is the bear itself. So if the bear likes the bear, then it would chase itself. But since we don't have any information that the bear likes itself, this is an unknown. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another way. Let's see. The bear visits the bald eagle and the squirrel. The rule: \"If someone visits the lion, then they are cold.\" But the bear doesn't visit the lion, so that do