 BE.\n\nSo that\'s another point. T does not eat BE. But not sure how that helps with the bear visiting the mouse.\n\nLet me see other conditionals. Let\'s look at conditional20: If something visits the mouse then it is kind.\n\nWe already derived that BE visits M (from conditional3 and fact4), so BE must be kind (as above). So BE is kind, and cold (fact2). Therefore, by conditional6 (cold and kind things are big), BE is big. So BE is big.\n\nNow, looking at conditional17: If something visits the bear and it is kind then the bear likes the tiger.\n\nThe "something" here is an entity that visits B and is kind. Let\'s see if any such entity exists.\n\nWe know that BE visits B (fact5). Is BE kind? Yes, because BE visits the mouse (from earlier) and thus must be kind (conditional20). So BE is kind. Therefore, the antecedent of conditional17 is satisfied: BE visits B and BE is kind. Therefore, the consequent is that the bear likes the tiger. So the bear (B) likes the tiger (T). \n\nWait, that\'s a new conclusion. So B likes T. But the original facts didn\'t state that. The fact12 says the tiger likes the bear, but not vice versa. So now, via this conditional, since BE visits B and is kind, then B likes T. So B likes T is now a conclusion.\n\nSo now, we have B likes T. \n\nNow, moving on. Let\'s see if there\'s any other conditionals that can help us with whether the bear visits the mouse.\n\nLooking at conditional5 again: "If something visits the bear and it is kind then the bear likes the tiger." We used that to get B likes T. But maybe other conditionals?\n\nConditional8: If something visits the mouse, it is kind. We know BE visits M, so BE is kind. But what about others?\n\nThe mouse visiting itself? Not sure. The question is about the bear visiting the mouse, so let\'s focus on B.\n\nIs there any information that could lead us to conclude that B visits M or that it doesn\'t?\n\nLooking at the facts given, the bear does not visit the tiger (fact8). But nothing about v