eing seen by the squirrel, not the other way around. The premise requires that the entity (X) sees the squirrel. So if the tiger sees the squirrel, then X (the tiger) would have to be cold. But does the tiger see the squirrel? The context says "The squirrel sees the tiger," which means the squirrel is looking at the tiger, but not necessarily the reverse. The tiger sees the bear, as per "The tiger sees the bear." So the tiger doesn\'t see the squirrel, so that rule doesn\'t apply to the tiger. \n\nAnother premise: "Round things are green." The bear is round, so it must be green. Wait, the bear is round (given), so according to this rule, the bear is green. But the context says "The bald eagle is green," not the bear. But according to the rule, since the bear is round, it must be green. That\'s a conflict unless the bear is also green. Wait, but the original context didn\'t state the bear\'s color. So perhaps the bear is green because it\'s round? That\'s an important point. Let me note that:\n\nPremise: "Round things are green." The bear is round (given), so the bear must be green. Therefore, the bear is green. But the context only says the bald eagle is green. So the bear is also green because of this rule. \n\nNow, with that in mind, let\'s see other implications. For example, the fifth premise about the bear\'s age. Since the bear likes the bald eagle, according to premise 5 ("If something likes the bald eagle then it is young"), the bear would have to be young. But the context only says the squirrel is young. So this would mean the bear is young. However, the context says "The squirrel is young," but the bear\'s age isn\'t stated. So according to the rules, the bear must be young because it likes the bald eagle. \n\nBut the question is about the tiger. Let me see if the tiger\'s age can be inferred. Let\'s see if any premises connect the tiger\'s age. \n\nAnother premise: "If something needs the bear and the bear is green then it is round." Since the bear is green (as per above), then any entity that needs the bear would have to be round. The tiger needs the bear (given). Therefore, the tiger must be round. But does being round have any implications? The premise "Round things are green" would make the tiger green if it\'s round. But the context doesn\'t say the tiger is green, but according to the rules, if it\'s round, then it must be green. \n\nWait, but the tiger\'s roundness is only determined if it needs the bear and the bear is green. Since the tiger does need the bear, and the bear is green (from being round), so yes, the tiger must be round. Therefore, the tiger is round. Then, since it\'s round, it must be green (from "Round things are green"). But the context didn\'t state the tiger\'s color, but according to the rules, it\'s green. \n\nNow, another premise: "If something is green then it needs the squirrel." Since the tiger is green (as per above), that means the tiger needs the squirrel. But the context says the tiger needs the bear, but not necessarily the squirrel. Wait, but according to this rule, if the tiger is green, then it must need the squirrel. So the tiger needs the squirrel. However, does that conflict with anything? The context says the squirrel needs the bald eagle, but not sure. \n\nNow, going back to the question: Is the tiger not young? We need to see if the tiger is young or not. Let\'s see if any rules apply here. \n\nLooking at premise 5 again: "If something likes the bald eagle then it is young." The tiger does not like the bald eagle, so that doesn\'t apply. \n\nIs there any other premise that can determine the tiger\'s age? Let me check again.\n\nAnother premise: "If something needs the squirrel then it needs the tiger." The tiger is green, so it needs the squirrel (from "If something is green then it needs the squirrel"). Therefore, the tiger needs the squirrel. Therefore, according to the premise "If something needs the squirrel then it needs the tiger," the tiger (which needs the squirrel) must also need the tiger? Wait, that\'s a bit confusing. Wait, the subject here is "something" that needs the squirrel. So if the tiger needs the squirrel, then the tiger must need the tiger itself? That seems a bit odd, but logically, it would mean the tiger must need itself. However, that might not be a problem, but perhaps there\'s another implication here. \n\nAlternatively, maybe that premise is saying that if an entity (X) needs the squirrel, then X must also need the tiger. Since the tiger (X) needs the squirrel, then X (the tiger) must need the tiger. But needing oneself might not be prohibited here. \n\nBut perhaps more importantly, are there any other rules that could affect the tiger\'s age? Let me think again. \n\nThe squirrel is young (given). The bear, according to the rules, must be young because it likes the bald eagle. So the bear is young. The squirrel is also young. \n\nNow, the question is about the tiger\'s age. The only way to determine that is if any rule implies the tiger must be young or must not be young. \n\nLooking at the premises again. \n\nIs there a premise that says if something is not young, then something else? Not that I can see. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the tiger\'s needs or relationships can lead us to its age. \n\nWait, the tiger needs the bear. The bear is green (because it\'s round). Also, the bear is young. \n\nAnother premise: "If something needs the bear and it needs the bald eagle then the bear likes the bald eagle." Wait, does the tiger need the bald eagle? The context says the squirrel needs the bald eagle, and the tiger needs the bear. The tiger\'s needs: the tiger needs the bear (given) and, since it\'s green, needs the squirrel (from the green premise). But does the tiger need the bald eagle? Not directly stated. \n\nWait, the premise says "If something needs the bear and it needs the bald eagle then the bear likes the bald eagle." The bear already likes the bald eagle (given), so this premise is a conditional that would be true in any case where that antecedent is true, but it doesn\'t give us new info unless we can find an entity that needs both the bear and the bald eagle. \n\nThe squirrel needs the bald eagle (given) and does it need the bear? The squirrel\'s needs are only stated as needing the bald eagle. So if the squirrel needed the bear as well, then the premise would apply, but since it\'s not stated, perhaps not. \n\nAlternatively, the tiger needs the bear, but does it need the bald eagle? Not as per the given context, unless via the green rule. The tiger is green (because it\'s round, as established earlier), so it must need the squirrel. So the tiger needs the squirrel and the bear. But not the bald eagle. \n\nSo the tiger doesn\'t need the bald eagle, so the antecedent of that premise isn\'t met for the tiger. \n\nHmm. \n\nLet me try another angle. The question is whether the tiger is not young (so the statement is "the tiger is not young" is true). To prove it\'s true, we need to show the tiger cannot be young. To prove it\'s false, show it is young. Otherwise, unknown. \n\nIs there any rule that would require the tiger to be young? \n\nLooking at all premises again. \n\nThe only premise that links liking the bald eagle to being young. Since the tiger doesn\'t like the bald eagle, that doesn\'t apply. \n\nAre there any other premises that could require the tiger to be young? \n\nAnother possibility: the squirrel is young, and maybe some relation? Not directly. \n\nWait, the bear is young because it likes the bald eagle. So the bear is young. The squirrel is also young. \n\nIs there any rule that would make the tiger young? For example, if the tiger likes something that requires it to be young? The tiger likes the bear and the squirrel. \n\nIs there a rule that if you like a young thing, you have to be young? Not that I can see. \n\nAlternatively, if the tiger is green (as established), then the premise "If something is green then it needs the squirrel" applies. But needing the squirrel doesn\'t directly affect age. \n\nWait, another premise: "If something needs the squirrel then it needs the tiger." Since the tiger needs the squirrel (from being green), it must need the tiger. But needing oneself? Not sure. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps there\'s a chain here. Let\'s see:\n\nTiger is green → needs squirrel → must need tiger. \n\nBut that\'s a loop. Not sure if that helps. \n\nAlternatively, is there any contradiction if the tiger were young? Let\'s see:\n\nSuppose the tiger is young. Does that conflict with any rules? The premises don\'t say anything about being young causing other things, except that if something is young, maybe... but the rules are all "if X then Y", so unless being young is a condition for something else. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the bear being young and the squirrel being young, but the tiger could be either. \n\nIs there any information that would determine the tiger\'s age? \n\nWait, the only other mention of "young" is in the squirrel being young and the premise about liking the bald eagle implying youth. Since the tiger doesn\'t like the bald eagle, that doesn\'t apply. \n\nTherefore, there\'s no information that directly states the tiger\'s age, and no rule that would force it to be young or not. Therefore, the statement "The tiger is not young" would be unknown? \n\nWait, but wait a second. Let me check again. The squirrel is young. The bear is also young because it likes the bald eagle. The bald eagle\'s age isn\'t mentioned. The tiger\'s age isn\'t mentioned anywhere except possibly through the rules. \n\nWait, the only way the tiger could be young is if it liked the bald eagle. But since it doesn\'t, it\'s not forced to be young. So the tiger could be young or not? Or maybe not, because there\'s no rule that requires it to be young unless it meets some condition. \n\nTherefore, since there\'s no information that the tiger is young, and no rule that would require it to be young or not, then the statement "The tiger is not young" would be unknown. \n\nWait, but let me check if there\'s a rule that could make the tiger not young. For example, if being green implies something, but green implies needing the squirrel, which doesn\'t relate to age. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the tiger\'s roundness (since it\'s round) and being green doesn\'t affect age. \n\nHmm. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a rule that could be applied indirectly. Let me see:\n\nThe bear is young. The bear likes the bald eagle. The bald eagle likes the squirrel and the tiger. \n\nWait, another premise: "If something likes the bald eagle then it is young." The bear likes the bald eagle, so the bear is young. The squirrel doesn\'t like the bald eagle, so that\'s okay. The tiger doesn\'t like the bald eagle, so no. \n\nAnother angle: The squirrel is young. The squirrel needs the bald eagle. The bald eagle likes the squirrel. \n\nIs there any rule that could link the squirrel\'s needs to the tiger\'s age? Not that I can see. \n\nWait, the squirrel sees the tiger. The premise says: "If something sees the squirrel and the squirrel likes the bear then it is cold." The squirrel likes the bear, so if something (like the tiger?) sees the squirrel, then that something would be cold. But does the tiger see the squirrel? The context says "The squirrel sees the tiger," which means the squirrel is looking at the tiger, but not necessarily the tiger looking at the squirrel. The tiger sees the bear, not the squirrel. Therefore, the premise doesn\'t apply to the tiger. \n\nTherefore, the tiger\'s coldness is already given, but that doesn\'t relate to age. \n\nHmm. \n\nSo, after going through all premises, I don\'t see any rule that would make the tiger\'s age determined. The only entities with stated or inferred ages are the squirrel (explicitly young) and the bear (inferred young because it likes the bald eagle). The bald eagle\'s age isn\'t mentioned, and the tiger\'s age isn\'t addressed by any of the rules unless I missed something. \n\nTherefore, the statement "The tiger is not young" cannot be proven true or false based on the given information. So the answer would be C) Unknown. \n\nWait, but let me check one more time to make sure I didn\'t overlook a rule. \n\nLooking at all premises again:\n\n"If something likes the bald eagle then it is young." So anything that likes the bald eagle must be young. The bear does, so it\'s young. The squirrel doesn\'t like the bald eagle, so that\'s okay. The tiger doesn\'t, so okay. \n\nNo other premises about youth except that. \n\nThe tiger\'s age is not determined, so the statement "The tiger is not young" is unknown. Therefore, the correct answer is C.\n'] 63%|██████▎   | 379/600 [11:54:41<8:28:07, 137.95s/it]
