mething is red and green then it is blue.\n7. If something is young and big then it is smart.\n8. If something is blue and green then it is smart.\n9. Blue, red things are smart. (Hmm, this might need rephrasing. Maybe "All blue and red things are smart"? Or perhaps it\'s saying "Blue red things are smart," but that\'s a bit confusing. Let me check the original again.)\n\nWait, the original says: "Blue, red things are smart." Maybe that\'s a typo or misphrasing. Alternatively, maybe it\'s "All blue and red things are smart." Or perhaps it\'s "Blue red things are smart," which could mean things that are both blue and red. But that might not make sense. Alternatively, maybe the comma is separating, so "Blue, red things are smart" might mean "Blue things and red things are smart," but that\'s unclear. I\'ll have to consider this as part of the rules. Maybe it\'s better to parse it as "All blue and red things are smart," but I need to be cautious here.\n\nLet me note all the rules again for clarity:\n\n1. All red → furry.\n2. All furry → red. (So red and furry are equivalent? Because if all red are furry and all furry are red, then they are the same set. So being red is equivalent to being furry.)\n3. Young → red. (So since Charlie is young, by rule 3, Charlie must be red. Wait, but the first thing is Charlie is young. So Charlie is young → red. Then by rule 1, since Charlie is red, he must be furry. Also, by rule 2, since he\'s furry, he is red, so that\'s consistent.)\n4. If (smart ∧ ¬red) → ¬green. (In other words, if something is smart and not red, then it can\'t be green.)\n5. All furry → green. (But from rule 1 and 2, furry is equivalent to red, so red things are furry, and all furry (i.e., red) things are green? Wait, but rule 5 says all furry are green. Since furry is equivalent to red (from 1 and 2), that would mean all red things are green. But then combining with rule 6: If red and green → blue. Wait, that could lead to some contradictions. Let me see.\n\nWai