ince he\'s furry, he\'s green (rule 5). Then since he\'s red and green, he must be blue (rule 6). So now Charlie is red, green, blue, and young. \n\nNow, the question is whether Charlie is smart. Let\'s see the rules about being smart.\n\nRule 4: If (smart ∧ ¬red) → ¬green. So if something is smart and not red, then it\'s not green. \n\nRule 7: If (young ∧ big) → smart. Charlie is young but we don\'t know if he\'s big. The context says Fiona is big, but Charlie\'s size isn\'t mentioned. \n\nRule 8: If (blue ∧ green) → smart. Charlie is both blue and green (since he\'s blue from rule 6 and green from rule 5). Therefore, by rule 8, Charlie would be smart. \n\nAdditionally, rule 9: "Blue, red things are smart." If that\'s interpreted as (blue ∧ red) → smart, then since Charlie is both blue and red, he would also be smart by this rule. \n\nWait, but let me confirm the interpretation of rule 9. The original says "Blue, red things are smart." The wording is a bit unclear. It could mean "Blue and red things are smart," meaning things that are both blue and red. Alternatively, it might mean "Blue things and red things are smart," meaning separate categories. But given that "Blue, red things" with a comma might be intended as "blue and red things" (i.e., things that are both blue and red), so that\'s probably the right interpretation. So rule 9 is (blue ∧ red) → smart. \n\nSo Charlie is blue and red (and also green, but that\'s okay), so by rule 8 (blue and green → smart) and rule 9 (blue and red → smart), he would satisfy both, so he is smart. \n\nWait, but let me make sure there\'s no conflict. Let me check all the rules again to see if there\'s any contradiction in Charlie\'s attributes.\n\nCharlie is red (from being young), so he must be furry (rule1). Furry implies green (rule5). So he is green. Then, red and green → blue (rule6). So blue is added. So he is red, green, blue, young, furry. \n\nNow, for smartness:\n\nRule 7: If young and big → smart. But Charlie\'s size i