le must see the lion. Is that a problem?\n\nWait, the bald eagle seeing the lion isn't contradicted, but does that add anything? The bald eagle seeing the lion would mean that the bald eagle now meets the condition of rule 1: seeing the lion and the bald eagle (since it is the bald eagle, so it sees itself?) Wait, no. Wait, rule 1 says if something sees the lion and the bald eagle, then it chases the cat. So the bald eagle, in this scenario, visits the tiger (so maybe it's \"something\" that visits the tiger?), but more importantly, if the bald eagle sees the lion and the bald eagle (itself?), then it would have to chase the cat. But does the bald eagle see the lion?\n\nFrom the inference, because the bald eagle visited the tiger and the tiger sees the cat, then according to rule 3, the bald eagle must see the lion. So now the bald eagle sees the lion. So the bald eagle now sees both the lion and itself (the bald eagle). Wait, but the bald eagle is an entity, so does it see itself? The problem doesn't say that, but according to the rule, if it visits the tiger and the tiger sees the cat, then it must see the lion. So the bald eagle now sees the lion. But does that trigger rule 1?\n\nRule 1: If something sees the lion and the bald eagle, then it chases the cat. The bald eagle sees the lion (from rule 3's application) and obviously sees itself (since it's the bald eagle). So that \"something\" is the bald eagle. Therefore, the bald eagle must chase the cat. But the premises don't mention the bald eagle chasing the cat, so that would be a new inference. But does that cause any problem?\n\nWell, if the bald eagle now chases the cat, then according to rule 2, the bald eagle must be young. But the original premises don't state anything about the bald eagle's age. However, that's okay because the question is about the tiger, not the bald eagle. But the problem here is that if the bald eagle is now required to chase the cat, does that contradict any existing statements? The