lf. But I don\'t see any such rule here. However, maybe some of the conditional statements can help us here.\n\nLet me check the conditional statements. Let\'s go through each one:\n\n12. If something eats the mouse, then it is round.\n\nFrom premise 10, the mouse eats the bald eagle. Wait, the mouse eats the bald eagle? That\'s interesting. So, the mouse is eating the bald eagle. So, according to premise 12, if something eats the mouse, then it must be round. But here, the bald eagle is being eaten by the mouse. Wait, the subject here is "something eats the mouse", so the eater of the mouse would have to be round. However, in premise 10, the mouse is the eater of the bald eagle, not the other way around. So maybe that\'s not directly relevant here.\n\n13. If the mouse is red, then the mouse sees the cow.\n\nBut we don\'t know the color of the mouse. The mouse\'s color isn\'t mentioned except in this conditional. Since the premise doesn\'t state the mouse is red, this might be irrelevant unless we can infer the mouse\'s color from elsewhere. The cow is blue (premise 5), but nothing about the mouse\'s color. So maybe this doesn\'t help.\n\n14. If something chases the cat, then it does not chase the mouse.\n\nSo, if an entity chases the cat, then that same entity does not chase the mouse. Now, the first premise says the bald eagle chases the cat. Therefore, applying this rule, the bald eagle cannot chase the mouse. But the question is about chasing itself, not the mouse. Hmm. But maybe there\'s another angle here.\n\n15. If the mouse chases the cat, then the cat eats the mouse.\n\nBut does the mouse chase the cat? Let\'s see. The mouse\'s actions: premise 9 says the mouse does not chase the cow, but there\'s no direct statement about the mouse chasing the cat. Unless we can infer it from other premises. Wait, premise 16 says if something is round, then it chases the cat. The mouse is round (premise 11), so according to premise 16, since the mouse is round, it must cha