bit is a new fact from the chain. So the lion chases the rabbit now. But the original context didn\'t mention that, but according to the logic, it must be true.\n\nNow, going back to the rabbit\'s relations. The rabbit needs the bald eagle (statement 5) and the lion (statement 6). Also, the rabbit does not chase the lion (statement 4). \n\nNow, the question is about the rabbit visiting the bald eagle. Let me see if any of the conditions would be triggered if the rabbit did or didn\'t visit the bald eagle.\n\nSuppose the rabbit does visit the bald eagle. Is there any contradiction? Let\'s see:\n\nVisiting the bald eagle isn\'t mentioned in any of the statements except maybe through some other relation. Let\'s check statement 15 again: "If something chases the rabbit and does not visit the lion then the lion does not need the rabbit."\n\nThe bald eagle chases the rabbit (statement 1). So "something" here could be the bald eagle. Let\'s see:\n\nIf the bald eagle (which chases the rabbit) does not visit the lion, then the lion does not need the rabbit. But statement 3 says the lion does need the rabbit. Therefore, the antecedent of statement 15 must be false. Because the conclusion would contradict statement 3, so the premise must be false. \n\nThe premise is: "something chases the rabbit (which is the bald eagle) and it does not visit the lion." Since the bald eagle chases the rabbit, we need to check if the bald eagle visits the lion. \n\nWait, but do we know anything about the bald eagle visiting the lion? There\'s no direct statement. However, perhaps we can infer something. \n\nWait, the bald eagle is big (from statement 8 and 1). Statement 11 says big things chase the lion, so the bald eagle chases the lion. But does that imply anything about visiting? Not directly. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the bald eagle must visit the lion to prevent the implication of statement 15 from happening. Let\'s see:\n\nStatement 15\'s antecedent is (bald eagle chases rabbit AND bald eagle does not visit lion) → (lion does not need rabbit). \n\nWe know that the bald eagle does chase the rabbit (given), so to prevent the implication from leading to a contradiction (since the lion does need the rabbit per statement 3), the second part of the antecedent must be false. Therefore, the bald eagle must visit the lion. \n\nTherefore, we can conclude that the bald eagle does visit the lion. \n\nSo, bald eagle visits the lion. \n\nNow, back to the original question: does the rabbit visit the bald eagle? \n\nIs there any condition that would require the rabbit to visit the bald eagle? Let\'s see. \n\nLooking at statement 5: the rabbit needs the bald eagle. Need and visit are different relations. There\'s no statement that links needing something to visiting it, unless through some other condition. \n\nAlternatively, maybe statement 10 or others. Let me think again.\n\nStatement 10: If something needs the lion and visits the rabbit, then it chases the lion. \n\nThe rabbit needs the lion (statement 6). If the rabbit also visits the rabbit (itself), then it would have to chase the lion. But the rabbit does not chase the lion (statement 4). Therefore, the rabbit cannot visit the rabbit. \n\nBut that\'s about visiting itself, which isn\'t the case here. \n\nAlternatively, if the rabbit visited the bald eagle, does that trigger any condition? Let\'s see:\n\nIf the rabbit visits the bald eagle, is there any statement that would be violated? Let\'s check all statements again for any implications.\n\nLooking at statement 12: If something chases the tiger, then the tiger needs the rabbit. Since the bald eagle chases the tiger (statement 2), so the tiger must need the rabbit. But that\'s already covered by statement 12\'s conclusion. \n\nStatement 13: If something visits the tiger, then the tiger is not green. The tiger visits the rabbit (statement 7), but that\'s the tiger visiting the rabbit, not the rabbit visiting the tiger. \n\nStatement 14: If something chases the lion, then the lion chases the rabbit. We already have that the bald eagle chases the lion, so the lion must chase the rabbit. Which we already considered. \n\nStatement 15: As before, we\'ve used that to conclude the bald eagle must visit the lion. \n\nSo, the rabbit visiting the bald eagle doesn\'t directly trigger any of the conditions except maybe through some other relation. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the rabbit visiting the bald eagle would be irrelevant to any of the conditions, so there\'s no way to determine it. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a path where if the rabbit visited the bald eagle, then some other condition is triggered. Let me see:\n\nSuppose the rabbit does visit the bald eagle. Then, is there any statement that would require something else? Let\'s see:\n\nThe rabbit needs the bald eagle (statement 5). Does needing imply visiting? Not necessarily. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the rabbit visiting the bald eagle would make it a "something" in some condition. \n\nLooking at statement 9: If something needs the tiger then it is big. The rabbit doesn\'t need the tiger, as per the given statements. The rabbit needs the lion and the bald eagle, but not the tiger. So that\'s not triggered. \n\nStatement 10: If something needs the lion and visits the rabbit, then it chases the lion. The rabbit needs the lion (statement 6), but if the rabbit also visits the rabbit (itself), then it must chase the lion. But visiting the rabbit (itself) isn\'t the case here. \n\nAlternatively, if the rabbit visits the bald eagle, does that relate to any other condition? \n\nWait, perhaps if the rabbit visited the bald eagle, then maybe the bald eagle would have some relation to it. But the bald eagle chases the rabbit (statement 1), so chasing and visiting are separate. \n\nAlternatively, maybe statement 15\'s converse? Not sure. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the rabbit\'s visiting the bald eagle is neither required nor prohibited by any of the given statements, so we can\'