ernatively, perhaps "see the lion" refers to seeing another lion, but the context mentions only one lion. So maybe that\'s just part of the given. Anyway, maybe that\'s not directly relevant here.\n\nLooking at statement 9: The lion sees the rabbit. So the lion does see the rabbit, but that\'s about the lion\'s perspective. The question is about the rabbit seeing the lion. So that\'s different.\n\nLooking at other statements: Let\'s see if there\'s any implication that the rabbit might see the lion. For example, statement 19: If someone is young and they like the rabbit then they visit the rabbit. The rabbit likes the cow (statement 11), but that\'s the rabbit\'s action towards the cow. The rabbit\'s own attributes: it\'s round (statement 10), but nothing about its color (red?) except in statement 13 which requires the rabbit to be red for the rabbit to be big if someone likes it. Since we don\'t know the rabbit\'s color, maybe that\'s unknown. \n\nWait, the problem is about the rabbit not seeing the lion. Since there\'s no direct statement about the rabbit seeing the lion or not, perhaps the answer is unknown. But maybe there\'s some inference here.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the rabbit must see the lion if certain conditions are met. Let me check all the conditionals again.\n\nStatement 12: If someone visits the rabbit then the rabbit likes the lion. So if someone visits the rabbit, then rabbit likes lion. But does that imply anything about the rabbit seeing the lion? Not directly. Unless "visiting" implies seeing, but the problem doesn\'t specify that.\n\nStatement 14: If someone likes the lion and they see the rabbit then the lion likes the rabbit. Wait, the subject here is "someone" who likes the lion and sees the rabbit, leading the lion to like the rabbit. But the lion\'s actions are already given (statement 9 says lion sees rabbit, but does the lion like the rabbit? Not stated unless this conditional is triggered. Wait, does "they" refer to the someone or the