ion of the lion\'s color in the direct statements. The only direct color mention is the cow being green. The other attributes given for animals are: mouse is rough and round, lion eats the cow and mouse, etc., but nothing about the lion\'s color.\n\nNow, are there any implications from the conditional statements that could lead to the lion being green? Let\'s see.\n\nFirst, the conditional statements involve relationships like chasing, eating, visiting, and properties like cold, big, round, rough. The question is about color, so perhaps we need to see if any of the conditions can link the lion to the color green. Since the cow is green, maybe there\'s a chain?\n\nLet me check each conditional:\n\n1. If something is cold → it visits the mouse. Not directly related to color.\n2. If something is round and eats the mouse → mouse chases cow. The mouse is round, but does anything else eat the mouse? The cat and the lion both eat the mouse. Wait, the lion eats the mouse (from the direct statements), and the cat eats the mouse. So, for the lion: since it is round? Wait, the lion isn\'t stated to be round. The mouse is round. So the condition here is if "something is round and it eats the mouse", then the mouse chases the cow. Since the mouse is round (from the direct statements: "The mouse is round"), then if the mouse eats the mouse? Wait no, the subject here is "something", so for example, if the mouse (which is round) eats the mouse, then the mouse chases the cow. But the mouse can\'t eat itself. Alternatively, maybe the lion is the "something" here. But the lion isn\'t round. The cat isn\'t round either. So maybe this condition doesn\'t apply here. Not sure yet, but maybe this isn\'t relevant to the lion\'s color.\n\n3. If something visits the lion → it is big. The cat visits the lion (from direct statements). So since the cat visits the lion, then the cat must be big. But there\'s no mention of the cat\'s size elsewhere. However, big things are cold (statement 8), so i