d.\n12. If someone is big and red then they chase the rabbit.\n13. If someone visits the rabbit then the rabbit visits the bear.\n14. If the squirrel chases the cow and the cow is round then the cow is young.\n15. If someone chases the squirrel then the squirrel eats the rabbit.\n16. If someone eats the cow and the cow eats the rabbit then the cow chases the rabbit.\n17. If someone chases the rabbit then the rabbit is red.\n18. All red people are big.\n19. If someone eats the bear then they chase the cow.\n\nNow, the question is asking if "The bear visits the rabbit" is true, false, or unknown. Let me see what information we have about the bear and the rabbit\'s interactions.\n\nLooking at the given statements:\n\n- From point 8: The rabbit visits the bear. That tells me that the rabbit goes to see the bear, but it doesn\'t necessarily say anything about the bear visiting the rabbit. However, maybe there are implications from other rules.\n\n- From point 13: If someone visits the rabbit, then the rabbit visits the bear. Wait, the rabbit already visits the bear (point 8). So if someone else visits the rabbit, then the rabbit would have to visit the bear again? But since the rabbit already does that, maybe that\'s just reinforcing it, but perhaps the converse?\n\nWait, the structure here is that if any entity (let\'s say X) visits the rabbit, then the rabbit (X visits rabbit → rabbit visits bear). Since the rabbit already visits the bear, this rule is a conditional, but it doesn\'t mean that the rabbit can\'t do it anyway. However, if someone (like the squirrel) visits the rabbit, then the rabbit must visit the bear. Let me check if the squirrel does visit the rabbit. From point 10: The squirrel visits the rabbit. So that would trigger the condition in point 13. Therefore, if the squirrel visits the rabbit, then the rabbit must visit the bear. Which it already does, so that\'s consistent.\n\nNow, back to the question: Does the bear visit the rabbit?\n\nLooking at the given facts, there is no direct statement that says the bear visits the rabbit. The only direct statements about the bear\'s actions are chasing and eating the cow, being young, and the rabbit visiting the bear. The question is whether there\'s an implication from the rules that would require or allow the bear to visit the rabbit.\n\nLet me check the rules again to see if any of them can lead to that conclusion.\n\nFirst, let\'s see if any of the conditional statements can be triggered by existing facts to derive new information.\n\nLooking at point 12: If someone is big and red, then they chase the rabbit.\n\nFrom point 4: The cow is red. Also, point 18 says all red people are big. Wait, "people"? The problem mentions animals like bear, cow, rabbit, squirrel. Maybe "people" here is a translation or a term used for all entities? Assuming that "red people" refers to any entity (bear, cow, etc.) that is red. So, since the cow is red (point 4), then by point 18, the cow is big. Therefore, the cow is big and red. Applying point 12: since the cow is big and red, they (the cow) must chase the rabbit. Wait, but does the cow chase the rabbit?\n\nLooking at the given statements, I don\'t see any mention of the cow chasing the rabbit. However, according to point 12, if the cow is big and red (which it is), then it must chase the rabbit. But there\'s no existing statement that the cow chases the rabbit. Therefore, this would be a new implication. So perhaps we can infer that the cow chases the rabbit?\n\nWait a second, but the problem says "if someone is big and red then they chase the rabbit". So "they" here refers to the someone (the big and red entity). Since the cow is big and red, then the cow must chase the rabbit. So this would be an inferred fact. Let me note that down as an implication:\n\nImplication 1: The cow chases the rabbit (because cow is big and red).\n\nNow, moving on. Let\'s see if that leads to anything else.\n\nLooking at point 16: If someone eats the cow 