.**\n6. **All round, red people are furry.**\n7. **All smart, furry people are rough.**\n8. **If Erin is red then Erin is white.**\n9. **All smart, rough people are furry.**\n10. **All furry, rough people are round.**\n11. **If someone is white then they are rough.**\n12. **If someone is red and rough then they are smart.**\n13. **All red, rough people are white.**\n\nThe question is asking whether Anne is white. Let\'s see what we know about Anne so far: Anne is rough. We need to determine if that leads to her being white or not.\n\nLet me start by looking at the direct statements first. From the context, Anne\'s attributes are only that she\'s rough. The other people have more attributes listed. Let\'s see if any of the conditional statements can help us deduce more about Anne based on her being rough.\n\nStarting with the conditional statements that might relate to Anne:\n\n- **Statement 11:** If someone is white then they are rough. The contrapositive of this would be: If someone is not rough, then they are not white. But since Anne is rough, this doesn\'t directly help us. However, if someone is white, they must be rough. But we can\'t conclude the reverse from this (i.e., being rough doesn\'t necessarily mean they are white).\n\n- **Statement 12:** If someone is red and rough then they are smart. Since Anne is rough, if she were red, she\'d have to be smart. But we don\'t know if she\'s red yet.\n\n- **Statement 13:** All red, rough people are white. So if Anne were red and rough, then she must also be white. But again, we don\'t know if she\'s red.\n\n- **Statement 11\'s contrapositive might not help here. Let\'s see other paths.**\n\nLooking at the other conditional statements that involve being rough:\n\n- **Statement 9:** All smart, rough people are furry. So if Anne were both smart and rough, she would be furry. But we don\'t know if she\'s smart.\n\n- **Statement 10:** All furry, rough people are round. So if Anne were furry and rough, she\'d be round. But again, we don\'t know about her fur.\n\n- **Statement 7:** All smart, furry people are rough. Since Anne is already rough, that doesn\'t add info unless she\'s smart and furry.\n\nHmm, maybe let\'s consider if Anne could be white. If she is white, then by statement 11, she must be rough, which she is. So being white is consistent with her being rough. But does that mean she must be white?\n\nAlternatively, could she not be white? Let\'s see.\n\nSuppose Anne is not white. Then, is there any contradiction?\n\nWait, let\'s also look at other possible attributes. Let\'s see if there\'s any rule that connects being rough to being white.\n\nWait, statement 13: All red, rough people are white. So if someone is both red and rough, they must be white. But that doesn\'t directly affect Anne unless she\'s red.\n\nAnother angle: Let\'s see if there\'s a path from rough to white.\n\nSuppose someone is rough. Let\'s see if that implies white under some conditions.\n\nIf someone is rough, then:\n\n- If they are red, then by statement 13, they must be white. So red and rough implies white.\n\n- Also, if they are red and rough, statement 12 says they must be smart.\n\nBut what if they are not red? Then, could they be white or not?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the statements about Fiona and others can give us some inferences.\n\nWait, Fiona is red, round, smart, white, and quiet. Since Fiona is red and round, by statement 6, all round and red people are furry, so Fiona must be furry. Let me check if that\'s stated. The original Fiona\'s attributes don\'t mention furry, but according to statement 6, since she\'s round and red, she must be furry. So Fiona is also furry. Then, since Fiona is smart and furry (from statements 4 and 6), by statement 7, all smart furry people are rough. But Fiona isn\'t listed as rough. Wait, that\'s a problem. Wait, hold on. Let me check Fiona\'s attributes again.\n\nWait the context says Fiona is "quiet, red, round, smart, white." So according to statement 