sits the mouse (since lion visits mouse per 10) → by premise 18, the lion must like the cat.\n\nTherefore, the lion likes the cat. \n\nNow that we know the lion likes the cat, we can apply premise 17: "If something likes the cat then it needs the bear." Here, the lion likes the cat (as we just concluded), so the lion must need the bear. \n\nTherefore, the lion needs the bear. The question is whether the statement "The lion does not need the bear" is true. Since we just deduced that the lion does need the bear, the statement is false. Therefore, the answer should be B) False.\n\nWait, let me double-check each step to make sure I didn\'t make a mistake.\n\n1. Lion is green (statement 7). So by premise 14 (if green, likes mouse), lion likes mouse. That\'s okay.\n\n2. Lion visits bear (statement 8). By premise 16, visiting the bear → not nice. So lion is not nice. Correct.\n\n3. Lion visits cat (statement 9). Since lion is not nice (from step 2), and visits cat → by premise 19 (if visits cat and not nice → young), so lion is young. That\'s correct.\n\n4. Lion visits mouse (statement 10). Lion is young (from step 3) and visits mouse → premise 18 (young + visits mouse → likes cat). Therefore, lion likes cat. That\'s right.\n\n5. Now, since lion likes cat (from step 4), apply premise 17: if something likes cat → needs bear. Therefore, lion needs bear. So the statement "lion does not need the bear" is false. Therefore, the correct answer is B) False.\n\nI think that\'s solid. Let me see if there\'s any other angle or possible contradiction.\n\nAnother check: The question is asking about the lion needing the bear. Our deduction leads us to the conclusion that the lion does need the bear. Therefore, the statement "the lion does not need the bear" is false. So answer B.\n\nWait, but let me confirm if all the steps are indeed valid. Let\'s go through the premises again for each step:\n\nPremise 19: If something visits the cat and it is not nice, then it is young. So the lion vi