, the squirrel is kind (statement 14), so by statement 20, the squirrel must eat the rabbit. Wait, the squirrel is kind, so according to statement 20, the squirrel must eat the rabbit. But does the squirrel eat the rabbit? The given statements don\'t mention that. The squirrel is eaten by the bald eagle (statement 3). So, if the squirrel is kind, then it must eat the rabbit (statement 20). But that\'s another implication. \n\nWait, the squirrel being kind (statement 14) requires that it eats the rabbit (statement 20). So the squirrel must eat the rabbit. If the squirrel eats the rabbit, then according to statement 19: If something eats the rabbit, then the rabbit sees the lion. So the rabbit must see the lion. \n\nBut the rabbit\'s vision is stated as seeing the squirrel (statement 11), but not the lion. However, this implication would require the rabbit to also see the lion. Since the problem didn\'t mention that the rabbit doesn\'t see the lion, only that it sees the squirrel, it\'s possible that the rabbit does see the lion. So that might be okay. \n\nBut the squirrel eating the rabbit would also mean, via statement 16: If something (the squirrel) sees the lion, then it chases the rabbit. Wait, but the squirrel\'s action of seeing the lion would require it to chase the rabbit. However, if the squirrel is eating the rabbit, does that imply it\'s chasing it? Maybe, but the problem is whether the squirrel sees the lion. \n\nWait, the squirrel\'s vision is not mentioned except that it\'s cold and kind. So unless there\'s a statement that the squirrel sees the lion, we can\'t assume that. \n\nThis is getting a bit tangled. Let me try to outline the dependencies step by step.\n\nFirst, known facts:\n\n- Lion is kind (statement 6). Therefore, by statement 20, lion must eat the rabbit. \n\nSo conclusion 1: Lion eats the rabbit.\n\nIf the lion eats the rabbit, then by statement 19: the rabbit must see the lion. \n\nConclusion 2: Rabbit sees the lion. \n\nThe rabbit\'s vis