les to see if there\'s any other condition that might invalidate this.\n\nLooking at Rule7: "If something is cold and not nice, then it eats the rabbit." The rabbit is cold. Is the rabbit nice? The squirrel is nice (fact5), the tiger is nice (fact11), but the rabbit\'s niceness isn\'t stated. Since niceness is only mentioned for squirrel and tiger, the rabbit\'s niceness is unknown. Therefore, we can\'t assume it\'s nice or not. Therefore, Rule7 can\'t be applied to the rabbit because we don\'t know if it\'s "not nice".\n\nTherefore, there\'s no contradiction there.\n\nWait, but what about the bear? The bear is cold and not green (fact2 and 3). The bear is rough (fact3). Since the bear is cold, it must chase the rabbit (Rule4). So the bear chases the rabbit, which per Rule2 means the bear must eat the bear? Wait, the bear must eat the bear? That\'s possible in the logic here, but perhaps the problem allows that. However, the question is about the rabbit, not the bear. So the rabbit\'s action is the main focus.\n\nAnother point: The tiger is cold and nice (facts10 and 11). So the tiger is cold and nice, so Rule7 (cold and not nice → eats rabbit) doesn\'t apply to the tiger. The tiger chases the bear (fact8), and according to Rule2, since the tiger chases the rabbit (from Rule4 because it\'s cold), then the tiger must eat the bear. So the tiger must eat the bear as well. But the problem states "the tiger does not eat the rabbit" (fact9). But that\'s okay, because Rule2 requires the tiger to eat the bear, not the rabbit. So the tiger eating the bear is allowed, and fact9 only says the tiger doesn\'t eat the rabbit, which is separate.\n\nWait, but if the tiger eats the bear, then according to Rule9, the tiger must chase the squirrel. The facts say the tiger visits the squirrel (fact14). Does "visits" imply chasing? Not necessarily. Chasing is a different action. So the tiger visiting the squirrel doesn\'t mean it chases it. But according to Rule9, if the tiger eats the 