      "text": ":\nOkay, let's try to tackle this problem step by step. First, I need to understand all the given information and then see what conclusion we can draw about Gary's smartness.\n\nSo the context gives us several statements. Let me list them out one by one and see how they connect.\n\nStarting with the individual attributes:\n\n- Charlie is young.\n- Erin is blue. Erin is green.\n- Fiona is big. Fiona is green.\n- Gary is blue. Gary is green.\n\nThen there are the general rules:\n\n1. All red things are furry.\n2. All furry things are red.\n3. Young things are red.\n4. If something is smart and not red then it is not green.\n5. All furry things are green.\n6. If something is red and green then it is blue.\n7. If something is young and big then it is smart.\n8. If something is blue and green then it is smart.\n9. Blue, red things are smart. (Hmm, this might be a bit ambiguous. Maybe it means that things that are both blue and red are smart? Or perhaps it's saying that blue things and red things are smart? The wording is a bit unclear. Let me think. The original says \"Blue, red things are smart.\" Maybe it's a list: Blue things and red things are smart? Or maybe \"things that are both blue and red are smart\"? The comma might be separating adjectives. Alternatively, maybe it's saying \"All blue things that are red are smart\", but that would be redundant. Alternatively, perhaps it's a mistranslation or typo, but given the context, perhaps it's intended to mean that all blue things and all red things are smart. But I need to check the possible interpretations. Alternatively, maybe \"All blue red things are smart\" meaning something that is both blue and red. Hmm, this is a bit tricky. Let's see the rest of the rules. Maybe the context will help.)\n\nWait, let me process each rule again carefully:\n\n1. All red things are furry. So if something is red \u2192 furry.\n2. All furry things are red. So furry \u2192 red. So combining 1 and 2, red \u2194 furry. So they are equivalent. So anything red is furry, and anything furry is red. So red and furry are the same set.\n\n3. Young things are red. So young \u2192 red. Since red \u2194 furry, so young \u2192 red \u2192 furry. So young things are also furry.\n\n4. If something is smart and not red then it is not green. So (smart \u2227 \u00acred) \u2192 \u00acgreen. Contrapositive: If something is green, then either it's not smart or it is red. So Green \u2192 (\u00acsmart \u2228 red). Which also can be written as Green \u2227 smart \u2192 red.\n\n5. All furry things are green. But from rule 2, furry \u2192 red. So if furry \u2192 red, and furry also \u2192 green, then all red things (since red \u2194 furry) must also be green. Wait, but that's conflicting with some other rules? Let me see. Wait, rule 5 says All furry things are green. But since furry \u2194 red (from 1 and 2), so all red things are green. So red \u2192 furry \u2192 green. So red things are green? But then rule 6 says If something is red and green then it is blue. So if something is both red and green, then it must be blue. But since red things are green (from 5 and 1-2), then all red things are green, so they must also be blue? That seems conflicting. Wait, let's see:\n\nLet me try to track this step by step.\n\nFirst, from rules 1 and 2:\n\nRed \u2194 Furry. So anything red is furry, and vice versa.\n\nRule 3: Young \u2192 Red, so young things are red (and hence furry).\n\nRule 5: Furry \u2192 Green. Since furry \u2194 red, then red \u2192 Green. So all red things (which are furry) are green. So red things are green.\n\nNow, rule 6 says: If something is red and green, then it is blue. But since all red things are green (from rule 5 and red \u2194 furry), then all red things must also satisfy red \u2227 green \u2192 blue. Therefore, all red things must be blue. Wait, that's a problem. Let me see:\n\nSuppose an object is red. Then:\n\n- From red \u2194 furry, it's furry.\n- From rule 5, furry \u2192 green, so