here\'s any conflicting information. Let me go through the other rules to see if there\'s a contradiction.\n\nRule 10: All quiet, kind people are furry. Since Harry would be quiet and kind (from above), that\'s okay because he is already furry. So that\'s consistent.\n\nRule 11: If Harry is furry and quiet, then he is round. Since Harry is furry and quiet (as per above), this implies he must be round. But the problem doesn\'t mention roundness, so that\'s just an additional fact, not conflicting.\n\nRule 14: All quiet, furry people are round. Since Harry is quiet and furry, he must be round. Again, no conflict here.\n\nSo all the rules seem to hold if Harry is quiet. Therefore, the statement "Harry is not quiet" is false. Hence the answer is B (False).\n\nWait a second, but let me check again. Let me see if there\'s any rule that could prevent Harry from being quiet. \n\nThe problem states "All quiet, kind people are furry" (rule 10). Since Harry is kind and quiet (from the above steps), he must be furry, which he is. So that\'s okay.\n\nAnother thing: Rule 12 says rough and kind people are quiet. Harry is both rough and kind, so that requires him to be quiet. So that\'s enforced. Therefore, Harry must be quiet, so the statement that he is not quiet is false. So the answer is B.\n\nHmm, but wait, let me check if there\'s any other rule that might have an exception. For example, is there a possibility that Harry can\'t be both quiet and something else? Let me see.\n\nLooking at Rule 14: All quiet, furry people are round. Since Harry is quiet and furry, he must be round. But the problem doesn\'t mention anything about roundness conflicting, so that\'s okay.\n\nAlso, the only people mentioned with specific traits are Anne (quiet), Bob (not rough), Gary (big, kind, rough, young), and Harry (young). The rules don\'t restrict quietness except through the other conditions.\n\nTherefore, based on the given information, Harry must be quiet. Hence the statement "Harry is not 