ot be green. \n\nWait, let me think again. \n\nThe conditionals are statements like \"if X then Y\", but they don't say that Y can only happen if X. So the mouse could be green for some other reason not covered by the conditionals. But in logic, unless stated otherwise, we can't assume that the given conditionals are the only ways something can happen. \n\nHowever, in typical logical reasoning problems like this, especially in exams, the conditionals are usually the only rules, so you can assume that the only way to be green is through the given conditionals. \n\nTherefore, since the mouse can't meet the conditions for being green (G requires round and eats dog; the mouse isn't round and doesn't eat the dog), there's no way for the mouse to be green. \n\nHence, the mouse is not green. \n\nTherefore, the statement \"the mouse is not green\" is true. \n\nWait, that changes things. \n\nLet me clarify this point again. \n\nThe problem's conditionals are all the rules provided. To determine if the mouse is green, we have to see if any of the conditionals' antecedents are met that would necessitate the mouse being green. \n\nThe only conditional that leads to someone being green is G: If someone is round and eats the dog, then they are green. \n\nThe mouse is not round (the dog is), and does the mouse eat the dog? \n\nLooking at the facts: \n\nThe cow eats the dog (fact1), the dog eats the cow (fact7). The mouse's actions: the mouse is eaten by the cow (fact2), but there's no mention of the mouse eating the dog. \n\nTherefore, the mouse doesn't eat the dog. \n\nThus, the mouse cannot satisfy the antecedent of G, so there's no conditional that requires the mouse to be green. \n\nTherefore, the mouse is not green. \n\nTherefore, the statement \"the mouse is not green\" is true (A). \n\nWait, but earlier I thought the mouse must be blue, but that's okay. \n\nSo the answer is A) True. \n\nBut let me confirm once more. \n\nBecause the only way to be green is via conditional G.