f it\'s possible for the mouse to be green (without contradiction), then the statement is not necessarily true, so the answer would be unknown. \n\nAlternatively, if being blue makes it impossible to be green, but that\'s an assumption. Since the problem doesn\'t state that colors are exclusive, we can\'t assume that. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s another rule. Let\'s check all the conditionals again. \n\nLooking at conditional G: If someone is round and eats the dog, then they are green. \n\nThe dog is round and eats the dog? Wait, the dog eats the cow, not the dog. So the dog doesn\'t eat the dog, so that condition doesn\'t apply. \n\nAlternatively, if the dog had to eat the dog (due to E), then maybe the dog would be green. But that\'s a separate issue. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the mouse can\'t be green because of some other reason. \n\nWait, let\'s see if the mouse being green would require anything else. \n\nIf the mouse is green, then it must like the tiger (B), and the tiger must see the cow (C). \n\nBut there\'s no other constraints. \n\nSo, the mouse could be green, provided that the tiger sees the cow. Since the problem doesn\'t say the tiger doesn\'t see the cow, that\'s possible. \n\nTherefore, the mouse could be green, so the statement "the mouse is not green" could be false. But we also have that the mouse must be blue (from D). \n\nBut the problem doesn\'t say that being blue precludes being green. So maybe the mouse is both, making the statement "the mouse is not green" possibly false. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a contradiction I\'m missing. \n\nWait, let\'s see another angle. \n\nThe mouse is blue (from D), but to be green, it would have to satisfy some condition. But there\'s no condition that says being blue affects being green unless stated. \n\nTherefore, the mouse\'s color could be both, or just blue. \n\nBut the question is about whether it\'s not green. Since we can\'t prove that it isn\'t green (because there\'s a possible scenari