agle. Therefore, the rabbit must like the bald eagle.\n\nWait, so the rabbit likes the bald eagle. But is that stated anywhere else? Let me check the original statements. The original statements mention that the bald eagle likes the cat (statement 1), the cow likes the bald eagle (statement 6) and the cow likes the rabbit (statement 7). The rabbit\'s likes are not mentioned except through this implication. So the rabbit must like the bald eagle because it needs the cat. So that\'s an inferred fact.\n\nNow, let\'s see if any of these lead to the rabbit visiting the cat.\n\nThe question is: Does the rabbit visit the cat? So we need to find any rule that connects the rabbit\'s actions to visiting the cat, or any conditions that might lead to that.\n\nLooking at the statements about visiting:\n\n- Bald eagle visits the rabbit (statement 2)\n- Cat visits the rabbit (statement 5)\n- Cow visits the bald eagle (statement 8)\n- Statement 12: If someone needs the bald eagle and they are red, then they visit the cow. The rabbit needs the bald eagle and is red (since it\'s green and red, but also via statement 15, being red is required because they need the bald eagle). Therefore, the rabbit would satisfy the antecedent of statement 12. So the rabbit would need to visit the cow. But does that mean the rabbit visits the cow? Wait, according to statement 12, if someone (in this case, the rabbit) needs the bald eagle and is red, then they visit the cow. Since the rabbit meets both conditions, that would mean the rabbit must visit the cow. But does that affect whether the rabbit visits the cat?\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s another way. Let\'s see:\n\nLooking for any rules about visiting the cat. Let me see:\n\nLooking through all the statements, the visiting actions mentioned are:\n\n- Bald eagle visits rabbit (2)\n- Cat visits rabbit (5)\n- Cow visits bald eagle (8)\n- Statement 17: If someone likes the bald eagle and needs the bald eagle, then the bald eagle visits the rabbit