gle (statement 3), so the cat is nice. Then statement 12 says if something is nice, it\'s big. So the cat is big. Not sure if that helps with the bald eagle visiting itself.\n\nStatement 16: If something visits the cat, then it visits the rabbit. The rabbit visits the cat (statement 10), so the rabbit must visit the rabbit? Wait, statement 10 says the rabbit visits the cat. So applying statement 16 to the rabbit: since the rabbit visits the cat, then it must visit the rabbit. That would mean the rabbit visits itself. But does that create a loop? Hmm, but the question is about the bald eagle. Maybe not directly relevant here.\n\nStatement 17: If something visits the rabbit and sees the cow, then it visits the cow. The rabbit visits the cat (statement 10), and the rabbit sees the cow (statement 9). Wait, so the rabbit visits the cat, which via statement 16 requires visiting the rabbit, but that\'s a bit of a loop. Also, the rabbit does see the cow (statement 9), so if the rabbit visits the rabbit (from visiting the cat via statement 16), then since it visits the rabbit (itself) and sees the cow, does that mean it must visit the cow? Not sure how that plays out. Maybe the rabbit would have to visit the cow again, but I\'m getting a bit tangled here.\n\nBack to the main question: the bald eagle\'s visits. Since none of the direct statements mention the bald eagle visiting anyone, including itself, maybe we need to see if any of the conditionals could force the bald eagle to visit itself or prevent it.\n\nLet me think of possible scenarios. Suppose the bald eagle were to visit itself. Is there any rule that would make that necessary? Let\'s see:\n\nSuppose the bald eagle visits the cow. Then, if it also sees the cow (statement 11\'s condition), it would have to visit the bald eagle. Wait, but if the bald eagle visited the cow and saw the cow, then according to statement 11, it must visit the bald eagle. But that would create a chain: visiting the cow and seeing the cow →