one is blue and rough, then they are smart. So, blue ∧ rough → smart.\n\nRule 4: All nice, rough people are smart. So, nice ∧ rough → smart. Hmm, but rule 2 already says rough → smart, so maybe this is redundant? Not sure yet.\n\nRule 5: If someone is nice and round, then they are rough. So, nice ∧ round → rough.\n\nRule 6: White, smart people are blue. So, if someone is both white and smart, then they are blue. So, white ∧ smart → blue.\n\nRule 7: All blue people are nice. So, blue → nice. This might be important because Fiona is blue, so she must be nice, which she is listed as.\n\nRule 8: Nice people are round. Wait, that\'s a general statement? So, if someone is nice, then they are round. Wait, but Erin is nice and round, which fits. But Fiona is nice but we don\'t know if she\'s round yet. Wait, the question is about Fiona not being round. Let me check.\n\nWait, rule 8: "Nice people are round." So, that would mean that if someone is nice, then they must be round. So, all nice people are round. Therefore, Fiona is nice (given in her attributes), so according to rule 8, she must be round. But the question is whether Fiona is not round. So if the rule says nice implies round, then Fiona must be round. Therefore the statement "Fiona is not round" would be false. So the answer would be B) False?\n\nBut wait, let me check again because sometimes there might be exceptions or conflicting rules. Let me go step by step.\n\nFirst, Fiona\'s known attributes: Blue, Nice, Smart. From her given data.\n\nFrom rule 7: All blue people are nice. That\'s consistent because Fiona is nice.\n\nRule 8: All nice people are round. Therefore, since Fiona is nice, she must be round. So Fiona is round, so the statement "Fiona is not round" would be false. So the answer would be B) False.\n\nBut wait, let me check if there\'s any reason to think Fiona might not be round. Maybe there\'s a rule that could override this?\n\nWait, let\'s check if there are any other rules that could affect this