abbit. But for chasing the bear, there's no such implication stated. So the only entity mentioned that might see the bear is maybe the rabbit? Not sure yet. Let me check the statements again.\n\nLooking at statement19: \"If the rabbit is not young and the rabbit does not need the bald eagle then the rabbit sees the bear.\" But the rabbit needs the bald eagle (statement9), so the antecedent here is \"if rabbit is not young AND does not need...\" which is false because it does need the bald eagle. So that implication's conclusion doesn't get triggered.\n\nSo perhaps no one else sees the bear except maybe the bald eagle? Wait, no. The bald eagle sees the rabbit (statement6), but not the bear. The bear is seeing the bald eagle. So maybe no one else sees the bear. Therefore, statement14's condition (something sees the bear) isn't met by any entity except maybe the rabbit? Not sure. Let's move on and see.\n\nLooking at statement15: \"If something needs the bald eagle then the bald eagle chases the rabbit.\" The rabbit needs the bald eagle (statement9), so that means the bald eagle must chase the rabbit. Which is already stated in statement1. So that's okay.\n\nStatement16: \"If something is big and it chases the rabbit then it sees the bear.\" The bald eagle chases the rabbit (statement1), so if the bald eagle is big, then it must see the bear. But does the bald eagle see the bear? The bald eagle sees the rabbit (statement6), but there's no mention of it seeing the bear. Unless there's a rule that implies it. Let me check.\n\nStatement18 says: \"If something chases the rabbit then it sees the bald eagle.\" Wait, that's for the chaser. Wait, the bald eagle chases the rabbit (statement1). So applying statement18 to the bald eagle: since it chases the rabbit, it must see the bald eagle. Wait, that would mean the bald eagle sees itself? Because the subject is \"something chases the rabbit\", so the \"it\" in \"it sees the bald eagle\" refers to the chaser (the bald eagle). So