n that entity sees the bald eagle.\n\n- If the lion sees the bald eagle then the bald eagle visits the tiger. Since the lion does see the bald eagle (statement 8), then the bald eagle must visit the tiger. Wait, so according to that rule, because the lion sees the bald eagle, the bald eagle must visit the tiger. But in the given context, is that stated? The context says "the bald eagle visits the tiger" only if the lion sees it. Since the lion does see the bald eagle, then the bald eagle must visit the tiger. So that\'s an inference we can make. So bald eagle visits the tiger.\n\n- If something is young then it visits the lion. So any young entity must visit the lion.\n\nNow, the question is: Does the bald eagle chase the cat? So we need to see if there\'s any rule that would make the bald eagle chase the cat, or any contradictions, or if it\'s unknown.\n\nFirst, check if the bald eagle has any actions mentioned. The bald eagle is mentioned as being visited by the cat (statement 5?), but the bald eagle\'s actions: it\'s stated that the bald eagle is green and red (maybe conflicting), but also, from the last rule, since the lion sees the bald eagle, then the bald eagle must visit the tiger. So bald eagle does visit the tiger. But does it chase the cat?\n\nLooking at the first conditional: If something sees the lion and the bald eagle, then it chases the cat. So, if an entity sees both the lion and the bald eagle, it must chase the cat. \n\nDoes the bald eagle see the lion? The context doesn\'t say that the bald eagle sees the lion. The lion sees the bald eagle (statement 8), but that\'s from the lion\'s perspective. The bald eagle\'s perspective isn\'t stated unless inferred. So unless there\'s a rule that if X sees Y, then Y sees X, but that\'s not stated here. So the bald eagle\'s perceptions aren\'t given except maybe through other rules?\n\nWait, let\'s see if there\'s any way the bald eagle could see the lion. For example, if the bald eagle visited the lion, but