r statement 2. But is there any contradiction here?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the squirrel doesn't see the mouse, so if it were to chase the bald eagle, that would violate statement 2, so that can't be possible. Therefore, maybe the conclusion is conflicting?\n\nWait, this is a bit confusing. Let me retrace:\n\nIf the squirrel chases the bald eagle (as per statements 1 and 6), then according to statement 2, the squirrel must see the mouse. But does the squirrel see the mouse?\n\nLooking at the given facts, the only mentions of who sees whom are:\n\n- Bald eagle sees squirrel, not the dog.\n\n- Mouse sees bald eagle and dog.\n\n- The squirrel's vision isn't directly mentioned except maybe through the conditional statements.\n\nThe squirrel's seeing of the mouse isn't stated. So if the squirrel were to chase the bald eagle, that would require the squirrel to see the mouse (from statement 2). But unless there's a fact that the squirrel does see the mouse, then this could be a problem. \n\nWait, but maybe there's another way the squirrel could see the mouse. Let me check other statements. \n\nLooking at statement 8: If something sees the mouse and the mouse needs the dog, then the dog chases the bald eagle. The mouse does need the dog (from the mouse's needs), so if something sees the mouse and the mouse needs the dog (which it does), then the dog chases the bald eagle. But the dog chasing the bald eagle isn't mentioned in the context. However, this is a conditional, so if the antecedent is true, then the consequent must be true. \n\nWait, but does the squirrel see the mouse? Let's think. The only entities that are stated to see the mouse are the bald eagle (no, the bald eagle sees the squirrel and not the dog, but not the mouse). The mouse sees the bald eagle and the dog. The squirrel's vision isn't directly stated. Unless another rule implies it. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the squirrel must see the mouse if it's chasing the bald eagle. But since the squirrel's vision is