hey are white. Bob is big but we don\'t know if he\'s smart. Since he\'s quiet, if he were red, he would be smart (from rule 2). But he\'s not necessarily red. So unless Bob is red and quiet, he wouldn\'t have to be smart. Since his color isn\'t given, maybe he\'s not red, so he doesn\'t have to be smart. Therefore, the first rule doesn\'t apply to him. So that doesn\'t affect his roundness.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a way that Bob can\'t be round because of some other condition. Let me see.\n\nRule 4: Red, round people are quiet. But that\'s a one-way implication. So if someone is red and round, then they\'re quiet. But Bob is already quiet, so if he were red and round, that\'s okay. But since his color isn\'t known, maybe that\'s not an issue. However, the reverse isn\'t necessarily true. Just because someone is quiet doesn\'t mean they are red and round.\n\nAnother point: The sixth rule says if Erin is round and quiet, then she is smart. But Erin\'s attributes are given as white. The question isn\'t about Erin, but maybe that\'s just extra info.\n\nWait, let me think again. The fifth rule is a direct implication: big → round. Since Bob is big, he must be round. So unless there\'s a rule that says big and something else makes him not round, but there\'s no such rule. All the other rules are about other attributes. So unless there\'s a contradiction, Bob must be round. Therefore, the answer should be A) True.\n\nBut wait, maybe I\'m missing something. Let me check each rule again carefully.\n\nRule 5: "If someone is big then they are round." So big → round. So Bob is big → must be round. So unless there is an exception, but none is stated. Therefore, the conclusion is Bob is round, so answer A.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there is a hidden contradiction. Let me see.\n\nWait, what if Bob is big and round, but then according to some other rule, he can\'t be? Let me check other rules that involve round.\n\nRule 4: Red and round → quiet. But Bob is already quiet, 