e squirrel\'s properties.\n\nFirst, let\'s note all the direct properties:\n\nBear: cold, rough, eats squirrel, visits lion.\n\nLion: visited by bear, visited by squirrel, likes cat, is eaten by cat and squirrel.\n\nSquirrel: cold, rough, eats lion, likes bear, visits lion.\n\nCat: eats lion, liked by lion.\n\nNow, looking at the conditionals:\n\nStarting with the first conditional: If someone eats the lion, then the lion is red. \n\nLooking at who eats the lion: the squirrel (point 8) and the cat (point 5). So both squirrel and cat eat the lion. Therefore, according to this rule, the lion must be red. So that means the lion is red. \n\nWait, so the lion is red because both the squirrel and the cat eat it. So the lion is red. \n\nNext, another conditional: If someone is green and they like the lion, then they eat the bear. \n\nHmm, so if an entity is green and likes the lion, then they must eat the bear. Let\'s see if any entities fit that. \n\nThe squirrel likes the bear (point 11) but does it like the lion? Let\'s check. The squirrel\'s likes: only mentions liking the bear. The lion likes the cat (point 6). The bear\'s likes? The context doesn\'t say the bear likes anything except maybe through the rules. The cat\'s likes? Not mentioned. \n\nSo maybe the squirrel doesn\'t like the lion, so that rule might not apply. \n\nNext conditional: If someone visits the bear, then the bear likes the lion. \n\nWho visits the bear? The lion does (point 7). So since the lion visits the bear, then according to this rule, the bear must like the lion. But does the bear like the lion? The context doesn\'t explicitly say that. Wait, but the rule says that if someone (in this case, the lion) visits the bear, then the bear likes the lion. Therefore, the bear must like the lion as a result of this. So that\'s an inferred fact: bear likes lion. \n\nAnother conditional: If someone likes the squirrel and they like the lion, then they visit the lion. \n\nWho likes the squirrel? The context