om statements 1 and 2, we know Anne is kind and round. Now, let\'s see if any of the other statements can help us connect these attributes to "cold".\n\nStarting with Anne\'s kindness. Statement 14 says "Kind people are green." Since Anne is kind, that would mean she must be green. Wait, but the problem doesn\'t explicitly state that Anne is green, but according to this rule, if she\'s kind, she must be. So that\'s an inference we can make. So Anne is green.\n\nNow, statement 12 says "All green people are blue." So if Anne is green, then she must also be blue. Therefore, Anne is blue.\n\nNow, looking at statement 17: "All blue, kind people are quiet." Since Anne is both blue (from above) and kind (given), she must be quiet. So Anne is quiet.\n\nNow, let\'s see if we can get to cold. The question is about Anne being cold. Let\'s see if any of the rules can link her attributes to being cold.\n\nStatement 13: "Quiet, round people are cold." Anne is quiet (from above) and round (given). So since she\'s both quiet and round, according to statement 13, she should be cold. That would mean the answer is A) True. But wait, let me check all the other rules to make sure there\'s no contradiction or something I missed.\n\nWait, but let me double-check the chain of reasoning again. Let me go step by step again to confirm.\n\n1. Anne is kind (given). So by statement 14, she must be green. So Anne is green.\n2. Since she\'s green, by statement 12, she must be blue. So Anne is blue.\n3. She\'s blue and kind (given), so by statement 17, she must be quiet. So Anne is quiet.\n4. She\'s quiet (from step 3) and round (given), so by statement 13, she must be cold. \n\nSo that would mean Anne is indeed cold, so the statement "Anne is cold" is true. So the answer would be A) True. But wait, let me check if there\'s any other constraints or possible contradictions.\n\nWait, another thought: statement 16 says "If someone is cold then they are furry." So if Anne is cold, she must be furry. Bu