he question is whether the mouse needs the lion. The only way to derive that would be through a rule that connects liking to needing, but there\'s no such rule. The only rules that mention needing are points 6,9,10,12,13,14. \n\nWait, point 13 says if someone likes the lion and needs the lion, then the lion visits the mouse. But that\'s about the lion\'s action, not the mouse\'s need. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the lion needs the mouse? But that\'s not stated. \n\nAlternatively, the mouse\'s need for the lion might be inferred through another chain. Let\'s see:\n\nThe cow is rough (point 5), so by point 12, the cow needs the lion. So the cow needs the lion. Then, since the cow needs the lion (point 12), by point 10, the cow must visit the lion. Then, since the cow visits the lion, by point 8, the cow must be round. But the context doesn\'t state the cow is round, but according to the logic, that must be true. So the cow is now inferred to be round. \n\nNow, does that help with the mouse? Not directly. \n\nAlternatively, since the mouse likes the lion, is there any condition that would require it to need the lion? Let me check all the conditions again for any possible path. \n\nLooking at point 15: If someone likes the lion and the lion is big, then they are kind. The lion\'s size isn\'t mentioned, so we can\'t use this. \n\nAlternatively, the lion is big? The cow is big (point 4), but the lion\'s size isn\'t stated. \n\nHmm. \n\nWait, another thought: The bear visits the lion (point 3). Since the bear visits the lion, then by point 8, the bear is round, which is already stated. Also, if the bear visits the lion and the lion is round (but we don\'t know if the lion is round), then by point 11, the bear would like the lion. But the bear likes the cow (point 2), not the lion. So unless there\'s a contradiction here, but since we don\'t know if the lion is round, point 11 isn\'t triggered. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps the lion is round because the bear is round? No, that\'s