w does that affect the squirrel needing the tiger?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the squirrel itself likes the squirrel (but that\'s not stated). Hmm, this might not be the right path.\n\nAnother approach: Let\'s see if there\'s any way the squirrel must need the tiger. Let\'s suppose that the squirrel doesn\'t need the tiger. Is that possible without violating any conditions?\n\nSuppose the squirrel does NOT need the tiger. Then, let\'s see if that leads to any contradictions.\n\nIf the squirrel does not need the tiger, then looking at the first conditional, the premise of that conditional requires that something chases the squirrel. Since the squirrel isn\'t being chased, that\'s okay. \n\nBut are there other conditions that would be triggered?\n\nLooking at the second conditional: If something needs the tiger, then it likes the rabbit. If the squirrel did need the tiger (which we are considering the opposite here), then the squirrel would have to like the rabbit. But if the squirrel doesn\'t need the tiger, this condition doesn\'t apply to the squirrel.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the squirrel\'s need for the tiger can be inferred through another path. Let me think again.\n\nWait, the tiger chases the cat (point 10). The first conditional says if something chases the squirrel then the squirrel needs the tiger. But since the tiger chases the cat, not the squirrel, that doesn\'t trigger the first conditional. So the squirrel isn\'t required to need the tiger unless something else is chasing it.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the squirrel is being chased by someone else? But there\'s no mention of that in the given facts. So I think the first conditional\'s premise isn\'t met.\n\nNow, looking at the fourth conditional: since the tiger chases the rabbit, the rabbit does not need the tiger. So the rabbit\'s need for the tiger is excluded. The squirrel needs the rabbit (point 9), but that\'s separate.\n\nThe question is whether the squirrel needs the tiger. The direct statements don\