ikes the lion (statement 3) and visits the dog (statement 4). There\'s no mention of the bald eagle eating anything. The cat\'s actions: it\'s stated that the cat visits the dog (10), but does the cat eat the lion? Let\'s see. From statement 20, if something (the dog) eats the cat and the cat eats the dog, then the cat eats the lion. But the cat doesn\'t eat the dog, so that\'s not applicable. So the cat\'s eating habits aren\'t stated except that the dog eats the cat.\n\nTherefore, the only entity that eats the lion is the dog. \n\nSo, does any entity both visit the dog and eat the lion? \n\nThe dog eats the lion but does not visit the dog. The bald eagle visits the dog but doesn\'t eat the lion. The cat visits the dog but doesn\'t eat the lion. So the only one eating the lion is the dog, who doesn\'t visit the dog. The other visitors (bald eagle and cat) don\'t eat the lion. Therefore, there is no entity that both visits the dog and eats the lion. Therefore, the antecedent of statement 21 is false, so the implication holds, but we can\'t conclude anything about the lion\'s roundness from this.\n\nWait, but wait a second. Wait, the dog eats the lion (from statement 16), but does the dog visit the dog? The problem says "something visits the dog and it eats the lion". The "it" refers to the same something. So the something must be an entity that both visits the dog and eats the lion. Since the dog eats the lion but doesn\'t visit itself, that\'s not it. The bald eagle visits the dog but doesn\'t eat the lion. The cat visits the dog but doesn\'t eat the lion. So there\'s no such entity. Therefore, the antecedent is false, so the implication is true, but the conclusion (lion is round) is not necessarily true or false. Therefore, statement 21 doesn\'t give us any information about the lion\'s roundness.\n\nHmm, okay. Let\'s try to see other paths. \n\nWe need to determine if the lion is round. The lion\'s attributes given are blue (statement 14). The round things are the bald eagle, the cat, and by statement 19, since round things are big, so both are big. The dog is also big (statement 12), but not necessarily round unless stated. \n\nIs there any other way to get the lion\'s roundness? Let\'s see.\n\nLooking at statement 21 again: If something visits the dog and it (the same something) eats the lion, then the lion is round. Since we established that no such something exists, then the statement doesn\'t help us. \n\nIs there another conditional that can lead us to the lion\'s roundness?\n\nLooking at the other conditionals:\n\nStatement 17: If bald eagle visits lion, then lion is nice. But bald eagle doesn\'t visit the lion, so no conclusion.\n\nStatement 16: Big things eat the lion. Since the dog is big, it eats the lion. But that doesn\'t relate to roundness.\n\nWait, perhaps the lion\'s roundness is derived through some other path. Let me think again.\n\nWait, is there a way that the lion could be round? Let\'s see. Round things are big (statement 19). So if the lion is round, then it must be big. But is the lion big? The dog is big (statement 12). The bald eagle and cat are also big because they are round (statement 19). The lion\'s size isn\'t stated. Unless we can infer it\'s big through another way, but I don\'t see any statement that says the lion is big. \n\nAlternatively, maybe through some other implication. Let me check statement 20 again. The antecedent requires that something eats the cat and the cat eats the dog. Since the cat doesn\'t eat the dog, that path is closed. \n\nWait, maybe through statement 15? The cat is nice and green, so it must visit the bald eagle. Therefore, the cat visits the bald eagle. So that\'s an implicit fact. So the cat\'s visits are both to the dog and the bald eagle. Therefore, the cat visits two places. \n\nBut does that affect anything? The bald eagle\'s visits are only to the dog. \n\nAnother angle: Let\'s check if the lion being round is possible. Suppose the lion is round. Then by s