he question is whether Fiona is not cold. So is Fiona cold? If we can prove Fiona is cold, then the statement \"Fiona is not cold\" is false (so answer B). If Fiona is definitely not cold, then answer A. If we can't determine, then C.\n\nFirst, let's note Fiona's attributes: Fiona is big and round. From the given info, Fiona is big and round. \n\nFrom rule 7: All big people are kind. Since Fiona is big, she must be kind. So Fiona is kind.\n\nNow, Fiona is kind and round (since she's round). Wait, rule 6 says all kind, round people are green. So Fiona is kind (from being big) and round, so she must be green. So Fiona is green.\n\nNow, rule 3 says green people are young. Therefore, Fiona must be young.\n\nAdditionally, rule 8 says young people are cold. So since Fiona is young, she must be cold.\n\nWait, that seems to form a chain:\n\nFiona is big \u2192 Fiona is kind (rule 7).\n\nFiona is kind and round \u2192 Fiona is green (rule 6).\n\nFiona is green \u2192 Fiona is young (rule 3).\n\nFiona is young \u2192 Fiona is cold (rule 8).\n\nSo Fiona must be cold. Therefore, the statement \"Fiona is not cold\" would be false. Therefore the answer should be B) False.\n\nBut let me check for any contradictions or possible exceptions.\n\nWait, but let's verify each step again.\n\nFiona is big (given). So by rule 7 (all big people are kind), Fiona must be kind. That's solid.\n\nFiona is kind (from above) and round (given). So kind + round \u2192 must be green (rule 6). So Fiona is green.\n\nGreen \u2192 young (rule 3). So Fiona is young.\n\nYoung \u2192 cold (rule 8). Therefore Fiona is cold.\n\nTherefore, Fiona must be cold. Hence the statement \"Fiona is not cold\" is false. So the answer is B.\n\nWait, but let me check if there's any other rule that might interfere. For instance, rule 5 says if someone is cold, then they are big. But Fiona is already big, so that doesn't conflict. Since Fiona is cold (from the conclusion), then she must be big, which she is. So that's okay.\