 cold. Wait, but let\'s see other rules.\n\nStatement 7: Harry is round and nice. So Harry is cold because of statement 9 (since nice implies cold). So Harry is cold.\n\nStatement 14: If something is round and cold then it is blue. Harry is round and cold (since he\'s nice, so cold), so that implies he\'s blue. Wait, but the original info says Harry is nice and round, but does it say Harry is blue? The problem doesn\'t, but according to this rule, he must be blue. But the context didn\'t mention Harry\'s color. So maybe that\'s a hidden fact.\n\nBut back to Charlie. Let\'s see if Charlie is cold. If Charlie were cold, then from statement 12 (blue and cold implies nice), Charlie would have to be nice, but we don\'t have that info. Since the context doesn\'t state Charlie is nice, perhaps we can\'t assume that. So maybe Charlie isn\'t cold. Let\'s see another way.\n\nStatement 14: If Charlie is round and cold, then he must be blue. But he\'s already blue, so that doesn\'t conflict, but doesn\'t tell us about coldness directly.\n\nWait, since Charlie is round and blue, and blue things are big (statement 15), so Charlie is big. Now, big, cold things are rough (statement 10). But we need to know if Charlie is cold to apply that.\n\nAlternatively, maybe Charlie isn\'t cold, so he isn\'t rough. Let\'s see.\n\nNow, to see if Charlie is red. Let\'s see the rules that connect redness.\n\nRule 8: All rough, blue things are red. So if Charlie is rough and blue, he must be red. But we need to see if Charlie is rough. To be rough, he would need to be big and cold (from statement 10). Since he\'s big (from being blue), if he\'s cold, then he\'s rough, which would make him red via rule 8.\n\nAlternatively, if Charlie is not cold, then he\'s not rough, so rule 8 doesn\'t apply, so he might not be red.\n\nBut how do we determine if Charlie is cold?\n\nCharlie\'s attributes: blue, round, big.\n\nLooking at statement 12: All blue, cold things are nice. So if Charlie were cold, he would