statement 12), then tiger must see the mouse. But does the tiger see the mouse? The premises don\'t say that directly, but according to this rule, since the tiger likes the mouse, it must see the mouse. So that\'s another inference: tiger sees mouse.\n\nSimilarly, the mouse likes the tiger (statement 4). Since the mouse likes the tiger, does that lead to anything? Maybe not directly, unless there\'s a rule involving that.\n\nRule 13: If something sees the mouse and likes the mouse, then it sees the bear. Let\'s see if any entity meets that. The tiger sees the mouse (from above inference), and the tiger likes the mouse (statement 12). So the tiger would satisfy the antecedent: tiger sees mouse and likes mouse → tiger sees bear. Therefore, we can conclude that the tiger sees the bear.\n\nSo that\'s another derived fact: tiger sees bear.\n\nNow, going back to rule 20: If something is round and it sees the bear, then the bear likes the mouse. The rabbit is round (statement 8). If the rabbit sees the bear, then the bear likes the mouse. Wait, does the rabbit see the bear? The premises say the bear sees the rabbit (statement 3), but that doesn\'t mean the rabbit sees the bear. Unless there\'s a rule that if A sees B then B sees A? No, that\'s not stated. So unless there\'s another fact, the rabbit might not see the bear. So unless we can infer that the rabbit sees the bear, then rule 20 can\'t be applied here.\n\nAlternatively, maybe another entity is round and sees the bear. The only round thing mentioned is the rabbit. So unless the rabbit sees the bear, we can\'t apply rule 20. Since the bear sees the rabbit (statement 3), but that\'s a one-way. So the rabbit\'s seeing the bear is unknown unless there\'s a rule that allows it.\n\nHmm, so maybe the rabbit doesn\'t see the bear, so rule 20 isn\'t triggered. Let\'s hold that thought.\n\nNow, looking for any statements that could lead to the bear liking the tiger. Let\'s see if any of the rules can be applied.\n\nLooking a