t see any. The only thing that could contradict is if another condition says the mouse is not big, but there's none. \n\nTherefore, the conclusion is that the mouse must be big, so the statement is false. Therefore, the answer is B. \n\nWait, but let me check again all the conditions step by step to be thorough. \n\n1. The mouse is green. So it must visit the dog (5th condition). So mouse\u2192visits dog.\n\n2. The mouse eats the tiger (given), so the tiger must visit the mouse (second condition). So tiger\u2192visits mouse.\n\n3. Since the tiger is visiting the mouse, then per first condition, the mouse must be big. So mouse is big.\n\n4. Since the mouse is big (step 3), then per last condition (if big then visit rabbit), mouse\u2192visits rabbit.\n\n5. Since mouse visits rabbit, and rabbit is young (given), then by 6th condition, the visitor (mouse) must be round. So mouse is round.\n\n6. Since mouse is round and it visits dog (from step1), then per 7th condition, it must chase the tiger. So mouse\u2192chases tiger.\n\nIs there any contradiction here? Let's see. The original statements say \"The rabbit does not chase the tiger,\" which is about the rabbit, not the mouse. So that's okay. The problem didn't mention anything about the mouse chasing the tiger, so that's acceptable. \n\nTherefore, all conditions are satisfied when the mouse is big, so there's no contradiction. Therefore, the mouse must be big, so the statement \"The mouse is not big\" is false. So the answer is B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe mouse eats the tiger (given), so by the conditional \"If something eats the tiger, then the tiger visits the mouse,\" the tiger must visit the mouse. Since the tiger visits the mouse, the first condition (\"If something visits the mouse, then the mouse is big\") applies, meaning the mouse **must be big**. Therefore, the statement \"The mouse is not big\" is **false**.\n\n**Answer:** B) False",
