e it. Hence, the answer must be unknown. Therefore, I think the correct answer is C.\n</think>\n\nC) Unknown\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Premises Analysis:**\n   - The bear chases the cow (chase relation).\n   - The bear sees the tiger (directional \"sees\").\n   - The cow chases the bear.\n   - The mouse chases the tiger.\n   - The tiger needs the mouse (direct statement).\n   - Key conditional statements include:\n     - If someone chases the cow, they are big (Statement 10).\n     - If someone is big, they need the tiger (Statement 12).\n     - If someone needs the tiger, the tiger must see the bear (Statement 11).\n     - If the tiger sees the bear and needs the mouse, it is big (Statement 13).\n\n2. **Deriving the Tiger Sees the Bear:**\n   - The bear chases the cow \u2192 bear is big (Statement 10).\n   - Being big (bear) \u2192 must need the tiger (Statement 12).\n   - If someone (bear) needs the tiger \u2192 tiger must see the bear (Statement 11).\n   - Thus, the tiger sees the bear (established fact).\n\n3. **Determining the Tiger\u2019s Size:**\n   - Tiger sees the bear (from above) and needs the mouse (given).\n   - Apply Statement 13: Tiger is big (since both conditions are met).\n\n4. **Tiger\u2019s Dependency on Itself:**\n   - Tiger is big \u2192 must need the tiger (via Statement 12). This is a self-referential but non-contradictory fact.\n\n5. **Evaluating the Cow\u2019s Need for the Tiger:**\n   - No direct statements link the cow needing the tiger.\n   - The cow\u2019s size is unknown: Chasing the bear doesn\u2019t trigger Statement 10 for the cow.\n   - If the cow were big, it would need the tiger (Statement 12), but its size is unproven.\n   - No contradictions arise if the cow does or does not need the tiger, as no premises enforce this.\n\n**Conclusion:** Since there\u2019s insufficient information to confirm or deny the cow needing the tiger, the statement is **unknown**. \n\n**Answer:** C) Unknown",
