Let\'s see:\n\nStatement 10: If someone chases the cow, then they are big. The bear chases the cow (statement 1). So, since the bear chases the cow, then according to statement 10, the bear must be big. Therefore, the bear is big. Then, by statement 12, since the bear is big, the bear must need the tiger. Wait, so the bear needs the tiger? But the question is about the cow needing the tiger. Hmm, but maybe this is a side track.\n\nLet\'s see what else. The cow chases the bear (statement 4). But chasing here might not directly relate to needing. Let\'s look for any statements that involve the cow needing the tiger.\n\nLooking through all statements again, the only direct statements about needing are statements 7, 11, 12, 13, 14.\n\nStatement 7 says the tiger needs the mouse.\n\nStatement 11: If someone needs the tiger, then the tiger sees the bear. Since the tiger does see the bear (statement 3: bear sees tiger, but does that mean tiger sees bear? Not exactly. Wait, the bear sees the tiger, but the tiger seeing the bear isn\'t directly stated. Wait, statement 3 says "The bear sees the tiger." That\'s from the bear\'s perspective. The tiger seeing the bear would be a separate fact unless inferred. Wait, maybe the statements are directional. So "sees" is a one-way relation. So the bear sees the tiger, but the tiger may or may not see the bear. Hmm, that\'s important.\n\nSo statement 3: Bear sees tiger. Statement 11 says if someone needs the tiger, then tiger sees the bear. So, for someone needing the tiger to be true, the tiger must see the bear. But since we don\'t know if the tiger sees the bear, unless there\'s another premise. Wait, maybe statement 13? Let\'s check.\n\nStatement 13: If the tiger sees the bear and the tiger needs the mouse, then the tiger is big. The tiger does need the mouse (statement 7), so if the tiger also sees the bear, then the tiger is big. But do we know if the tiger sees the bear? The bear sees the tiger, but that\'s not the same as the ti