se. So is there someone who sees the mouse?\n\nLooking at the statements, the only one that could lead to someone seeing the mouse is statement 10: If someone is rough, then they see the mouse. But that requires that someone is rough first, which might be circular. Alternatively, maybe another way.\n\nAlternatively, if someone else sees the mouse, like the bald eagle or the dog or lion? The problem doesn\'t explicitly state that any of them see the mouse except possibly through implications.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the mouse is seen by someone because of another condition. Let\'s see:\n\nStatement 13 says if someone sees the dog, then they need the mouse. The mouse doesn\'t see the dog (statement 7), but maybe another entity does. For instance, does the bald eagle see the dog? The bald eagle likes the dog (statement 3), but "likes" doesn\'t necessarily mean seeing. Unless "likes" implies seeing, but the problem doesn\'t state that. So we can\'t assume that.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the lion sees the dog? Not sure. The dog needs the lion (statement 4), but again, "needs" might not imply seeing.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the mouse is seen by someone else through another rule. Let\'s think of all possibilities.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a chain of implications here. Let\'s try to see step by step.\n\nSuppose we want to see if the mouse is rough. Let\'s consider possibilities:\n\nCase 1: The mouse is rough.\n\nIf the mouse is rough, then by statement 10, the mouse sees the mouse (since "they" would be the mouse itself). So the mouse sees itself. Then, according to statement 11, if someone (the mouse) sees the mouse, then the mouse is kind. Therefore, the mouse would be kind. Then, by statement 14, since the mouse is kind, it must be rough. Wait, that\'s consistent. So if the mouse is rough, then it\'s kind, and then because it\'s kind, it\'s rough. So that\'s a loop but consistent. But does that mean it\'s possible?\n\nBut the problem is, can the mouse be rough with