ing a bit tangled. Let me try to approach it systematically.\n\nFirst, let\'s note all the entities: bald eagle, dog, lion, mouse.\n\nWe need to determine if the mouse is rough.\n\nTo be rough, per statement 14, if the mouse is kind, then it must be rough. Alternatively, maybe there\'s another path to being rough.\n\nAlternatively, if someone sees the mouse, then the mouse is kind (statement 11). So if the mouse is seen, then it\'s kind, leading to roughness via statement 14.\n\nSo, if the mouse is seen by someone, then it\'s kind, hence rough.\n\nBut is the mouse seen by someone?\n\nTo be seen by someone, there must be an entity that sees the mouse.\n\nLooking at the statements, the only way someone sees the mouse is through statement 10 (if they are rough, they see the mouse), or some other condition?\n\nWait, statement 10 is a conditional: If someone is rough, then they see the mouse. But that doesn\'t mean that seeing the mouse can only happen through being rough. It\'s possible that someone else sees the mouse for another reason not mentioned, but the problem doesn\'t state any other way. So unless there\'s another rule, the only way to see the mouse is if you\'re rough, but that requires that the seer is rough. Alternatively, maybe the seer is the mouse itself, but that would require the mouse being rough.\n\nAlternatively, maybe another entity is rough and thus sees the mouse. Let\'s see.\n\nSuppose there\'s someone else who is rough. For example, the bald eagle is kind (statement 1), so by statement 14, all kind people are rough. Wait, the bald eagle is kind (statement 1), so according to statement 14, the bald eagle must be rough. Because "All kind people are rough," so if the bald eagle is kind, it\'s rough. That\'s an important point!\n\nAh, yes! The bald eagle is kind (statement 1), so by statement 14, the bald eagle must be rough. So that\'s a key point.\n\nSo the bald eagle is rough. Therefore, by statement 10, since the bald eagle is rough, the bald e