se the mouse\'s roughness is a result of being kind, which is because someone (the bald eagle) saw it. The mouse\'s own seeing of itself is an additional consequence but doesn\'t affect the prior reasoning. So it\'s okay.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s an issue with the mouse seeing the bald eagle? Let me check statement 8: If someone needs the mouse, then the mouse does not see the bald eagle.\n\nDoes anyone need the mouse? Let\'s see:\n\nFrom statement 13: If someone sees the dog, they need the mouse. The mouse doesn\'t see the dog (statement 7), but if someone else sees the dog, like the bald eagle, then that someone would need the mouse. \n\nWait, does the bald eagle see the dog? The bald eagle likes the dog (statement 3), but does that mean it sees the dog? The problem doesn\'t explicitly say that. So unless "likes" implies seeing, we can\'t assume that. So maybe the bald eagle doesn\'t see the dog. \n\nTherefore, unless someone else sees the dog, the condition in statement 13 isn\'t triggered. Since we don\'t know if anyone else sees the dog, we can\'t assume that. Therefore, maybe no one needs the mouse except possibly through other means.\n\nWait, but even if someone needed the mouse, like the bald eagle, then according to statement 8, the mouse would not see the bald eagle. However, in our previous reasoning, the bald eagle sees the mouse, but does the mouse see the bald eagle? Not necessarily. The bald eagle seeing the mouse doesn\'t require the mouse to see the bald eagle. So statement 8 would only say that if someone needs the mouse, then the mouse does not see the bald eagle. But unless someone needs the mouse, that condition doesn\'t apply. \n\nSo, in our case, even if the bald eagle doesn\'t need the mouse, but just sees it because it\'s rough, then the mouse\'s seeing the bald eagle is not restricted. Wait, but the mouse\'s seeing the bald eagle isn\'t mentioned. The only thing is that the mouse doesn\'t see the dog (statement 7). So the mouse coul