iven information. Therefore, the statement "Dave is not smart" would be unknown. So the answer is C.\n\nWait, but wait a second. Let me check again if there is any rule that might have an implication.\n\nSuppose someone is not smart. Is there any rule that would require that? The only one is Gary\'s explicit "Gary is not smart". \n\nAlternatively, is there a rule that says "All things are not smart unless stated otherwise"? No, that\'s not how these problems work. In logic, unless stated otherwise, you can\'t assume anything about an attribute unless it\'s inferred from the given premises. Since there\'s no premise that mentions Dave\'s smartness, either directly or via a rule, then it\'s unknown.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be C) Unknown.\n'] 93%|█████████▎| 560/600 [17:33:32<58:31, 87.79s/it]  
