lternatively, maybe the fact that the bear visits the rabbit (statement 4) can be part of a condition. But none of the conditionals mention visiting leading to anything except statement 16's part.\n\nWait, let me think again. The question is whether the rabbit likes the bear. Let's see if any of the conditionals can be triggered by that, but since we don't know yet, maybe we can see if it's necessary for some other conclusion.\n\nSuppose the rabbit does like the bear. Then, in statement 16, if the squirrel also visits the rabbit, then the bear must visit the squirrel. But since we don't know if the squirrel visits the rabbit, that's not helpful. However, if the squirrel does visit the rabbit, then the bear would have to visit the squirrel. But since we don't have info on the squirrel's visit to the rabbit, that's a dead end.\n\nAlternatively, if the rabbit doesn't like the bear, then statement 16's condition isn't met, so nothing happens. So that doesn't give us a contradiction.\n\nWait a second, maybe the fact that the bear is green (from statement 15) could be linked to the rabbit's color? The rabbit is green, but that's just their colors. There's no rule connecting that.\n\nHmm. Maybe the only way to determine the rabbit's feelings is if there's a conditional that requires it. Since none of the conditionals directly state anything about the rabbit liking the bear, except perhaps through statement 16's antecedent, but that's not enough.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there's an implicit assumption that if A is chased by B, then B doesn't like A. But that's not stated in the problem. The rabbit chases the bear (statement 6), but that might not mean anything about the rabbit's feelings.\n\nAlternatively, the squirrel likes the bear (statement 9), and the bear likes the rabbit (from earlier), but that doesn't imply anything about the rabbit's feelings towards the bear.\n\nHmm. Let me check all the facts again to see if I missed something.\n\nWait, the problem says \"If so