tatement about that, and none of the conditionals seem to require it. \n\nLooking at statement 16 again: If the squirrel visits the rabbit AND the rabbit likes the bear \u2192 then the bear visits the squirrel. \n\nIf we could find that the squirrel does visit the rabbit, then the rabbit's liking of the bear would be required for the bear to visit the squirrel. But since we don't know if the squirrel visits the rabbit, and the conclusion (bear visits squirrel) isn't mentioned elsewhere, we can't infer anything.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the fact that the bear visits the rabbit (statement 4) could be part of another condition, but I don't see any.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the rabbit's chasing of the bear (statement 6) has some implication. For instance, if the rabbit chases the bear, does that mean the bear is being chased, but that's already stated (the bear is chased by the rabbit and the squirrel). Not sure.\n\nSince there's no direct information or conditional that links the rabbit's feelings towards the bear except possibly through statement 16's antecedent, but we can't confirm the squirrel's visit, I think this might be unknown.\n\nWait, but let me check again if there's any other conditionals. Let me look at all the conditionals again:\n\n11: chasing squirrel \u2192 squirrel is red (already used)\n12: likes lion \u2192 chases squirrel (used for squirrel and lion)\n13: red \u2192 likes lion (used for lion and squirrel)\n14: nice and chases lion \u2192 likes rabbit (used for bear)\n15: (likes bear \u2227 bear likes rabbit) \u2192 bear is green (used)\n16: (squirrel visits rabbit \u2227 rabbit likes bear) \u2192 bear visits squirrel (not used)\n\nThere's nothing else. So unless there's a way to determine whether the squirrel visits the rabbit, or if the rabbit likes the bear, we can't use statement 16. Since we don't have information about the squirrel visiting the rabbit, and the question is about the rabbit's feelings, it seems like there's no way to determine it