uirrel. So if the squirrel visits the cow, then the squirrel cannot visit the squirrel (itself?), which is impossible, but maybe that\'s a different case.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the squirrel is visiting the mouse, and that\'s fine unless the squirrel also visits the cow. But the problem is whether the squirrel\'s visit to the mouse is possible given the other rules.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the squirrel visiting the mouse is allowed unless another rule says otherwise. Let me check other rules.\n\nLooking at statement 15: If something sees the mouse, then the mouse eats the squirrel. So if someone sees the mouse, then mouse must eat squirrel. But who sees the mouse? Let\'s see.\n\nThe lion sees the cow (statement 5), and from statement 16: If something sees the lion, then it sees the mouse. Wait, so if an animal sees the lion, then it sees the mouse. But who is seeing the lion?\n\nStatement 18: If something is blue, then it sees the lion. The mouse is blue (statement 7), so the mouse is blue → mouse sees the lion. Therefore, according to statement 18, the mouse sees the lion. Therefore, by statement 16, if the mouse sees the lion, then it must see the mouse (itself?), which is trivial. Wait, but the mouse seeing the lion would require it to see the mouse (itself). That\'s okay.\n\nAdditionally, the mouse seeing the lion would mean via statement 15: If the mouse sees the mouse (the mouse sees the mouse?), but maybe that\'s not the case here. Wait, let\'s clarify.\n\nWait, the mouse is an entity. Let\'s track who sees whom.\n\nFirst, the lion sees the cow (statement 5). The mouse is blue (statement 7), so by statement 18, the mouse sees the lion (since it\'s blue → sees lion). Therefore, the mouse sees the lion. Then, by statement 16, since the mouse sees the lion, it must see the mouse (itself). That\'s okay, perhaps. But then, according to statement 15: If something sees the mouse, then the mouse eats the squirrel. So since the mouse sees the mouse (itself), then t