nt 14 applies: \"If something eats the bald eagle and the bald eagle likes the tiger, the tiger likes the mouse.\" Thus, the **tiger likes the mouse**.  \n\n3. **Further Implications**:  \n   - The tiger likes the mouse and the mouse does not eat the tiger (statement 10: the tiger eats the mouse, but there\u2019s no mention of the mouse eating the tiger). Thus, by statement 16, the **mouse is big**.  \n\n4. **Bear\u2019s Feelings Toward the Mouse**:  \n   - No direct statements indicate the bear\u2019s feelings toward the mouse.  \n   - Statement 17 (\"If something visits the bear and is kind, the bear likes the tiger\") could relate, but the mouse does not visit the bear (it only visits itself). The bald eagle visits the bear (statement 5), but its kindness is unknown, so this path doesn\u2019t clarify the bear\u2019s feelings toward the mouse.  \n\n**Conclusion**: None of the statements provide information about whether the bear likes the mouse. The chain of reasoning confirms the mouse\u2019s traits and the tiger\u2019s feelings but leaves the bear\u2019s opinion of the mouse undetermined.  \n\n**Answer: C) Unknown**",
