isitsMouse. \n\nIs there any information about DogVisitsMouse? \n\nLooking through the statements, I don't see any direct statements about the dog visiting the mouse. So DogVisitsMouse could be either true or false. \n\nTherefore, in case 1 (DogIsYoung = true), if DogVisitsMouse is false, then according to statement 10, the mouse does not visit the rabbit (\u00acMouseVisitsRabbit). However, earlier we had a path where MouseVisitsRabbit is true (because it's big). \n\nThis would be a contradiction. Therefore, this case would require that either DogIsYoung is not true, or DogVisitsMouse is true, or the initial assumption that MouseVisitsRabbit is true must be wrong. \n\nAlternatively, if in case 1, DogVisitsMouse is true, then the antecedent of statement 10 is false (since DogIsYoung is true but DogVisitsMouse is true, so the conjunction is false), so the implication holds but doesn't affect anything. Therefore, MouseVisitsRabbit could still be true. \n\nBut since we don't know whether DogVisitsMouse is true or false, and we don't know if the dog is young, there's a possibility that the dog is young and doesn't visit the mouse, leading to \u00acMouseVisitsRabbit. \n\nAlternatively, if the dog is not young (DogIsYoung = false), then the antecedent of statement 10 is false, so the implication is true regardless, so it doesn't impose any restrictions. \n\nTherefore, there are two scenarios where MouseVisitsRabbit could be true or false depending on the dog's age and actions. \n\nWait, but in our initial reasoning, we concluded that MouseIsBig \u2192 MouseVisitsRabbit (from statement 15). That seems solid because MouseIsBig is established via statement 8 because the tiger visits the mouse. \n\nHowever, if in some scenario, the mouse doesn't visit the rabbit (because of statement 10), that would contradict that conclusion. \n\nSo the contradiction arises only if the dog is young and doesn't visit the mouse, which would make MouseVisitsRabbit false, but we also have that MouseIsBig must visit the rabbit (statement 15). So that would create a contradiction. \n\nTherefore, in such a case, the premises would be inconsistent, which can't happen in a logical system. Therefore, we must consider that such a scenario is impossible. \n\nWait, maybe I need to think in terms of logical consistency. If assuming that the dog is young and doesn't visit the mouse leads to a contradiction (because then statement 10 would require \u00acMouseVisitsRabbit, but statement 15 requires MouseVisitsRabbit), then that scenario must be impossible. Therefore, the dog cannot be both young and not visiting the mouse. \n\nTherefore, either the dog is not young, or it does visit the mouse. \n\nSo, let's formalize this. \n\nFrom MouseIsBig (established), statement 15 gives MouseVisitsRabbit must be true. \n\nSuppose, for contradiction, that the dog is young (DogIsYoung = true) and the dog does not visit the mouse (\u00acDogVisitsMouse = true). Then by statement 10, we must have \u00acMouseVisitsRabbit. But this contradicts MouseVisitsRabbit (from statement 15). Therefore, this scenario is impossible. \n\nTherefore, the conjunction (DogIsYoung \u2227 \u00acDogVisitsMouse) cannot be true. So either DogIsYoung is false, or DogVisitsMouse is true. \n\nTherefore, even if the dog is young, the dog must visit the mouse to avoid contradiction. \n\nAlternatively, if the dog is not young, then there's no problem. \n\nSo, in any case, the conclusion MouseVisitsRabbit holds unless there's another contradiction. \n\nWait, but do we have any information that would require DogVisitsMouse? \n\nLooking at the premises, the only direct relation to DogVisitsMouse is in statement 10's antecedent. But unless there's another rule that forces the dog to visit the mouse, perhaps it's possible for DogVisitsMouse to be false as long as the dog is not young. \n\nWait, but in the case where DogIsYoung is true, then Dog must visit the mouse to prevent the contradiction. \n\nTherefore, in all pos