erhaps necessary.\n\nWait, so to be green, a thing must be both nice and furry (from statement 9). Let\'s check if Harry is nice. The problem states Harry\'s attributes as smart and young. However, is he nice? Let\'s see.\n\nLooking at statement 16: "Furry, smart things are nice." Harry is furry (from 15 and 7) and smart (statement 6). Therefore, by statement 16, Harry must be nice. So that\'s a key point. So Harry is furry, smart, young, and therefore also nice.\n\nTherefore, Harry is a nice, furry thing. According to statement 9, "Nice, furry things are green." Therefore, Harry would be green. So that would mean Harry is green, making the statement "Harry is not green" false. Therefore, the answer would be B) False.\n\nBut wait, let me check again to make sure I didn\'t miss any contradictions or other rules. Let\'s go through step by step.\n\nHarry is young (7), so by 8 (Young things are furry), Harry is furry. Also, statement 15 is an implication that if Harry is young, then he\'s furry. Since he is young, that also confirms he\'s furry. So that\'s consistent.\n\nHarry is smart (6) and furry (from above). Then, by statement 16: "Furry, smart things are nice." So that makes Harry nice. So Harry is nice, furry, smart, young.\n\nNow, being nice and furry, by statement 9, "Nice, furry things are green," so Harry must be green. Therefore, the statement "Harry is not green" is false, so the answer is B.\n\nWait, but let me check the other rules. For example, statement 10 says all green things are nice. Since Harry is already nice, that\'s okay. Statement 12 says all green things are smart, which Harry is. Statement 14 says all green things are big. So Harry would also be big, which would then lead to statement 13: if big and young, then round. So Harry would also be round, but that\'s not needed for the current question.\n\nIs there any rule that could prevent Harry from being green? Let me see. The only way the conclusion that Harry is green would be wrong is if there\'s a rule that says something contradictory. But according to the given premises, all the steps seem to lead to Harry being green. Therefore, the statement "Harry is not green" is false. So the answer should be B) False.\n\nHmm, but wait a second. Let me double-check statement 9 again. It says "Nice, furry things are green." So that\'s a conditional: if something is both nice and furry, then it is green. Since Harry is both, then he must be green. Therefore, the statement "Harry is not green" must be false. So the answer is B.\n'] 98%|█████████▊| 585/600 [18:17:42<20:40, 82.72s/it]
