f). So that would satisfy the first part of statement 17\'s condition. \n\nWait, so in statement 17: If something (X) likes the mouse, and the mouse likes the cat, then the cat is blue. Since the mouse likes the mouse (because of statement 18\'s conclusion), then X could be the mouse itself. So substituting X as the mouse:\n\nThe mouse (X) likes the mouse (the subject), and the mouse likes the cat (which is true). Therefore, the antecedent is true, so the conclusion is that the cat is blue. \n\nWait, that\'s a crucial point! Let me verify again. \n\nStatement 18 says that if something is cold and red, it likes the mouse. The mouse is cold and red, so the mouse must like the mouse. Therefore, the mouse likes itself. \n\nThen, applying statement 17: If (something likes the mouse) AND (mouse likes the cat) → cat is blue. \n\nThe mouse does like the mouse (from statement 18), so the first part is true (since X is the mouse here), and the second part (mouse likes the cat) is true (from statement 12). Therefore, the antecedent is met, so the conclusion is that the cat is blue. \n\nTherefore, the cat must be blue. \n\nNow, since the cat is blue, we can apply other rules. \n\nFirst, statement 16 says that blue things are red, so the cat is also red. \n\nNow, going back to statement 15: If something needs the cat and the cat is red, then it is blue. The mouse needs the cat (13), and now the cat is red (from being blue and statement 16). Therefore, the mouse (which needs the cat and the cat is red) must be blue. \n\nWait, but the mouse is already red (from statement 10). But according to statement 15, if the mouse needs the cat (which it does) and the cat is red (which it is now, because it\'s blue and thus red), then the mouse must be blue. But the mouse was already stated to be red. However, since it\'s now blue, that\'s okay because blue things are red (statement 16). So the mouse can be both blue and red. That\'s allowed. \n\nSo now, the mouse is blue (from statement 15) 