 rule 8), but Fiona is already round, so that\'s covered.\n\nWait, let\'s see if there\'s a way Erin could be big. To be big, perhaps through another rule? Let\'s see all the rules again.\n\nRule 5 is the only one that mentions "big" besides Fiona\'s properties. So unless there\'s another rule, maybe through being furry and something else?\n\nAlternatively, maybe through being round? Fiona is round and big, but maybe round things are big? Let me check the rules for that. The rules don\'t explicitly state that round things are big, except in Fiona\'s case. Wait, Harry is rough, so by rule 4, he must be round. Then, is Harry big? Let\'s see. Harry is nice and rough, so from rule 4, he must be round. If Harry is round, then by rule 8, he must be furry? Wait, rule 8 says if something is round, it must be furry. So Harry must be furry if he\'s round. Wait, but Harry\'s properties given are only nice and rough. So we can deduce that Harry is round (because he\'s rough), so he must be furry (by rule 8). Therefore, Harry is furry, which wasn\'t stated before. So that\'s an inference.\n\nWait, that\'s a good point. Let me track Harry\'s inferred properties:\n\nHarry is rough → by rule 4: If rough, then round. So Harry is round. Then by rule 8: If round, then furry. Therefore, Harry is furry. So Harry has at least the properties: nice, rough, round, furry. Also, since he\'s furry, by rule 1, he must be white. Wait, but rule 1 says all furry things are white, so Harry must also be white. So Harry is white as well. Therefore, Harry has: nice, rough, round, furry, white. \n\nNow, with that, let\'s check other rules for Harry. For example, rule 7: All nice, white things are red. Harry is nice and white, so according to rule 7, he must be red. Wait, but Harry\'s color is now white (from being furry) but rule 7 says if he\'s nice and white, he must be red. That\'s a contradiction. Wait, that\'s a problem. Let me retrace.\n\nHarry is furry (inferred from being round via rule 8), so 