dressed in statement 9: if something sees the tiger, then the tiger is kind. So if someone sees the tiger, then tiger becomes kind?\n\nWait, the original statements:\n\nStatement 9: If something sees the tiger then the tiger is kind.\n\nSo if an entity sees the tiger, then the tiger is kind. So the tiger's kindness depends on whether someone sees it.\n\nWe know that the mouse sees the tiger (from above conclusion from statement 11), so since mouse sees the tiger, then according to statement 9, the tiger is kind.\n\nTherefore, tiger is kind.\n\nWait, so the tiger is now kind because the mouse sees it. So tiger is kind, blue, cold, young.\n\nNow, since the tiger is kind, from statement 12, the tiger must see the mouse. So tiger sees mouse.\n\nAlso, since the tiger is blue and kind (from now being kind), statement 13 says that it should see the rabbit. So tiger sees rabbit.\n\nOkay, moving on. Let's see if any of these lead us to the mouse chasing itself.\n\nLooking at statement 14: \"If something chases the lion and the lion chases the rabbit then the rabbit chases the mouse.\"\n\nHmm, so if there's an entity that chases the lion, and the lion chases the rabbit, then the rabbit chases the mouse.\n\nWe know that the mouse chases the lion (statement 2). So mouse chases lion. So \"something\" (the mouse) chases the lion. Now, does the lion chase the rabbit?\n\nThe lion's behavior: the problem states the lion is kind, but we don't have any direct info on the lion chasing the rabbit. Unless there's an implication.\n\nWait, the lion is kind. From statement 12, the lion sees the mouse. But does that mean the lion chases the rabbit? Not directly.\n\nWait, the statement 14 requires two conditions: something (the mouse, in this case?) chases the lion, and the lion chases the rabbit. Then the rabbit chases the mouse.\n\nWait, the first part of the statement 14 is \"something chases the lion and the lion chases the rabbit\". So the \"something\" could be the mouse, as the mouse chases the lion. But the second part requires that the lion chases the rabbit. Do we know if the lion chases the rabbit?\n\nWe don't have any direct info. The lion's actions are only mentioned in statement 2 (mouse chases lion) and the fact that it's kind, so sees the mouse (from statement 12). So unless there's another rule that causes the lion to chase the rabbit, that part is unknown.\n\nTherefore, the condition for statement 14 is only partially met. The first part (something chases the lion) is true (mouse does), but the second part (lion chases the rabbit) is unknown. Therefore, we can't conclude that the rabbit chases the mouse via this statement.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's another way to get the rabbit to chase the mouse. Let me see.\n\nAlternatively, the rabbit is kind and round. Since the rabbit is kind, from statement 12, it sees the mouse, which we already have. Also, from statement 15, if something is kind and sees the mouse, it must see the lion. Which we already applied to the rabbit.\n\nNow, the rabbit is chasing the mouse? Not sure yet.\n\nWait, the question is about the mouse chasing itself. So maybe looking for any rules that could imply that.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the mouse is involved in a loop. Let me think.\n\nThe mouse chases the lion (given). The lion is kind, so sees the mouse. So the lion sees the mouse. But that doesn't mean the lion chases the mouse.\n\nWait, the problem is only about \"chases\" relationships. The \"sees\" is a different relation. So chasing is separate from seeing.\n\nHmm. Let's see if there's any other rule that can lead us to the mouse chasing itself.\n\nLooking at all the statements again, perhaps statement 14 could lead to the rabbit chasing the mouse, but as we saw, that requires the lion to chase the rabbit. Since that's unknown, maybe that's a dead end.\n\nAlternatively, statement 13 says that if something is blue and kind, it sees the rabbit. The rabbit is blue and kind, so rabbit sees itself. Wait, that