(5), so since it\'s blue and kind (now), then tiger sees rabbit (13). So tiger sees rabbit.\n\n- Since tiger is kind, it must see mouse (12). So tiger sees mouse.\n\n- Since the tiger sees the rabbit (from 13), then by statement 8, rabbit is cold. So rabbit is cold.\n\nNow, any other implications?\n\nThe rabbit is cold now, but that\'s not directly related to chasing.\n\nNow, considering the question again: The mouse chases the mouse.\n\nIs there any rule that would require this to be true or false?\n\nLet me think of all the rules again, perhaps I missed one.\n\nStatement 14: "If something chases the lion and the lion chases the rabbit then the rabbit chases the mouse."\n\nThe first part of the antecedent is satisfied: the mouse chases the lion. The second part requires the lion to chase the rabbit. We don\'t know if the lion does that. If we can determine that the lion does chase the rabbit, then the rabbit would chase the mouse. But we can\'t confirm the lion chases the rabbit. So that path doesn\'t help.\n\nAlternatively, if the rabbit chases the mouse, does that lead to anything? Let\'s suppose that the rabbit does chase the mouse (even if we can\'t confirm it yet). Then, would that create another implication?\n\nSuppose the rabbit chases the mouse. Then, perhaps some other rule applies. But I don\'t see any rule that takes that as an antecedent.\n\nAlternatively, if the mouse sees the tiger, and the tiger is kind, then maybe the tiger\'s actions? The tiger being kind means it sees the mouse (from 12), but again, no chasing implied.\n\nWait, the tiger is kind and blue, so it sees the rabbit (from 13). So tiger sees rabbit. But again, no chasing.\n\nHmm. Let me think differently. The problem asks whether "The mouse chases the mouse" is true, false, or unknown.\n\nSince none of the statements directly state this, and there\'s no chain of implications that force it to be true or false, then it might be unknown. But let me check again for any possible deductions.\n