s it\'s round or something else. Since the mouse isn\'t stated to be round, it\'s not kind unless another rule applies. There\'s no rule that would make the mouse kind unless it\'s round. Therefore, the mouse is not kind.\n\nTherefore, the mouse\'s chasing of itself wouldn\'t trigger any of the rules related to kindness, since it\'s not kind.\n\nAlternatively, if the mouse were kind, then from statement 12 it would have to see the mouse, but since it\'s not kind, that\'s irrelevant.\n\nHmm. Since there\'s no information leading to the mouse chasing itself or preventing it, I think the answer must be "unknown" (option C). But wait, let me check again for any possible deductions I missed.\n\nWait, perhaps the rabbit chasing the mouse? If the rabbit does, then maybe the mouse sees the tiger, which is already true. Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the fact that the rabbit is kind and sees the mouse means that the rabbit must see the lion (from statement 15), which we\'ve already used.\n\nWait, going back to the original question: "The mouse chases the mouse." Is there any rule that could make this false?\n\nSuppose the mouse does chase itself. Is there any contradiction? I don\'t see any. The rules don\'t prohibit it, and there\'s no implication that would make it impossible.\n\nAlternatively, could it be false?\n\nIf there\'s a rule that says "nothing chases itself" but that\'s not stated. Since the problem doesn\'t have such a rule, we can\'t assume that.\n\nTherefore, since there\'s no information to confirm it\'s true or false, the answer must be unknown. But wait, maybe there\'s a way to infer it\'s false?\n\nWait, let me think of another angle. Let\'s see if there\'s any statement that could lead to a contradiction if we assume the mouse chases itself. Suppose it does. Does that create any inconsistency?\n\nWell, if the mouse chases itself, then "something" (the mouse) chases the lion (as given), and if the lion also chases the rabbit (unknown), then rabbit wou