s round. \n3. Since Bob is round, Rule 14: round → young. So Bob is young. \n4. Since Bob is young, Rule 13: young → kind. So Bob is kind. \n5. Now, Bob is kind and white → Rule 12: kind and white → green. So Bob is green. \n6. Bob is quiet and green → Rule 16: quiet and green → red. So Bob must be red. \n\nTherefore, according to these rules, Bob is red. Therefore, the statement "Bob is not red" is false. Hence the correct answer is B) False. \n\nWait, but is there a way that Bob could not be red? Let\'s see if any of these steps have exceptions or if I missed a rule that might prevent this. \n\nLooking at Rule 16 again: "Quiet, green things are red." There\'s no exception mentioned, so if something is both quiet and green, it must be red. Since Bob is both, he must be red. \n\nTherefore, unless there\'s a mistake in the chain of reasoning, Bob must be red. Therefore, the statement "Bob is not red" is false. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I made an error in assuming that being kind and white necessarily leads to green? Let me check Rule 12 again: "If something is kind and white then it is green." The wording is "If A and B then C." So whenever A and B are true, then C must be true. So since Bob is both kind and white, he must be green. That seems correct. \n\nTherefore, the conclusion is that Bob is red, so the statement is false. \n\nWait, but the problem\'s options are A) True, B) False, C) Unknown. So the answer would be B. \n\nBut let me check if there\'s a possibility that my reasoning is wrong somewhere else. For instance, could Bob not be kind?\n\nWait, Bob is young because he\'s round (from being white), and young things are kind. So Bob must be kind. Therefore, he has to be kind. \n\nTherefore, the steps are correct. \n\nAnother angle: the problem says "All quiet, kind things are white." (Rule 15). Since Bob is quiet and kind (from the chain), he must be white, which he is. So that\'s okay. \n\nAnother check: Fiona is round → young (Rule 14), so Fiona must be yo