All green, round things are white.\" So if Bob is green and round, he must be white. But does that interfere with any other rules?\n\nIf Bob is green, then he must be green. But the problem's question is only about being green. The fact that he would also have to be white doesn't contradict anything stated, so that's acceptable. Therefore, the conclusion holds.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a hidden contradiction. Let me see: If Bob is green, round, and white, then is there a rule that says white and round must not be something else? I don't see any such rule. The only rules related to white are:\n\n- Rule 14: Green and round \u2192 white.\n\n- Rule 16: Smart and white \u2192 big.\n\nSo Bob would be smart (from quiet), white (from green and round), so by rule 16, he must be big. But the problem doesn't state that Bob is big or not. However, the question is about being green, so even if Bob has to be big, that doesn't affect the truth of him being green. Unless there's a rule that says big things can't be round or something, but the only big-related rules are:\n\n- Rule 8: Gary is big.\n\n- Rule 16: Smart and white \u2192 big.\n\n- Rule 17: If something is nice and quiet \u2192 big.\n\n- Rule 20: Big \u2192 nice.\n\nSo if Bob must be big (from being smart and white), then he must be big and nice. But since the problem doesn't state Bob's size, that's okay. The conclusion that he's green still holds.\n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is A) True. However, I'm a bit uncertain because sometimes in these logic problems, there might be a trick. Let me check again.\n\nWait, another angle: The problem states \"All quiet things are smart.\" So quiet \u2192 smart. The contrapositive is not smart \u2192 not quiet. \n\nAlso, rule 18: If quiet and not smart \u2192 not green. But since quiet things are smart, that antecedent can't happen, so the rule is vacuously true. So no problem there.\n\nRule 19: All quiet and smart \u2192 green. Since quiet implies smart, this rule 