out being round. So that\'s okay.\n\nWait, but Gary is green and white. Is there a rule that prohibits that? Rule 14 says green and round must be white, but that doesn\'t stop something from being green and white without being round. So that\'s fine.\n\nNow, going back to Bob. Since he\'s round and quiet, so smart, so green, so then green and round → white (rule 14). Therefore, Bob must be white as well. But the problem doesn\'t state that Bob is not white, so that\'s acceptable.\n\nTherefore, there\'s no contradiction here, so Bob must be green. Therefore, the answer is A) True.\n\nWait, but the options include "Unknown". Maybe I missed something. Let me check again.\n\nWait, the problem says "All quiet things are smart" (rule 12). So since Bob is quiet (from being round), he must be smart. Then, since he is both quiet and smart (from rule 12 and his quietness), rule 19 says he must be green. Therefore, the conclusion is inescapable unless there\'s a rule that overrides this.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I misapplied rule 19. Let me read it again: "All quiet, smart things are green." So that\'s a universal statement: if something is both quiet and smart, then it\'s green. Since Bob is both, he must be green. So that\'s solid.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be A) True. Hmm. But I have to be careful here. Let me see if the problem\'s given information could have any conflicting statements about Bob\'s color.\n\nThe problem\'s context only mentions Bob\'s attributes as being round. There\'s no direct statement about his color, so the inference must come from the rules. Since the rules lead to Bob being green, that\'s the conclusion.\n\nWait, but let me check the exact wording of the rules again to make sure I didn\'t misread anything.\n\nRule 14: "All green, round things are white." So if something is green AND round, then it must be white. Since Bob is round and we\'re concluding he\'s green, then he must be white. But that\'s okay because there\'s no rule against being 